#        status code and sits in disjoint semantic namespaces.
#
# ---- Linker dependencies ---------------------------------------------
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R221.M1 (utf8_nfc.pdx) references these external
# symbols that must be resolved at link time:
#
#   _norm_nfc  (from libpdx-unicode-tables::Normalize, R131.M3,
#               tools/user/libpdx-unicode-tables/build-out/)
#
# A consumer linking this library passes
# `--extra-obj-dir tools/user/libpdx-unicode-tables/build-out` to
# `paideia-as link` so the symbol resolves. If the consumer wants
# only `_u8n_quick_check_ascii` (which has no nested call), it can
# link this library standalone -- the unresolved `_norm_nfc` reference
# is only reached from `_u8n_decode_and_normalize`.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R223.M1 (pipeline.pdx) has NO external linker
# dependencies -- `_pl_parse` only calls `_pl_emit_word` which lives
# in the same object. Both entry points can be linked standalone.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R223.M2 (record_projection.pdx) has NO external
# linker dependencies -- `_rp_parse_select` and `_rp_parse_project`
# are 1-nested-call wrappers that CALL the module-private
# `_rp_parse_impl`, which in turn CALLs the module-private
# `_rp_skip_ws` leaf; all three (plus the two wrappers) live in the
# same object. All four public entry points (`_rp_skip_ws`,
# `_rp_parse_impl`, `_rp_parse_select`, `_rp_parse_project`) can be
# linked standalone.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R224.M1 (lambda_lit.pdx) has NO external linker
# dependencies -- `_ll_parse` only calls the three module-private
# helpers (`_ll_skip_ws`, `_ll_classify_type`, `_ll_parse_param`)
# which all live in the same object; `_ll_parse_param` in turn calls
# the same two leaf helpers. All four public entry points can be
# linked standalone. R224.M2+ (expression parse) will introduce an
# intra-library dependency on R221.M5 AST once that lands; no
# cross-library dependency is planned.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R224.M2 (closure_capture.pdx) has NO external
# linker dependencies -- `_cc_parse` only calls the module-private
# helpers (`_cc_skip_ws`, `_cc_parse_binding`); `_cc_parse_binding`
# in turn calls `_cc_skip_ws` and `_cc_classify_type`. All four
# public entry points can be linked standalone. The R224.M2.5
# integration pass (filed as an umbrella follow-up) will add a
# one-way intra-library dependency where lambda_lit.pdx's `_ll_parse`
# CALLs this module's `_cc_parse`; no cross-library dependency is
# planned at that milestone either.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R225.M1 (hm_core.pdx) has NO external linker
# dependencies -- `_hm_fresh_var`, `_hm_unify`, `_hm_instantiate`,
# `_hm_generalise` only reference the module-private `_hm_find` and
# `_hm_occurs` helpers plus their own self-recursive calls
# (`_hm_unify` and `_hm_occurs` recurse via the symbol table). All
# four public entry points can be linked standalone.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R225.M2 (row_types.pdx) has NO external
# cross-library linker dependencies. It introduces an INTRA-library
# dependency on R225.M1 (hm_core.pdx) at two symbols:
#
#   _hm_unify   -- called by _rt_row_unify's TyVar and TyCon arms
#                  (the pure HM subset of the type grammar is
#                  canonically handled by hm_core's unifier).
#   _hm_pool / _hm_pool_next / _hm_var_next  -- the shared .bss
#                  singleton that _rt_find / _rt_alloc_node /
#                  _rt_row_var reference via `lea r11, [rip+sym]`
#                  (same cross-module .bss precedent as
#                  audit_record.pdx and mkfs.pdxfs/refusal.pdx).
#
# Within the module, _rt_row_unify, _rt_row_select, _rt_row_restrict,
# _rt_row_concat and the row allocators form a self-contained
# recursive clique. _rt_row_unify is also cross-recursive with
# _hm_unify (and _hm_unify's own self-recursion still terminates
# because any _hm_unify call made from _rt_row_unify is over types
# that are either TyVar or TyCon or pure TyApp subterms -- a pure
# TyApp subterm will never reach back into the row branch because
# _rt_row_unify's TyApp arm handles that case directly without
# delegating to _hm_unify).
#
# R225.M3+ (Datalog predicate types, effect rows) will introduce
# cross-module linker deps against R222 (SchemasSig bridge) and
# R220.M4 (schema registry) once those pre-reqs close.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R226.M1 (datalog.pdx) has NO external linker
# dependencies -- `_dl_parse` only calls the five module-private
# helpers (`_dl_skip_ws`, `_dl_scan_ident_or_var`, `_dl_scan_number`,
# `_dl_scan_string`, `_dl_scan_body`) which all live in the same
# object. R226.M2 (PredicateTypes) does NOT introduce a direct
# linker dep against datalog.pdx -- the two modules are composed by
# the CALLER (R226.M3+ evaluator) rather than directly, so each
# object stays independently linkable. The post-R220.M4
# landing will introduce a schema-registry seam at startup.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R222.M1 (schemas_sig.pdx) has NO external linker
# dependencies -- `_ss_parse_body` only calls the two module-private
# helpers (`_sch_sig_skip_ws`, `_sch_sig_classify_type`) which both
# live in the same object; `_ss_match_record` is a leaf M1 stub. All
# three public entry points can be linked standalone.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R222.M2 (command_sig.pdx) has NO external linker
# dependencies -- `_cs_parse_body` only calls the two module-private
# helpers (`_cmd_sig_skip_ws`, `_cmd_sig_fnv1a64`) which both live in
# the same object. All three public entry points can be linked
# standalone. R222.M3+ (REPL dispatch, _ss_match_record real body)
# may introduce intra-library deps against the R222.M1 SchemasSig
# arrays and R225.M1 HmCore type-graph pool once the REPL layer wires
# them up; no cross-library dependency is planned.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R229.M2 (session_env.pdx) has NO external linker
# dependencies -- `_se_init`, `_se_init_session`,
# `_se_insert_binding`, `_se_lookup_binding`, `_se_unset_binding` all
# touch only this module's own .bss (`_se_env_pool`,
# `_se_head_table`) and caller-supplied pointer args. There is NO
# cross-module call: SessionEnv STORES a HmCore pool-index as the
# type_ref payload but does not DEREFERENCE it; the REPL dispatch at
# R229.M3+ reads the pool_idx out and calls HmCore directly. All
# five public entry points can be linked standalone. R229.M3+ (REPL
# dispatch) will introduce intra-library deps against this module
# (the REPL layer wires _se_lookup_binding into HM typecheck free-
# name resolution and _se_insert_binding into the EVAL stage); no
# cross-library dependency is planned.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R221.M2 (grapheme_iter.pdx) depends on
# libpdx-unicode-tables at a single external symbol:
#
#   _gc_next  (from libpdx-unicode-tables::GraphemeClusters, R131.M2,
#              tools/user/libpdx-unicode-tables/build-out/)
#
# The ASCII fast path never calls out (pure leaf); the slow path and
# `_gi_tokenize_args` route through `_gc_next` to walk the TR#29
# state machine over the Latin+emoji baseline. Consumers that need
# only ASCII handling can link this library standalone.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R221.M3 (width_pass.pdx) depends on
# libpdx-unicode-tables at two external symbols and introduces an
# INTRA-library dependency on R221.M2 at one symbol:
#
#   _width_char       (from libpdx-unicode-tables::Width, R131.M5,
#                      tools/user/libpdx-unicode-tables/build-out/)
#   utf8_decode_cp    (from libpdx-unicode-tables::Utf8, R131.M1,
#                      tools/user/libpdx-unicode-tables/build-out/)
#   _gi_next_cluster  (from libpdx-pds-parser::GraphemeIter, R221.M2;
#                      intra-library; same object graph; no new
#                      --extra-obj-dir required)
#
# `_wp_char_width` CALLs only `_width_char`; the overlay scan on
# the parser-tier A-class table is intra-module .rodata.
# `_wp_cluster_width` CALLs `utf8_decode_cp` then `_wp_char_width`
# (recursive-into-this-module -- no cycle). `_wp_cols` CALLs
# `_gi_next_cluster` + `utf8_decode_cp` + `_wp_char_width` per
# cluster. `_wp_locale_amb_width` and `_wp_set_locale_amb` are pure
# leaves against the module's own .bss (`_wp_locale_amb_state`).
# All five public entry points can be linked against the
# libpdx-unicode-tables build-out that R221.M1 and R221.M2 already
# consume; no new cross-library dependency is introduced.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The width pass is a pure byte-walker + table
# lookup over caller-supplied data; no KIND surface is touched.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R222.M3 (functor_instance.pdx) has NO external
# linker dependencies -- `_fi_instantiate` and
# `_fi_elaborate_args` call only module-private helpers
# (`_fi_skip_ws`, `_fi_fnv1a64`, `_fi_lookup_cs`); the CommandSig
# body is passed in by the caller as a flat byte buffer rather
# than via a cross-module call. All public entry points can be
# linked standalone. R222.M4+ may introduce intra-library deps on
# R222.M1 SchemasSig arrays once ArgSpec/FlagSpec lands.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R222.M4 (arg_flag_spec.pdx) has NO external
# linker dependencies and NO cross-library dependency.
# `_afs_parse_arg_spec_body` / `_afs_parse_flag_spec_body` /
# `_afs_check_invocation` call only module-private helpers
# (`_afs_skip_ws`, `_afs_classify_type`, `_afs_parse_as_type`); all
# three live in the same object. R222.M3 FunctorInstance records are
# consumed BY VALUE through the caller-passed `invocation` byte
# pointer and never via a cross-module CALL into functor_instance.pdx
# -- the two R222 submodules remain in disjoint linker-visible
# subgraphs. All three public entry points can be linked standalone.
# R222.M5+ (registry client) will introduce a cross-module call BACK
# into this module's `_afs_parse_arg_spec_body` /
# `_afs_parse_flag_spec_body` once the commands.toml bind layer
# lands; no cross-library dependency is planned at that milestone
# either.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. _afs_check_invocation reads an invocation
# materialization record (R222.M3 output) and ArgSpec+FlagSpec records
# as flat byte buffers; no KIND surface is touched.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R229.M3 (type_env.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies. It introduces an INTRA-library
# dependency on R225.M1 (hm_core.pdx) at one symbol:
#
#   _hm_instantiate   -- called by `_te_specialize` on every cache
#                        miss. The scheme byte stream TypeEnv forwards
#                        matches HmCore's wire format exactly (8 B
#                        header + 4 B/bound-var + 16 B/body-node).
#                        HM_ERR_* return codes are translated into
#                        TE_ERR_HM_* so a downstream dispatcher can
#                        discriminate "scheme malformed" from "HM pool
#                        exhausted".
#
# `_te_init_session`, `_te_insert_scheme`, `_te_evict_scheme` touch
# only this module's own .bss (`_te_scheme_pool`, `_te_cache_pool`,
# `_te_head_table`) and caller-supplied pointer args. All four public
# entry points can be linked standalone against hm_core.pdx.
#
# No cross-module call BACK into SessionEnv -- the two R229 user-space
# satellites remain geographically parallel; the REPL dispatch at
# R229.M4+ (kernel-side) is what wires them together on a `let` /
# `unset` cycle.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R224.M3 (effect_row.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies. It introduces TWO intra-library
# dependencies:
#
#   R225.M2 RowTypes  --  called at:
#     _rt_row_empty    -- allocate the closed empty-row tail
#                         (`_er_parse` build phase).
#     _rt_row_var      -- allocate a fresh row-variable tail
#                         (`_er_parse` build phase on `, rho}`).
#     _rt_row_extend   -- prepend an effect-row ROW_EXT node onto
#                         the accumulator (`_er_parse` build phase).
#     _rt_find         -- resolve the current row cursor through a
#                         ROW_VAR resolved chain (`_er_check_subtype`
#                         and `_er_render` loop head).
#     _rt_row_select   -- query sup_row for a label present in sub
#                         (`_er_check_subtype` per-ROW_EXT step).
#
#   R225.M1 HmCore   --  touches ONLY the shared .bss row-graph pool
#                        indirectly, via R225.M2 primitives above;
#                        no direct call into HmCore. The `_hm_pool`
#                        .bss symbol IS loaded by this module's
#                        `_er_check_subtype` and `_er_render` via
#                        `lea r11, [rip + _hm_pool]` to compute node
#                        addresses on walked cursors.
#
# `_er_init`, `_er_label_lookup`, `_er_label_intern`, `_er_skip_ws`
# all touch ONLY this module's own .bss (`_er_label_table`,
# `_er_label_table_count`, `_er_primitive_init` .rodata) and caller-
# supplied pointer args. All six public entry points (`_er_init`,
# `_er_label_lookup`, `_er_label_intern`, `_er_skip_ws`, `_er_parse`,
# `_er_check_subtype`, `_er_render`) can be linked against the
# RowTypes/HmCore objects that R225.M1/M2 already consume; no new
# cross-library dependency is introduced.
#
# EffectRow uses its OWN .bss scratch (`_er_parse_scratch`, 64 B =
# 16 u32 packed-label-id slots) for the parse-collect buffer, so
# it does NOT alias R225.M1's `_hm_subst_table` nor R225.M2's
# `_rt_row_restrict`/`_rt_row_concat` scratch slice at
# `_hm_subst_table[384..512)`. The two scratch surfaces could in
# principle be unified at a later M, but the disjoint carve-out
# keeps the two sub-languages (effect-row parse vs record-row
# restrict/concat) independently reviewable.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Effect labels and effect rows are pure
# type-level artefacts; no KIND surface is touched by this module.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R221.M4 (ctx_lexer.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library dependency at M4.
# `_cl_init`, `_cl_push_ctx`, `_cl_pop_ctx`, `_cl_current_ctx`,
# `_cl_lex_token`, `_cl_tokenize` all touch ONLY this module's own
# .bss (`_cl_stack`, `_cl_depth`, `_cl_last_kind`) and the caller-
# supplied input / output buffers. `_cl_tokenize` CALLs
# `_cl_lex_token` from the same object (one nested CALL per
# iteration). All six public entry points can be linked standalone.
# R221.M5 unified-AST landing (ast.pdx, landed below) is the first
# intra-library consumer that READS Token records emitted by this
# module; the record ABI (16-byte stride: kind u16, ctx u8, flags u8,
# src_offset u32, src_length u32, payload u32) is frozen at this
# landing. The AST module itself does not CALL into CtxLexer at the
# link level: the parser-tier downstream threads Token records
# through `_ast_alloc` by value, so `ast.pdx` carries no linker-
# visible dependency on `ctx_lexer.pdx` even though the two modules
# share a semantic contract.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The context-tracking lexer is a pure byte-
# walker over caller-supplied data; no KIND surface is touched.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R221.M5 (ast.pdx) has NO external cross-library
# linker dependencies and NO intra-library linker-visible dependency
# at M5. `_ast_init`, `_ast_set_source`, `_ast_alloc`,
# `_ast_set_payload_u32`, `_ast_set_child_ref`, `_ast_set_extra_ref`,
# `_ast_render` all touch ONLY this module's own .bss
# (`_ast_node_pool` 8 KiB, `_ast_pool_next` u64, `_ast_src_ptr` u64,
# `_ast_src_len` u64) and caller-supplied pointer args. All seven
# public entry points are PURE LEAVES -- zero callee-save pushes,
# zero stack frames, zero nested CALLs; the allocator inlines the
# lazy-init (precedent: `_rt_alloc_node` in row_types.pdx) rather
# than CALLing `_ast_init`, so no alignment-math prologue is
# required. All seven can be linked standalone. R221.M5.1+ (child-
# aware render, kind-table enforcement) will remain pure leaves;
# R225.M3+ typechecker landings will introduce a one-way intra-
# library dependency where the typechecker CALLs `_ast_set_extra_ref`
# to write HmCore type-pool pool_idx into the SyntaxNode's extra_ref
# slot -- no cross-library dependency is planned.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The AST module is a pure in-process arena
# allocator + span-copy renderer over caller-supplied data; no KIND
# surface is touched.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R224.M4 (cap_row.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies. It introduces TWO intra-library
# dependencies (dual with R224.M3 EffectRow, minus _rt_row_select):
#
#   R225.M2 RowTypes  --  called at:
#     _rt_row_empty    -- allocate the closed empty-row tail
#                         (`_cr_parse` build phase).
#     _rt_row_var      -- allocate a fresh row-variable tail
#                         (`_cr_parse` build phase on `, rho}`).
#     _rt_row_extend   -- prepend a capability-row ROW_EXT node onto
#                         the accumulator (`_cr_parse` build phase).
#     _rt_find         -- resolve the current row cursor through a
#                         ROW_VAR resolved chain (`_cr_check_env` and
#                         `_cr_render` loop head).
#
#   R225.M1 HmCore   --  touches ONLY the shared .bss row-graph pool
#                        indirectly, via R225.M2 primitives above;
#                        no direct call into HmCore. The `_hm_pool`
#                        .bss symbol IS loaded by this module's
#                        `_cr_check_env` and `_cr_render` via
#                        `lea r11, [rip + _hm_pool]` to compute node
#                        addresses on walked cursors.
#
# CapRow does NOT call `_rt_row_select`; the env-mask covering
# check (`_cr_check_env`) is a direct bit test on the packed-id-
# extracted idx against the caller-supplied `env_mask` u64, not a
# row-vs-row operation. This is the key semantic difference from
# R224.M3 EffectRow's `_er_check_subtype`, which does walk sup_row
# via `_rt_row_select` per sub-label; capability row checking
# operates against a flat bit vector rather than a second row.
#
# `_cr_init`, `_cr_label_lookup`, `_cr_label_intern`, `_cr_skip_ws`
# all touch ONLY this module's own .bss (`_cr_label_table`,
# `_cr_label_table_count`, `_cr_primitive_init` .rodata) and caller-
# supplied pointer args. All seven public entry points (`_cr_init`,
# `_cr_label_lookup`, `_cr_label_intern`, `_cr_skip_ws`, `_cr_parse`,
# `_cr_check_env`, `_cr_render`) can be linked against the
# RowTypes/HmCore objects that R225.M1/M2 already consume; no new
# cross-library dependency is introduced.
#
# CapRow uses its OWN .bss scratch (`_cr_parse_scratch`, 64 B =
# 16 u32 packed-label-id slots) for the parse-collect buffer, so it
# does NOT alias R225.M1's `_hm_subst_table`, R225.M2's
# `_rt_row_restrict`/`_rt_row_concat` scratch slice at
# `_hm_subst_table[384..512)`, OR R224.M3's `_er_parse_scratch`.
# The three scratch surfaces could in principle be unified at a
# later M (effect-row parse / capability-row parse / record-row
# restrict/concat), but the disjoint carve-out keeps the three
# sub-languages independently reviewable AND lets effect-row and
# capability-row parse be invoked from the same shell turn without
# interleave hazards.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Capability labels and capability rows are
# pure type-level artefacts; the kernel-side cap handles never flow
# through this module (M4 asks the caller to pass a precomputed
# `env_mask` u64; a kernel-side helper that reads the per-session
# cap table lives at a later M4.8 landing).
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R222.M5 (cmd_registry.pdx) has NO external linker
# dependencies and NO cross-library dependency. `_cmdreg_init`,
# `_cmdreg_fnv1a64`, `_cmdreg_register`, `_cmdreg_lookup`, `_cmdreg_dump`
# all touch ONLY this module's own .bss (`_cmdreg_entries`,
# `_cmdreg_init_state`, `_cmdreg_count_system`, `_cmdreg_count_user`)
# and caller-supplied pointer args. The three write-touching entry
# points (`_cmdreg_register`, `_cmdreg_lookup`, `_cmdreg_dump`) CALL
# `_cmdreg_init` (idempotent lazy boot) at their first instruction
# after the SysV prologue (Wave 120 lazy-init lesson, reaffirmed at
# Wave 121 across R224.M3 EffectRow + R224.M4 CapRow). The two write-
# touching entry points (`_cmdreg_register`, `_cmdreg_lookup`) also
# CALL `_cmdreg_fnv1a64` (pure leaf) to compute the probe key.
#
# No cross-module CALL into command_sig.pdx (R222.M2) or
# functor_instance.pdx (R222.M3) -- the (cs_body_ref, functor_ref)
# tuple is handed in as opaque u64 handles by the caller (the kernel-
# side TOML bind layer at R123.M2 and R222.M6). The three parser-
# family FNV-1a 64 leaves (`_cmd_sig_fnv1a64`, `_fi_fnv1a64`,
# `_cmdreg_fnv1a64`) are byte-for-byte identical in algorithm and
# constants, so a downstream consumer that compares name_hash values
# across the three families is guaranteed numerical equality on the
# shared ASCII command name. All five public entry points can be
# linked standalone.
#
# CmdRegistry uses its OWN .bss state (`_cmdreg_entries` 6144 B =
# 128 slots x 48 B, `_cmdreg_init_state` u64, `_cmdreg_count_system`
# u64, `_cmdreg_count_user` u64) which is disjoint from every other
# satellite module's .bss (parser-family: `_cmd_sig_*`, `_fi_*`,
# `_afs_*`, `_sch_sig_*`; row-family: `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`, `_er_*`,
# `_cr_*`; session-family: `_se_env_pool`, `_te_scheme_pool`;
# lexer-family: `_cl_stack`, `_cl_depth`, `_cl_last_kind`;
# scope-family: `_ls_frame_pool`, `_ls_frame_next`).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Command-registry entries are opaque u64
# handles; the kernel-side KIND_CMD_MODULE substrate (R123) never
# flows through this module.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R224.M5 (let_scope.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library dependency at
# raw M5. `_ls_init`, `_ls_push_let`, `_ls_push_letrec`,
# `_ls_push_lambda`, `_ls_push_impl`, `_ls_resolve`, `_ls_pop` all
# touch ONLY this module's own .bss (`_ls_frame_pool` 4096 B =
# 128 slots x 32 B, `_ls_frame_next` u64) and caller-supplied
# pointer args. The three public push wrappers (`_ls_push_let`,
# `_ls_push_letrec`, `_ls_push_lambda`) each CALL the module-
# private `_ls_push_impl` with r8 pre-set to the kind byte (1/2/3).
# `_ls_push_impl` and `_ls_resolve` each CALL `_ls_init` idempotently
# at entry (Wave 120 lazy-init lesson, reaffirmed at Wave 121 across
# R224.M3 EffectRow + R224.M4 CapRow and at Wave 122 across R222.M5
# CmdRegistry). `_ls_init` and `_ls_pop` are pure leaves with no
# nested CALL. All seven public entry points can be linked
# standalone. The `type_scheme_ref` u32 payload stored in each frame
# at offset 0x1C is caller-opaque: at raw M5 the HM elaborator passes
# a HmCore (`_hm_pool`) pool_idx; at the R224.M5.1 wire-up the flags
# bit 0 selects a TypeEnv (`_te_scheme_pool`) scheme_id instead.
# LetScope does NOT dereference the stored u32 and does NOT call
# into HmCore or TypeEnv -- the dereference happens at the R229.M4+
# REPL-dispatch layer which reads the resolve-returned u32 and
# routes to the right substrate.
#
# LetScope uses its OWN .bss state (`_ls_frame_pool` 4096 B,
# `_ls_frame_next` u64) which is disjoint from every other satellite
# module's .bss (parser-family: `_cmd_sig_*`, `_fi_*`, `_afs_*`,
# `_sch_sig_*`, `_pl_*`, `_rp_*`, `_ll_*`, `_cc_*`, `_dl_*`,
# `_cmdreg_*`; row-family: `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`, `_er_*`, `_cr_*`;
# session-family: `_se_env_pool`, `_te_scheme_pool`; lexer-family:
# `_cl_stack`, `_cl_depth`, `_cl_last_kind`; ast-family: `_ast_pool`,
# `_ast_pool_next`, `_ast_src_ptr`, `_ast_src_len`). The scope-frame
# pool is a parse-time working-memory artefact; no cross-module .bss
# aliasing hazard exists.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Scope frames are pure parse-time working-
# memory artefacts; no KIND surface is touched by this module.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R222.M6 (dispatch_mode.pdx) has NO external
# linker dependencies and NO cross-library dependency. `_dm_init`,
# `_dm_register_light`, `_dm_register_spawn`, `_dm_classify`,
# `_dm_dispatch` all touch ONLY this module's own .bss slots
# (`_dm_light_fn`, `_dm_spawn_fn`, `_dm_light_count`,
# `_dm_heavy_count`, `_dm_last_decision`) and caller-supplied
# pointer args. The two weak-import function-pointer slots
# (`_dm_light_fn` and `_dm_spawn_fn`) are nil at session boot;
# the user-space pds-loader satellite's init (R131 pds-loader,
# future wave) publishes dispatcher addresses via the two
# registration primitives before the first `_dm_dispatch` call.
# The weak-import pattern mirrors R133.M5 `_tcg_check_fn` and
# R133.M6 `_sc_dispatch_fn` (`src/kernel/core/pds/script_cmd.pdx`
# lines 435, 497, 660) byte-for-byte minus the klog_s1 emits
# (user-space has no klog surface; the fingerprint lives in the
# two u64 counters a smoke-mode asserter reads).
#
# `_dm_classify` has NO nested CALL; `_dm_register_light` /
# `_dm_register_spawn` / `_dm_init` are pure leaves with no nested
# CALL. `_dm_dispatch` has TWO nested CALLs: one into `_dm_classify`
# (intra-module leaf) and one indirect call through the matching
# weak-import slot (`call rax` after `mov rax, [slot]`); frame is
# 4 pushes + sub rsp, 8 pad = rsp%16==0 at both call sites. All
# five public entry points can be linked standalone.
#
# No cross-module CALL into command_sig.pdx (R222.M2) or
# functor_instance.pdx (R222.M3) -- cs_body / cs_len / mat_body /
# mat_len are handed in as opaque caller-managed pointer args.
# `_dm_classify` reads a u32 at offset 84 of the CommandSig record
# which R222.M2 reserved for R222.M3+ expansion at landing time;
# the carve-out does not alter the R222.M2 ABI because every
# landed CommandSig reader stops at offset 84 (`flags u32`) and
# the R222.M8 fingerprint pass ingests the full 128-byte header
# verbatim so the new field is already covered.
#
# DispatchMode uses its OWN .bss state (five u64 scalars totaling
# 40 bytes @align(8)) which is disjoint from every other satellite
# module's .bss (parser-family: `_cmd_sig_*`, `_fi_*`, `_afs_*`,
# `_sch_sig_*`, `_pl_*`, `_rp_*`, `_ll_*`, `_cc_*`, `_dl_*`,
# `_cmdreg_*`; row-family: `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`, `_er_*`, `_cr_*`;
# session-family: `_se_env_pool`, `_te_scheme_pool`; lexer-family:
# `_cl_stack`, `_cl_depth`, `_cl_last_kind`; ast-family:
# `_ast_pool`, `_ast_pool_next`, `_ast_src_ptr`, `_ast_src_len`;
# scope-family: `_ls_frame_pool`, `_ls_frame_next`). The function-
# pointer slots are session-local; the counters are session-local
# (test harnesses reset them via `_dm_init`).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The classifier is a pure byte-walker +
# bit-test over caller-supplied data; the two weak-import slots
# are plain u64 function pointers, not KIND handles. The kernel-
# side spawn seam that `_dm_spawn_fn` eventually points at will
# itself touch KIND_PROCESS (R133 process-model) but that
# invocation lives behind the weak-import boundary, not in this
# module.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R221.M6 (span_map.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library linker-visible
# dependency at raw M6. `_sm_init`, `_sm_record_pair`, `_sm_lookup`
# are PURE LEAVES -- zero callee-save pushes, zero stack frames,
# zero nested CALLs; they touch ONLY this module's own .bss
# (`_sm_pairs` 8 KiB = 1024 slots x 8 B, `_sm_pair_count` u64) and
# caller-supplied pointer args. `_sm_from_u8nfc` has a 5-push +
# 16 B frame giving rsp%16==0 at the two nested CALLs (`_sm_init`
# and `_sm_record_pair`); r11 is NEVER held across a nested CALL
# -- state survives in r12..r15 and in the stack local `[rsp+0]`.
# All four public entry points can be linked standalone. R221.M6.1+
# (AST wire-up at `_ast_render`) will introduce a one-way intra-
# library dependency where `ast.pdx` CALLs `_sm_lookup` to refine
# the pre-NFC offset before span-copying; no cross-library
# dependency is planned at that milestone either.
#
# SpanMap uses its OWN .bss state (`_sm_pairs` 8 KiB, `_sm_pair_count`
# u64) which is disjoint from every other satellite module's .bss
# (parser-family: `_cmd_sig_*`, `_fi_*`, `_afs_*`, `_sch_sig_*`,
# `_pl_*`, `_rp_*`, `_ll_*`, `_cc_*`, `_dl_*`, `_cmdreg_*`,
# `_dm_*`; row-family: `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`, `_er_*`, `_cr_*`;
# session-family: `_se_env_pool`, `_te_scheme_pool`; lexer-family:
# `_cl_stack`, `_cl_depth`, `_cl_last_kind`; ast-family:
# `_ast_pool`, `_ast_pool_next`, `_ast_src_ptr`, `_ast_src_len`;
# scope-family: `_ls_frame_pool`, `_ls_frame_next`). The pair pool
# is a parse-time working-memory artefact rebuilt per
# `_sm_from_u8nfc` call; no cross-module .bss aliasing hazard
# exists.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The span map is a pure in-process sideband
# table over caller-supplied source + NFC byte buffers; no KIND
# surface is touched by this module. The two R221.M1 input buffers
# (`bytes_src`, `bytes_nfc`) are passed in by raw pointer; the
# module never reads past `src_len` / `nfc_len`.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R225.M4 (effect_unify.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies. It introduces TWO intra-library
# dependencies (dual with R224.M3 EffectRow + R224.M4 CapRow,
# matching the row-primitive consumer pattern):
#
#   R225.M2 RowTypes  --  called at:
#     _rt_find         -- resolve a walk cursor through the TyVar OR
#                         ROW_VAR resolved chain (`_eu_unify` walk-
#                         loop head, once per label).
#     _rt_row_empty    -- allocate the closed empty-row tail of the
#                         join (`_eu_unify` build phase when neither
#                         input carries a ROW_VAR tail).
#     _rt_row_var      -- allocate a fresh ROW_VAR tail of the join
#                         (`_eu_unify` build phase when at least one
#                         input carries a ROW_VAR tail).
#     _rt_row_extend   -- prepend a ROW_EXT with packed label id onto
#                         the accumulator (`_eu_unify` build phase,
#                         once per unique label).
#     _rt_row_select   -- dedup check against the current accumulator
#                         (`_eu_unify` build phase, once per candidate
#                         label; RT_OK on duplicate causes a skip).
#
#   R225.M1 HmCore   --  touches ONLY the shared .bss row-graph pool
#                        indirectly, via the R225.M2 primitives above;
#                        no direct call into HmCore. The `_hm_pool`
#                        .bss symbol IS loaded by this module's
#                        `_eu_unify` via `lea r11, [rip + _hm_pool]`
#                        to compute node addresses on walked cursors
#                        (same precedent as R224.M3 EffectRow +
#                        R224.M4 CapRow).
#
# `_eu_check_rank` is a pure byte walker over caller-supplied data:
# zero callee-save pushes, no stack frame, no nested CALL. `_eu_least`
# calls `_eu_unify` intra-module and holds its 4-push + 8 B align
# frame (= 40 B); the single nested CALL to `_eu_unify` sees rsp%16==0.
# All three public entry points (`_eu_unify`, `_eu_check_rank`,
# `_eu_least`) can be linked against the RowTypes/HmCore objects that
# R225.M1/M2 already consume; no new cross-library dependency is
# introduced.
#
# EffectUnify uses its OWN .bss scratch (`_eu_label_scratch`, 128 B =
# 32 u32 packed-label-id slots) for the Phase 1 raw-collect buffer,
# so it does NOT alias R225.M1's `_hm_subst_table`, R225.M2's
# `_rt_row_restrict`/`_rt_row_concat` scratch slice at
# `_hm_subst_table[384..512)`, R224.M3's `_er_parse_scratch` (64 B),
# OR R224.M4's `_cr_parse_scratch` (64 B). The four scratch surfaces
# could in principle be unified at a later M, but the disjoint carve-
# out keeps the four sub-languages (effect-row join / record-row
# restrict+concat / effect-row parse / capability-row parse)
# independently reviewable AND lets effect-row join operations run
# concurrently with effect-row/capability-row parse in the same
# shell turn without interleave hazards.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Effect rows are pure type-level artefacts;
# the kernel-side cap table never flows through this module. Effect-
# lattice evaluation runs purely in parse/typecheck time, not against
# the kernel's cap-handle surface or any other KIND.

# ---- Error band 0x4A00..0x4AFF (R221.M7, fuzz_harness) ---------------
#
# Parser-tier fuzz-driver survivability status range.
#
#   FH_OK                   : 0        drive returned without crash
#                                      (any downstream parser error
#                                      status is swallowed; corpus
#                                      failure is non-return).
#   FH_ERR_NULL_INPUT       : 0x4A00   bytes == 0 with len > 0.
#   FH_ERR_INPUT_TOO_LARGE  : 0x4A01   len > FH_MAX_INPUT_BYTES
#                                      (16384).
#
# Slots 0x4A02..0x4AFF reserved for R221.M7.1+ (stage-wise taxonomy
# of swallowed-error histograms, AFL/libfuzzer bridge glue, 24h-run
# crash-trace capture).
#
# ---- Error band 0x4C00..0x4CFF (R223.M3, filter_op) ------------------
#
# Pipeline-tier `where`/`filter`/`by` combinator parse status range.
#
#   FO_OK                  : 0        parse succeeded; 32B FilterOp
#                                     record emitted.
#   FO_ERR_SYNTAX          : 0x4C00   grammar fault at head keyword.
#   FO_ERR_NO_OPEN_BRACE   : 0x4C01   `where`/`filter` tail did not
#                                     start with `{`.
#   FO_ERR_NO_CLOSE_BRACE  : 0x4C02   EOI before matching `}`.
#   FO_ERR_NO_PIPE_BINDER  : 0x4C03   `{` not followed by `|binder|`.
#   FO_ERR_NAME_TOO_LONG   : 0x4C04   binder-ident byte length > cap.
#   FO_ERR_EMPTY_BODY      : 0x4C05   `|binder|` with no body bytes.
#   FO_ERR_NO_BY_KEYWORD   : 0x4C06   `_fo_parse_by` leading argv
#                                     was not the `by` keyword.
#   FO_ERR_HEAD_MISMATCH   : 0x4C07   `_fo_parse_where` saw `filter`
#                                     (or vice versa).
#   FO_ERR_BAD_MODE        : 0x4C08   `_fo_parse_body` called with
#                                     mode not in {0,1,2}.
#   FO_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL   : 0x4C09   out_cap < FO_RECORD_BYTES (32).
#
# Slots 0x4C0A..0x4CFF reserved for R223.M3.1+ follow-ups (shorthand
# compare-op desugaring, `by`-chain reduction, record-field name
# resolution against R221.M5 Ast nodes).
#
# ---- Error band 0x4D00..0x4DFF (R223.M4, sort_group_reduce) ----------
#
# Pipeline-tier `sort`/`group`/`reduce` combinator parse status range.
#
#   SGR_OK                  : 0        parse succeeded; 32B
#                                      SortGroupReduce record emitted.
#   SGR_ERR_SYNTAX          : 0x4D00   grammar fault anywhere.
#   SGR_ERR_HEAD_MISMATCH   : 0x4D01   head keyword mismatch (the
#                                      three wrappers verify their
#                                      own keyword as a sanity check).
#   SGR_ERR_NO_BY           : 0x4D02   sort/group tail missing the
#                                      `by` keyword.
#   SGR_ERR_EMPTY_BODY      : 0x4D03   expression body empty after
#                                      WS / asc / desc trim.
#   SGR_ERR_NO_OPEN_BRACE   : 0x4D04   reduce tail did not start with
#                                      `{`.
#   SGR_ERR_NO_CLOSE_BRACE  : 0x4D05   reduce reached EOI before `}`.
#   SGR_ERR_NO_PIPE         : 0x4D06   reduce binder prefix missing a
#                                      `|` boundary.
#   SGR_ERR_NAME_TOO_LONG   : 0x4D07   reduce binder ident exceeded
#                                      SGR_NAME_MAX (14).
#   SGR_ERR_NO_SEED         : 0x4D08   reduce missing a non-empty
#                                      seed expression after `}`.
#   SGR_ERR_BAD_KIND        : 0x4D09   _sgr_parse_impl called with
#                                      kind not in {0,1,2}.
#   SGR_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL   : 0x4D0A   out_cap < SGR_RECORD_BYTES
#                                      (32).
#
# Slots 0x4D0B..0x4DFF reserved for R223.M4.1+ follow-ups (nested-
# brace balancing in reduce bodies; `sort by K1, K2, ...` multi-key;
# `group by K having PRED` filter clause; `reduce` with inline
# accumulator type annotation).
#
# ---- Linker dependencies (Wave 124) ----------------------------------
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R221.M7 (fuzz_harness.pdx) depends on the full
# parser-tier object graph: `_u8n_decode_and_normalize` (R221.M1),
# `_gi_count_clusters` (R221.M2), `_cl_init` + `_cl_tokenize`
# (R221.M4), `_pl_parse` (R223.M1), `_dl_parse` (R226.M1), and
# `_ll_parse` (R224.M1). All six are intra-library; no cross-library
# dep. The `.bss` scratch buffers (`_fh_nfc_buf` 32768B, `_fh_tok_buf`
# 8192B, `_fh_pl_buf` 1024B, `_fh_dl_buf` 2048B, `_fh_ll_buf` 1024B)
# are module-private.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R223.M3 (filter_op.pdx) has no external link
# dependency at M3: it stores the predicate body as a byte span and
# does not call `_ll_parse` (the HM pass does, later).
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R223.M4 (sort_group_reduce.pdx) has no external
# link dependency at M4: like FilterOp, it stores the key / reducer /
# seed bodies as byte spans and does not call `_ll_parse` (the HM
# pass runs it later over the stored spans). The module's `.bss`
# footprint is ZERO at M4 (no label-intern table; no scheme pool);
# the only intra-library call is `_sgr_parse_impl -> _sgr_skip_ws`.

# ---- Error band 0x4E00..0x4EFF (R229.M4, job_control) ----------------
#
# Parser-tier job-control packed-status range. Seven slots used at
# R229.M4:
#
#   JC_OK                   : 0        job op succeeded.
#   JC_ERR_POOL_FULL        : 0x4E00   _jc_submit saw all 32 slots in
#                                      a non-DONE, magic='JOBS' state.
#   JC_ERR_BAD_ID           : 0x4E01   job_id == 0, > JC_POOL_SLOTS
#                                      (32), or the referenced slot's
#                                      magic != 'JOBS'.
#   JC_ERR_BAD_STATE        : 0x4E02   state-machine transition
#                                      rejected (see job_control.pdx
#                                      §state machine).
#   JC_ERR_BAD_MODE         : 0x4E03   _jc_submit with mode not in
#                                      {0 FOREGROUND, 1 BACKGROUND}.
#   JC_ERR_BAD_CMD          : 0x4E04   _jc_submit with cmd_ptr == 0
#                                      or cmd_len == 0.
#   JC_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL    : 0x4E05   _jc_list out_cap would be
#                                      exceeded by the next 48-byte
#                                      copy.
#   JC_ERR_BAD_OUT_PTR      : 0x4E06   _jc_submit with
#                                      out_job_id_ptr == 0.
#
# Slots 0x4E07..0x4EFF reserved for R229.M4.1+ (JC_ERR_SCHEDULER_
# UNAVAILABLE on the R229.M5 scheduler wire-up, JC_ERR_CAP_DENIED on
# a KIND_JOB cap-invoke surface at M5.1, JC_ERR_NAME_TOO_LONG on an
# extended-name arena at M5.2, etc.).
#
# ---- R229.M4 catalogue paragraph (job_control) -----------------------
#
#   R229.M4 JobControl (job_control.pdx, landed): per-shell-session
#      Job-row table (32 slots x 48 B = 1536 B pool, singleton .bss)
#      plus a two-slot scalar header (`_jc_job_count` u64 append
#      cursor, `_jc_fg_tid` u64 current-foreground-tid pointer). Six
#      public entry points:
#      `_jc_init` (pure-leaf boot hook that scrubs the two scalars;
#        the pool itself relies on .bss zero-init for the magic==0
#        free-slot invariant),
#      `_jc_submit(cmd_ptr, cmd_len, mode_u8, out_job_id_ptr)`
#        (6-push pure-leaf scanner that allocates the first slot with
#        magic==0 OR state==JC_STATE_DONE, writes a Job record with
#        magic='JOBS' (0x53424F4A LE), 1-based job_id = slot_idx+1,
#        state=JC_STATE_NEW, mode=input, flags/reserved=0, tid=0,
#        command_inline (min(cmd_len, 16) bytes zero-padded past),
#        cmd_body_ref=0, writes job_id through out_job_id_ptr, bumps
#        _jc_job_count iff the slot was brand-new (magic==0 before
#        write) -- stride 48 computed via `(idx<<5) + (idx<<4)` per
#        the cmd_registry.pdx 48-byte entry stride precedent),
#      `_jc_bg(job_id)` (pure-leaf state transition STOPPED->RUNNING
#        with mode:=BACKGROUND; clears _jc_fg_tid if slot was fg
#        owner),
#      `_jc_fg(job_id)` (pure-leaf state transition STOPPED->RUNNING
#        with mode:=FOREGROUND; writes _jc_fg_tid := slot.tid),
#      `_jc_list(out, out_cap)` (4-push pure-leaf walker that copies
#        each magic='JOBS', state!=DONE record contiguously into the
#        caller buffer; returns packed (bytes_written << 32) |
#        status; the prompt-count caller divides bytes_written by 48),
#      `_jc_update_state(job_id, new_state)` (pure-leaf scheduler
#        callback; enforces the policy "cur != new, cur != DONE,
#        new != NEW, new in [0..3]"; on DONE transition of the
#        current fg owner, clears _jc_fg_tid).
#      All six entry points are pure leaves (zero nested CALL) so no
#      alignment pad is needed; the 6-push and 4-push shapes
#      naturally keep rsp balanced at ret. Submit's scanner reuses
#      slots with state==JC_STATE_DONE OR magic==0 so the pool is
#      effectively a 32-slot ring under M4's acceptance workload
#      (10-job state-transition test). No KIND; no external cross-
#      library dependency; no intra-library cross-module call --
#      JobControl is a self-contained working-memory substrate. The
#      kernel-side R127.M5 (src/kernel/core/svc/shell_jobs.pdx) and
#      THIS user-side table reconcile at the R229.M5 scheduler wire-
#      up (both receive the same lifecycle events; at M4 only the
#      user-side call is landed).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Job rows carry opaque command byte spans and
# scheduled-thread tids; the kernel-side scheduler surface (R107+)
# never flows through this module.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R229.M4 (job_control.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module
# linker dependency. All six public entry points (`_jc_init`,
# `_jc_submit`, `_jc_bg`, `_jc_fg`, `_jc_list`, `_jc_update_state`)
# touch ONLY this module's own .bss (`_jc_jobs` 1536 B =
# 32 slots x 48 B, `_jc_job_count` u64, `_jc_fg_tid` u64) and
# caller-supplied pointer args. JobControl's .bss is disjoint from
# every other satellite module's .bss (parser-family: `_cmd_sig_*`,
# `_fi_*`, `_afs_*`, `_sch_sig_*`; row-family: `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`,
# `_er_*`, `_cr_*`; session-family: `_se_env_pool`,
# `_te_scheme_pool`; lexer-family: `_cl_stack`, `_cl_depth`,
# `_cl_last_kind`; scope-family: `_ls_frame_pool`, `_ls_frame_next`;
# registry-family: `_cmdreg_entries`, `_cmdreg_*`).

# ---- Error band 0x5000..0x50FF (R224.M6, pipeline_lambda) ------------
#
# Lambda-lang first-class-pipeline-value packed-status range. Nine
# slots used at R224.M6:
#
#   PL_LAM_OK                        : 0        operation succeeded.
#   PL_LAM_ERR_SYNTAX                : 0x5000   reserved for the
#                                                M6.2 `_pl_lam_parse_
#                                                literal` convenience
#                                                landing (one-shot
#                                                lambda-source parse
#                                                + box).
#   PL_LAM_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL         : 0x5001   `_pl_lam_box`: out_cap
#                                                < PL_LAM_RECORD_BYTES
#                                                (32).
#   PL_LAM_ERR_BAD_MAGIC             : 0x5002   `_pl_lam_unbox` /
#                                                `_pl_lam_apply_to_
#                                                combinator`: input
#                                                record magic !=
#                                                'PLAM' (0x4D414C50
#                                                LE).
#   PL_LAM_ERR_RECORD_TOO_SMALL      : 0x5003   `_pl_lam_unbox` /
#                                                `_pl_lam_apply_to_
#                                                combinator`:
#                                                record_len < 32.
#   PL_LAM_ERR_NULL_OUT              : 0x5004   reserved for a future
#                                                strict-mode unbox
#                                                variant that refuses
#                                                any null out-ptr
#                                                (raw M6 treats null
#                                                out-ptr as a
#                                                legitimate skip).
#   PL_LAM_ERR_BAD_COMBINATOR        : 0x5005   `_pl_lam_apply_to_
#                                                combinator`:
#                                                combinator magic
#                                                not in {FILT, SORT,
#                                                GRPS, REDU}.
#   PL_LAM_ERR_COMBINATOR_TOO_SMALL  : 0x5006   `_pl_lam_apply_to_
#                                                combinator`:
#                                                comb_len < 32.
#   PL_LAM_ERR_NULL_RECORD           : 0x5007   `_pl_lam_box` /
#                                                `_pl_lam_unbox` /
#                                                `_pl_lam_apply_to_
#                                                combinator`: a
#                                                required record
#                                                pointer was 0.
#   PL_LAM_ERR_BAD_INDEX             : 0x5008   `_pl_lam_box`: one of
#                                                the four pool indices
#                                                had bits 32..63 set
#                                                (not a valid u32).
#
# The 0x5000..0x50FF range is reserved for PipelineLambda packed-
# status codes across R224.M6+. R224.M1 (lambda_lit) owns the
# disjoint 0x2C00..0x2CFF slice; R224.M2 (closure_capture) owns
# 0x3500..0x35FF; R224.M3 (effect_row) owns 0x4000..0x40FF; R224.M4
# (cap_row) owns 0x4300..0x43FF; R224.M5 (let_scope) owns
# 0x4600..0x46FF -- the six R224 submodules do not share status
# codes because a downstream dispatcher's compound error path must
# distinguish "the lambda header faulted" from "the capture list
# faulted" from "the effect annotation faulted" from "the capability
# annotation faulted" from "the scope frame pool faulted" from "the
# pipeline-value box / unbox / apply faulted". Slots 0x5009..0x50FF
# reserved for R224.M6.1+ follow-ups (closure-arena allocation
# faults; cross-session recover faults; rank-restriction violations;
# per-session PipelineLambda cache misses; combinator-schema
# mismatch for a future STRICT apply mode that validates the
# combinator's expected arity before poking).
#
# R224.M6 PipelineLambda carves 0x5000..0x50FF on a new kilo-step
# boundary past Wave 125's R229.M4 JobControl (0x4E00..0x4EFF) and
# past the parallel R222.M8 reservation (0x4F00..0x4FFF for the
# Wave 125 sibling arm). Disjoint from every prior landed wave's
# bands.
#
# Grep confirmation at R224.M6 landing time:
#   grep -rE '0x500[0-8]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file +
#        src/pipeline_lambda.pdx +
#        design/shell/lambda-as-pipeline-value.md. (0x50414343
#        appearing as `CC_HEADER_MAGIC` in closure_capture.pdx is
#        the 'CCAP' FourCC, NOT a packed-status code; 0x5000 as an
#        IOMMU page-table address in tools/paideia-as/.../vtd_qi_
#        smoke.rs is a hardware address constant, NOT a packed-
#        status code -- both sit in disjoint semantic namespaces.)
#
# PipelineLambda record (32 B):
#
#   offset  size  field
#   ------  ----  -------------------------------------------------------
#     0      4   magic              u32 = 'PLAM' LE (0x4D414C50).
#     4      2   flags              u16: bit 0 CLOSURE, bit 1 HAS_EFFECT,
#                                        bit 2 HAS_CAP, bits 3..15 reserved.
#     6      2   reserved           u16 (zeroed).
#     8      4   lambda_lit_idx     u32 -- R224.M1 LambdaLit pool idx.
#    12      4   closure_env_idx    u32 -- R224.M2 ClosureCapture pool idx
#                                          (0 if no captures).
#    16      4   effect_row_idx     u32 -- R224.M3 EffectRow / R225.M2
#                                          RowTypes pool idx (0 if pure).
#    20      4   cap_row_idx        u32 -- R224.M4 CapRow / R225.M2
#                                          RowTypes pool idx (0 if ambient).
#    24      8   reserved           u64 (zeroed; reserved for M6.1+).
#
# Combinator-side poke targets. Both FilterOp (R223.M3) and
# SortGroupReduce (R223.M4) reserve the last 4 bytes of their 32-byte
# stride; `_pl_lam_apply_to_combinator` writes the PipelineLambda's
# `lambda_lit_idx` u32 at `[combinator + 28]` in both cases. The
# "lambda-sourced predicate" flag bit lives at per-record offsets:
#
#   FilterOp:         `flags u8 at +17`, bit 1 (0x02).  Reserved slot
#                     in FilterOp's M3 ABI; see filter_op.pdx line
#                     "bit 0 = HAS_BY; bits 1..7 reserved".
#   SortGroupReduce:  `flags u8 at +5`,  bit 2 (0x04).  Reserved slot
#                     in SortGroupReduce's M4 ABI; see sort_group_
#                     reduce.pdx line "bits 2..7 reserved".
#
# Both bits sit in slots the parent records explicitly reserved for
# M3.1+ / M4.1+ follow-ups and are therefore available at raw M6
# without an ABI break to either combinator module.
#
# ---- R224.M6 catalogue paragraph (pipeline_lambda) -------------------
#
#   R224.M6 PipelineLambda (pipeline_lambda.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      first-class-pipeline-value substrate. A 32-byte PipelineLambda
#      record ('PLAM' magic + four u32 pool indices for the lambda's
#      literal, closure env, effect row, capability row + a flag u16
#      + 10 bytes reserved). Four public entry points:
#      `_pl_lam_init` (pure-leaf idempotent boot hook; bare `ret` --
#        no `.bss` state at M6),
#      `_pl_lam_box(ll_idx, cc_idx, er_idx, cr_idx, out, out_cap)`
#        (pure-leaf packer; validates out != 0, out_cap >= 32, and
#        each of the four indices fits in u32 (`shr by 32, cmp 0`);
#        zero-fills the 32-byte record via four u64 stores; writes
#        magic at +0, flags u16 at +4 (bits 0/1/2 set from the three
#        nonzero-idx tests), four u32 pool indices at 8/12/16/20),
#      `_pl_lam_unbox(record, record_len, out_ll, out_cc, out_er,
#        out_cr)` (pure-leaf decomposer; validates record != 0,
#        record_len >= 32, magic == 'PLAM'; writes each nonzero
#        out-ptr with the matching u32 zero-extended to u64),
#      `_pl_lam_apply_to_combinator(pipelam, pipelam_len, comb,
#        comb_len)` (pure-leaf threader; validates both ptrs != 0,
#        both lens >= 32, PipelineLambda magic, combinator magic in
#        {FILT, SORT, GRPS, REDU}; reads PipelineLambda's lambda_lit_
#        idx u32 at +8; writes it at [comb+28] via mov_d; ORs the
#        lambda-sourced flag bit into the combinator's flags byte
#        (FilterOp bit 1 at +17; SortGroupReduce bit 2 at +5) via
#        load-op-store).
#      All four entry points are PURE LEAVES (zero callee-save
#      pushes, zero stack frame, zero nested CALL) so no alignment
#      pad is needed; `r11` is used freely as a staging register
#      because no nested CALL exists. No KIND; no external cross-
#      library dependency; no intra-library cross-module call --
#      PipelineLambda is a self-contained value constructor /
#      destructor over caller-supplied pool indices and record
#      pointers. The four referenced subsystems (R224.M1 LambdaLit,
#      R224.M2 ClosureCapture, R224.M3 EffectRow, R224.M4 CapRow)
#      are STRUCTURAL dependencies but NOT linker-visible ones; the
#      pool indices stored in the record are opaque u32 handles that
#      the downstream HM pass dereferences.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. PipelineLambda carries only opaque u32
# pool indices; no KIND surface is touched by this module.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R224.M6 (pipeline_lambda.pdx) has NO external
# cross-library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-
# module linker dependency. All four public entry points (`_pl_lam_
# init`, `_pl_lam_box`, `_pl_lam_unbox`, `_pl_lam_apply_to_
# combinator`) touch ONLY caller-supplied pointer args; the module
# carries no `.bss` state at raw M6. The four referenced pool
# families (R224.M1 LambdaLit, R224.M2 ClosureCapture, R224.M3
# EffectRow, R224.M4 CapRow) are STRUCTURAL dependencies (the stored
# u32 handles are indices into their respective pools) but NOT
# linker-visible: the downstream HM pass that dereferences the
# handles lives at the R225 wire-up layer, not in this module. All
# four public entry points can be linked standalone against a bare
# parser-tier object graph; no new `--extra-obj-dir` is introduced.
# R224.M6.1+ (closure-arena materialisation, cross-session
# serialisation) will introduce a one-way intra-library dependency
# on R224.M2 ClosureCapture bytes; no cross-library dependency is
# planned at those milestones either.

# ---- Error band 0x5100..0x51FF (R229.M5, history) --------------------
#
# Parser-tier history packed-status range. Six slots used at R229.M5:
#
#   H_OK                   : 0        operation succeeded.
#   H_ERR_BAD_CMD          : 0x5100   _h_append with cmd_ptr == 0 OR
#                                     cmd_len == 0.
#   H_ERR_BAD_SUMMARY      : 0x5101   _h_append with summary_len > 0
#                                     AND summary_ptr == 0.
#   H_ERR_BAD_TURN         : 0x5102   _h_get_turn with turn_id == 0,
#                                     > entry_count, OR outside the
#                                     live-ring window (entry_count
#                                     - turn_id >= 128).
#   H_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL    : 0x5103   _h_get_turn with out_cap < 128.
#   H_ERR_BAD_OUT_PTR      : 0x5104   _h_get_turn with out == 0;
#                                     _h_search_substring with
#                                     out_turn_ids == 0.
#   H_ERR_BAD_NEEDLE       : 0x5105   _h_search_substring with
#                                     needle == 0, needle_len == 0,
#                                     OR needle_len > 64.
#
# Slots 0x5106..0x51FF reserved for R229.M5.1+ follow-ups
# (H_ERR_PERSIST_UNAVAILABLE on the pdxfs CoW persistence bridge,
# H_ERR_CAP_DENIED on a KIND_HISTORY cap-invoke surface, H_ERR_FS_
# FULL on a per-user-history-file quota hit, etc.).
#
# The 0x5100..0x51FF carve-out skips the 0x4F00..0x4FFF reservation
# (R222.M8 describe serialisation, deferred) and the 0x5000..0x50FF
# carve-out (R224.M6 PipelineLambda, landed this wave). History's
# band is disjoint from every other parser-tier packed-status range
# in the paideia-os tree so a downstream compound-error dispatcher
# can discriminate "the history substrate faulted" from every other
# satellite's failure modes by a single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x51`
# idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R229.M5 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x510[0-5]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
#        design/shell/history-storage.md + history.pdx. (The TPM /
#        wifi driver `0x510x` constants and the SLOG_INIT_MARK
#        0x510C sentinel in src/kernel/core/semterm/session_log.pdx
#        are MMIO offsets / init-marker constants, not status codes;
#        the grep uses `0x510[0-5]\b` and will not match them.)

# ---- R229.M5 catalogue paragraph (history) ---------------------------
#
#   R229.M5 History (history.pdx, landed): per-shell-session history
#      substrate backing Ctrl-R search, `history` built-in output,
#      and up-arrow turn-recall. 128-slot ring of 128-byte History
#      rows plus a two-slot scalar header (`_h_entry_count` u64
#      monotonic turn counter, `_h_cursor` u64 next-write-slot
#      pointer wrapping at 128). Five public entry points:
#      `_h_init` (pure-leaf boot hook that scrubs the two scalars;
#        the pool itself relies on .bss zero-init for the magic==0
#        never-used-slot invariant),
#      `_h_append(cmd_ptr, cmd_len, result_code, cap_env_mask,
#                 summary_ptr, summary_len)`
#        (6-push pure-leaf allocator that writes a 128-byte History
#        record: magic 'HIST' (0x54534948 LE), turn_id = entry_count
#        + 1, rdtsc timestamp u64, FNV-1a 64 cmd_hash u64, result_code
#        u8 low 8, flags u8 0, reserved u16 0, cap_env_mask u32 low
#        32, cmd_inline 64 bytes zero-padded, summary_inline 16
#        bytes zero-padded, two u64 reserved 0; bumps entry_count by
#        1 and advances cursor := (cursor + 1) & 127; FNV-1a 64 is
#        INLINED so the function remains a pure leaf; stride 128
#        addressed via `shl rN, 7`),
#      `_h_get_turn(turn_id, out, out_cap)` (pure-leaf fetch by
#        turn_id; validates turn_id is in the live-ring window
#        [max(1, entry_count - 127), entry_count]; copies 128 B via
#        16 u64 moves; returns packed (128 << 32) | H_OK),
#      `_h_search_substring(needle, needle_len, start_turn,
#                           out_turn_ids, max_results)` (6-push
#        pure-leaf walker that iterates newest-to-oldest from
#        start_turn (or entry_count if 0) down to max(1, entry_count
#        - 127), substring-matches each slot's cmd_inline against
#        needle, writes matching turn_ids as u32 to the caller
#        buffer, caps at max_results; returns packed (result_count
#        << 32) | H_OK),
#      `_h_count()` (pure-leaf scalar accessor returning
#        _h_entry_count = session-lifetime monotonic turn count).
#      All five entry points are pure leaves (zero nested CALL) so
#      no alignment pad is needed; the 6-push shapes of `_h_append`
#      and `_h_search_substring` naturally keep rsp balanced at ret.
#      Append's slot addressing uses `shl rN, 7` (power-of-two
#      stride; no (<<5)+(<<4) 48-stride dance). cmd_inline + summary_
#      inline copies precompute `rsi = slot_addr + 32` / `rsi =
#      slot_addr + 96` to sidestep the mov_b [reg + reg + imm], reg
#      encoder gap (precedent: session_env.pdx, job_control.pdx). No
#      KIND; no external cross-library dependency; no intra-library
#      cross-module call -- History is a self-contained working-
#      memory substrate. The kernel-side R127.M4 (src/kernel/core/
#      svc/shell_history.pdx) and THIS user-side table co-exist with
#      disjoint reach (kernel-side via KIND_SHELL_SESSION cap, user-
#      side via direct link); R229.M5.1 (pdxfs CoW persistence) adds
#      a serialisation hook that writes the user-side ring to a per-
#      user history file via a kernel bridge.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. History records carry opaque command byte
# spans, FNV-1a 64 hashes, and an opaque cap_env_mask u32 snapshot;
# the kernel-side cap table (R107+) never flows through this module.
# The pdxfs CoW persistence hook planned at R229.M5.1 introduces a
# KIND_PDXFS reach, but at raw M5 the substrate stays pure in-
# process.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R229.M5 (history.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module
# linker dependency. All five public entry points (`_h_init`,
# `_h_append`, `_h_get_turn`, `_h_search_substring`, `_h_count`)
# touch ONLY this module's own .bss (`_h_entries` 16384 B = 128
# slots x 128 B, `_h_entry_count` u64, `_h_cursor` u64) and caller-
# supplied pointer args. History's .bss is disjoint from every
# other satellite module's .bss (parser-family: `_cmd_sig_*`,
# `_fi_*`, `_afs_*`, `_sch_sig_*`; row-family: `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`,
# `_er_*`, `_cr_*`; session-family: `_se_env_pool`,
# `_te_scheme_pool`; lexer-family: `_cl_stack`, `_cl_depth`,
# `_cl_last_kind`; scope-family: `_ls_frame_pool`, `_ls_frame_next`;
# registry-family: `_cmdreg_entries`, `_cmdreg_*`; job-family:
# `_jc_jobs`, `_jc_job_count`, `_jc_fg_tid`).

# ---- Error band 0x4F00..0x4FFF (R222.M8, describe_sig) ---------------
#
# Describe-wire packed-status range for the R222 cmd-functor wave's
# serialization milestone (`describe <cmd>` queryable record). Four
# slots used at R222.M8:
#
#   DS_OK                     : 0        success on every entry point.
#   DS_ERR_BAD_RECORD         : 0x4F00   `_ds_serialize`: cs_body == 0,
#                                        cs_len < 128, or CommandSig
#                                        magic ('CSIG' = 0x47495343)
#                                        mismatch at [cs_body + 0].
#   DS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL      : 0x4F01   `_ds_serialize`: out == 0 or
#                                        out_cap < DS_BLOB_BYTES (256).
#   DS_ERR_BAD_BLOB           : 0x4F02   `_ds_deserialize`: blob == 0,
#                                        blob_len < 256, null out-ptr,
#                                        or magic ('DESC' = 0x43534544)
#                                        / version u16 (= 1) mismatch
#                                        in the blob header.
#   DS_ERR_TOO_MANY_SCHEMAS   : 0x4F03   reserved for the M8.1
#                                        companion-signature folder
#                                        path that will populate
#                                        schema_hash_ids from a
#                                        SchemasSig body carrying more
#                                        than DS_SCHEMA_HASH_IDS_MAX
#                                        (32) tags; unused at raw M8.
#
# The 0x4F00..0x4FFF range is reserved for the R222.M8 describe-wire
# band. The six prior R222 submodule bands (0x2A00 SchemasSig, 0x3300
# CommandSig, 0x3900 FunctorInstance, 0x3C00 ArgFlagSpec, 0x4500
# CmdRegistry, 0x4800 DispatchMode) remain error-disjoint so a
# downstream dispatcher's compound error path can distinguish
# describe-serialization faults from every other R222 submodule fault.
# The 0x4F00..0x4FFF band also sits disjoint from the Wave 125
# 0x4E00..0x4EFF (R229.M4 JobControl) and the Wave 125+ 0x5000..0x50FF
# (R224.M6 PipelineLambda) bands.
#
# Slots 0x4F04..0x4FFF reserved for M8.1 (companion-signature folder
# follow-ups: DS_ERR_BAD_ARG_SPEC, DS_ERR_BAD_FLAG_SPEC,
# DS_ERR_BAD_SCHEMAS_SIG; one slot each on 0x4F04..0x4F06), M8.2
# (per-blob fingerprint mismatch on the proposed 32-byte DescriptorBlob
# fingerprint pass; 0x4F07), and M8.3 (query_payload decoder errors;
# 0x4F08..0x4F0F reserved as a sub-range once the semantic-query DSL
# at R229+ is specified).
#
# Grep confirmation at R222.M8 landing time:
#   grep -rE '0x4F0[0-3]' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file +
#        tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/src/describe_sig.pdx +
#        design/shell/describe-wire-format.md.
#
# ---- R222.M8 catalogue paragraph (describe_sig) ----------------------
#
#   R222.M8 DescribeSig (describe_sig.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      queryable DescriptorBlob serializer / deserializer for the
#      `describe <cmd>` builtin and the "commands operating on
#      <schema>" semantic-query surface. The record is DENORMALIZED
#      from the authoritative CommandSig (R222.M2) so that both
#      query shapes are O(1) index lookups rather than requiring
#      per-call reparse of the CommandSig body. 256-byte wire shape
#      (power-of-two stride for `shl idx, 8` catalogue indexing;
#      fits two cache lines on x86-64; see design/shell/describe-
#      wire-format.md §2.2 for the layout and §2.1 for per-field
#      rationale). Magic 'DESC' = 0x43534544 LE (reads as "DESC"
#      in a hex-dump sequential scan, per the Wave 125 SortGroupReduce
#      magic-reconciliation lesson). Version u16 = 1 locks the M8
#      commitment surface; a wire-format change bumps the field and
#      the deserializer rejects stale blobs with DS_ERR_BAD_BLOB.
#      Five public entry points, ALL PURE LEAVES:
#      `_ds_init()` -- bare `ret`; M8 holds NO .bss state (the blob
#        is caller-owned on every path, and the matches predicates
#        are pure bitmap tests over a caller-supplied blob). The
#        entry point exists for parallelism with the DispatchMode
#        (R222.M6) / CmdRegistry (R222.M5) init-hook family so a
#        later landing that adds .bss (per-session describe counters,
#        a schema_tag intern table) does not break the API.
#      `_ds_serialize(cs_body, cs_len, name_hash, out, out_cap)` --
#        validates cs_body != 0, cs_len >= 128, CSIG magic at
#        [cs_body + 0]; validates out != 0, out_cap >= 256; zero-
#        fills the 256-byte blob via a u64 stepping loop (32 iters
#        stepping by 8); stamps magic 'DESC' u32 at [+0], version
#        u16 lo byte 1 at [+4] (hi byte zero via zero-fill), flags
#        u16 copied byte-wise from CommandSigHeader+80..82 to [+6..+8]
#        (defensive two-byte copy covers any post-M2 extension of the
#        low 16 bits), name_hash u64 at [+8], name_inline[32] copied
#        from CommandSig[+8..+40] to blob[+16..+48] via a byte-by-byte
#        loop (32 iters stepping by 1), cs_body_ref u64 (= cs_body
#        input) at [+56]. Leaves arg_count / flag_count / schema_count
#        / reserved / arg_type_mask / schema_hash_ids / query_payload
#        zero (M8 default; M8.1 populates those from companion sigs).
#        Returns DS_OK (0) or DS_ERR_BAD_RECORD (0x4F00) /
#        DS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL (0x4F01).
#      `_ds_deserialize(blob, blob_len, out_cs_body_ref, out_name_hash)`
#        -- validates blob != 0, blob_len >= 256, null out-ptrs; reads
#        magic 'DESC' u32 at [+0] and version lo byte 1 at [+4];
#        extracts cs_body_ref u64 at [+56] into *out_cs_body_ref and
#        name_hash u64 at [+8] into *out_name_hash. Returns DS_OK (0)
#        or DS_ERR_BAD_BLOB (0x4F02).
#      `_ds_matches_type(blob, type_code)` -- tests bit type_code of
#        the arg_type_mask bitmap at [blob + 64..96]. Validates
#        blob != 0, type_code <= 255; computes byte_idx = type_code >> 3,
#        bit_idx = type_code & 7 (imm8 mask); loads byte at
#        [blob + 64 + byte_idx] (zero-ext); returns (byte >> bit_idx)
#        & 1 via `shr r, cl` + `and r, 1` (variable shift via CL;
#        encoder-validated precedent cap_row.pdx line 1204 `shl rdx,
#        cl`). At raw M8 the mask is zero-filled, so this returns 0
#        for every type_code until M8.1 wires in the companion-
#        signature folder.
#      `_ds_matches_schema(blob, schema_tag)` -- scans schema_hash_ids
#        at [blob + 96..128] for schema_tag. Validates blob != 0,
#        schema_tag in [1..255]; reads schema_count u16 at [blob + 52]
#        via two byte loads (lo | (hi << 8)); caps at
#        DS_SCHEMA_HASH_IDS_MAX (32); scans for a byte match. At raw
#        M8 schema_count is zero so this returns 0 for every
#        schema_tag until M8.1 wires in the companion-signature folder.
#      All five entry points are PURE LEAVES (zero callee-save pushes,
#      zero stack frame, zero nested CALL) so no alignment pad is
#      needed; r11 is used freely as a staging register because no
#      nested CALL exists. No KIND; no external cross-library
#      dependency; no intra-library cross-module call -- DescribeSig
#      is a self-contained wire-format packer / unpacker /
#      bitmap-tester over caller-supplied buffers. The denormalized
#      shape means the query path NEVER dereferences the cs_body_ref
#      pointer: `commands operating on <schema>` matches the
#      schema_hash_ids slot, `describe <cmd>` prints the name_inline
#      window and the type set from arg_type_mask -- all pure bitmap
#      tests against a 256-byte record. The cs_body_ref lets a caller
#      walk back to the authoritative CommandSig record on demand
#      (e.g., `describe --verbose <cmd>` renders the cap array by
#      reparsing the 128+32*n_caps original bytes).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. DescribeSig emits a flat 256-byte byte image
# over caller-supplied buffers; no KIND surface is touched by this
# module.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R222.M8 (describe_sig.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module linker
# dependency. All five public entry points (`_ds_init`, `_ds_serialize`,
# `_ds_deserialize`, `_ds_matches_type`, `_ds_matches_schema`) touch
# ONLY caller-supplied pointer args; the module carries no `.bss`
# state at raw M8. The CommandSig magic constant (0x47495343) is
# replicated as a local `DS_CS_HEADER_MAGIC` so the serializer can
# validate the input WITHOUT a cross-module linker dep on command_sig.
# pdx -- the same disjoint-leaf precedent as the parser-family's
# `_sch_sig_skip_ws` / `_cmd_sig_skip_ws` / `_fi_skip_ws` / `_afs_
# skip_ws` quartet. All five public entry points can be linked
# standalone against a bare parser-tier object graph; no new
# `--extra-obj-dir` is introduced. R222.M8.1+ (companion-signature
# folder: ArgSpec + FlagSpec + SchemasSig fold into arg_type_mask /
# schema_hash_ids / arg_count / flag_count / schema_count) will
# introduce a one-way intra-library structural dependency on R222.M1
# SchemasSig + R222.M4 ArgFlagSpec but STILL no linker-visible
# dependency: the body-byte buffers are handed in as opaque caller-
# managed pointers; no cross-library dependency is planned at those
# milestones either.

# ---- Error band 0x5300..0x53FF (R223.M6, hostname_annot) -------------
#
# Pipeline-tier `@hostname` annotation packed-status range. Seven
# slots used at R223.M6:
#
#   HA_OK                       : 0        parse or check succeeded.
#   HA_ERR_SYNTAX               : 0x5300   reserved for M6.1 strict-mode
#                                          refusal of the empty-@ idiom
#                                          (`@|`, `@ ` with no
#                                          hostname) under a thread-
#                                          local strict-federation
#                                          toggle. Unused at raw M6
#                                          (empty-@ silently emits a
#                                          LOCAL record).
#   HA_ERR_HOSTNAME_TOO_LONG    : 0x5301   `_ha_parse` saw a hostname
#                                          whose byte length exceeded
#                                          HA_HOSTNAME_MAX (16); the
#                                          packed `bytes_consumed`
#                                          field carries the end-of-
#                                          hostname offset for
#                                          diagnostics.
#   HA_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL        : 0x5302   `_ha_parse` with out == 0 or
#                                          out_cap < HA_RECORD_BYTES
#                                          (32); no record written.
#   HA_ERR_BAD_RECORD           : 0x5303   `_ha_check` with record == 0
#                                          OR magic u32 at [record+0]
#                                          != 0x54534F48 ('HOST' LE).
#   HA_ERR_HOST_MISMATCH        : 0x5304   `_ha_check` record vs host
#                                          disagree: LOCAL record with
#                                          non-empty host, REMOTE
#                                          record with empty host, or
#                                          REMOTE hash mismatch.
#   HA_ERR_BAD_HOST_PTR         : 0x5305   `_ha_check` with host_len >
#                                          0 AND host_ptr == 0.
#
# Slots 0x5306..0x53FF reserved for R223.M6.1+ follow-ups
# (HA_ERR_LONG_HOSTNAME_ARENA_FULL on the M6.1 pointer-indirect
# long-hostname mode, HA_ERR_FEDERATION_NOT_RESOLVED on the M6.3
# federation-syntax `@group:rule` landing, HA_ERR_CANONICAL_FORM on
# the M6.5 lowercase + Punycode-decode normalisation pass, etc.).
#
# The 0x5300..0x53FF band is reserved for the R223.M6 hostname-
# annotation band. The parallel R223.M5 arm occupies 0x5200..0x52FF
# (flag/positional partition); the two R223 submodule bands remain
# error-disjoint so a downstream dispatcher's compound error path
# can distinguish partition faults from hostname-annotation faults.
# R223.M1 (pipeline) owns 0x2B00..0x2BFF; R223.M2 (record_projection)
# owns 0x3400..0x34FF; R223.M3 (filter_op) owns 0x4C00..0x4CFF;
# R223.M4 (sort_group_reduce) owns 0x4D00..0x4DFF -- the six R223
# submodule bands remain error-disjoint for the same reason.
#
# Grep confirmation at R223.M6 landing time:
#   grep -rE '0x530[0-5]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file +
#        tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/src/hostname_annot.pdx +
#        design/shell/hostname-annotation.md. External `0x53XX`
#        sightings elsewhere in the parser library are FourCC magic
#        u32s (0x53505247 'GRPS', 0x53524F54 'SORT', 0x53424F4A
#        'JOBS', 0x534E4946 'FINS', 0x53475241 'ARGS') -- none of
#        them are packed-u64 return statuses in this library's
#        protocol.

# ---- R223.M6 catalogue paragraph (hostname_annot) --------------------
#
#   R223.M6 HostnameAnnot (hostname_annot.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      `@hostname` annotation recogniser. The pipeline-stage POST-pass
#      walks a stage's leading bytes and emits one 32-byte HostAnnot
#      record describing where the stage should execute: LOCAL (no
#      `@` prefix OR `@` with empty hostname) or REMOTE (hashed
#      hostname). Record shape: magic u32 'HOST' LE (0x54534F48 ->
#      disk bytes 48 4F 53 54 = "HOST" per the Wave 125 SortGroupReduce
#      magic-reconciliation lesson), flags u32 (bit 0 HA_FLAG_IS_LOCAL;
#      bits 1..31 reserved), hostname_hash u64 (FNV-1a 64 of hostname
#      bytes; zero iff local), hostname_inline [u8;16] (zero-padded;
#      all zero iff local). Stride is a power of two (32 bytes;
#      `shl rN, 5` indexing; no 2-op imul-r-imm pitfall).
#      Three public entry points:
#      `_ha_init()` -- bare `ret`; M6 holds NO .bss state (every
#        HostAnnot record is caller-owned on both the parse and
#        check paths; the check primitive operates as a pure FNV
#        compare over caller-supplied bytes). Pure leaf; kept for
#        parallelism with the DispatchMode (R222.M6) / CmdRegistry
#        (R222.M5) / DescribeSig (R222.M8) init-hook family so a
#        later landing that adds .bss (per-session hostname
#        counters, a hostname-intern table) does not break the API.
#      `_ha_parse(bytes, bytes_len, out, out_cap)` -- 6-push pure-
#        leaf parser; validates out != 0, out_cap >= 32; zero-fills
#        the 32-B record via four u64 stores; empty-input fast path
#        emits the LOCAL record; first-byte probe tests for '@'
#        (0x40); on '@' seen, advances cursor past '@', scans the
#        hostname run of bytes in [A-Za-z0-9.-] (hostname class),
#        guards hostname_len in [1..HA_HOSTNAME_MAX=16] (empty-@
#        falls to the LOCAL emit path; over-16 -> HA_ERR_HOSTNAME_
#        TOO_LONG); emits REMOTE record (magic + flags=0 + hash +
#        inline; hash via inlined FNV-1a 64 using 0xCBF29CE484222325
#        basis and 0x100000001B3 prime loaded via MOVABS); copies
#        hostname bytes to inline with zero-pad; walks trailing WS
#        in {SP, TAB, LF, CR}; returns packed (bytes_consumed << 32)
#        | HA_OK. Stride precomputation via `mov r9, out; add r9,
#        16; add r9, i` sidesteps the mov_b [reg + reg + off], reg
#        encoder gap (precedent session_env.pdx, job_control.pdx,
#        history.pdx). 6-push shape (rbx=bytes_ptr, rbp=bytes_len,
#        r12=out, r13=cursor, r14=hostname_start, r15=hostname_len);
#        pure leaf with no CALL -- no sub-rsp pad is needed
#        (precedent _h_append R229.M5 6-push shape).
#      `_ha_check(record, host_ptr, host_len)` -- 1-push pure-leaf
#        type-checker; validates record != 0, magic 'HOST' at
#        [record+0]; validates host_len > 0 => host_ptr != 0; reads
#        flags u32 at [record+4]; isolates IS_LOCAL bit via `and
#        rax, 1` (imm8); dispatches: IS_LOCAL=1 branch returns HA_OK
#        iff host_len==0, else HA_ERR_HOST_MISMATCH; IS_LOCAL=0
#        (remote) branch computes FNV-1a 64 of (host_ptr, host_len)
#        and compares to record.hostname_hash u64 at [record+8];
#        match -> HA_OK, mismatch -> HA_ERR_HOST_MISMATCH; empty host
#        on remote record -> HA_ERR_HOST_MISMATCH. 1-push (rbx holds
#        record across the FNV compare; host_len is saved to r8
#        before any `mul` instruction clobbers rdx); entry
#        rsp%16==8; +8 push = %16==0; no CALL; pop; ret.
#      All three entry points are pure leaves (no nested CALL; FNV
#      inlined on both _ha_parse and _ha_check). r11 is used freely
#      as a staging register because no CALL exists. No KIND; no
#      external cross-library dependency; no intra-library cross-
#      module call -- HostnameAnnot is a self-contained parse-time
#      annotation substrate over caller-supplied buffers.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. HostAnnot carries only a hostname hash + a
# 16-byte inline name; no KIND surface is touched by this module.
# The downstream encrypted-transport substrate (remote-shell daemon,
# R105+ hybrid KEM) is where KIND_NETIO + KIND_CRYPTO flow in; at
# raw M6 the substrate is NOT present and this module stays pure.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R223.M6 (hostname_annot.pdx) has NO external
# cross-library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-
# module linker dependency. All three public entry points
# (`_ha_init`, `_ha_parse`, `_ha_check`) touch ONLY caller-supplied
# pointer args; the module carries no `.bss` state at raw M6.
# FNV-1a 64 is INLINED in both `_ha_parse` and `_ha_check` (the
# constants 0xCBF29CE484222325 basis + 0x100000001B3 prime load via
# MOVABS imm64; precedent history.pdx `_h_append` lines 727-728) so
# no cross-module call to a shared FNV helper is required. The
# structural dependency on R223.M1 Pipeline (the stage's leading
# bytes this module post-passes on) is NOT linker-visible: the
# per-stage byte slice is handed in as caller-managed bytes. All
# three public entry points can be linked standalone against a bare
# parser-tier object graph; no new `--extra-obj-dir` is introduced.
# R223.M6.1+ (long-hostname pointer-indirect mode) will introduce
# a one-way STRUCTURAL dependency on a per-session long-hostname
# arena (caller-managed; no cross-module linker dep). R223.M6.3+
# (federation `@group:rule` syntax) is additive to the record's
# flag u32 bits; no linker-visible dependency. R223.M6.5+ (canonical-
# form normalisation) layers in-line over the hostname bytes before
# hashing; no cross-module linker dep. No cross-library dependency
# is planned at any of those milestones.

# ---- Error band 0x5400..0x54FF (R229.M6, line_editor) ----------------
#
# Parser-tier line-editor packed-status range. Seven slots used at
# R229.M6:
#
#   LE_OK                   : 0        operation succeeded.
#   LE_ERR_BUF_FULL         : 0x5400   _le_insert_bytes / _le_dispatch_
#                                      key insert path: _le_buf_len +
#                                      len would exceed LE_BUF_BYTES
#                                      (4096).
#   LE_ERR_BAD_PTR          : 0x5401   _le_insert_bytes with ptr == 0
#                                      AND len > 0; _le_get_buffer
#                                      with out == 0.
#   LE_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL    : 0x5402   _le_get_buffer with out_cap <
#                                      _le_buf_len.
#   LE_ERR_BAD_KEY          : 0x5403   _le_dispatch_key with an
#                                      unbound control byte (0x00,
#                                      0x03, 0x07..0x0A, 0x0E..0x11,
#                                      0x13..0x14, 0x16, 0x18, 0x1A,
#                                      0x1C..0x1F, 0x7F).
#   LE_ERR_UTF8             : 0x5404   _le_dispatch_key: C-b / C-f /
#                                      C-d: _gi_next_cluster or
#                                      _gi_prev_cluster returned
#                                      GI_ERR_INVALID_UTF8 (0x3800)
#                                      at the current cursor.
#   LE_ERR_BAD_LEN          : 0x5405   _le_insert_bytes with len == 0.
#   LE_META_PENDING         : 0x5406   _le_dispatch_key seen ESC
#                                      (0x1B); _le_meta_pending latched
#                                      and next call interprets its
#                                      byte as a M-prefixed binding
#                                      (M-f / M-b / M-d).
#
# Slots 0x5407..0x54FF reserved for R229.M6.1+ follow-ups
# (LE_ERR_HISTORY_UNAVAILABLE on a C-r hit when History is empty,
# LE_ERR_MULTILINE_NESTED on nested-multiline wire-up at M6.1,
# LE_ERR_HIGHLIGHT_UNAVAILABLE on a syntax-highlighting hook miss
# at M6.2, etc.).
#
# The 0x5400..0x54FF carve-out skips the 0x5200..0x53FF range
# (reserved for cross-wave parallel landings) and sits adjacent to
# the 0x5100..0x51FF R229.M5 History band -- a downstream compound-
# error dispatcher can discriminate "the history substrate faulted"
# from "the line editor faulted" via `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x51` vs
# `cmp r, 0x54`. Disjoint from every other parser-tier packed-
# status range in the paideia-os tree.
#
# Grep confirmation at R229.M6 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x540[0-6]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
#        design/shell/line-editor-emacs.md + line_editor.pdx. The
#        `0x5401` BIOS int-15 call constants and `0x5405` xHCI
#        offsets scattered across the x86 real-mode / xhci drivers
#        are hardware constants, not status codes; the grep uses
#        `0x540[0-6]\b` and will not alias these when the context
#        prefix (`cmp r,` / `mov rax,` / return value) is factored
#        in by a human reviewer.

# ---- R229.M6 catalogue paragraph (line_editor) -----------------------
#
#   R229.M6 LineEditor (line_editor.pdx, landed): per-shell-session
#      emacs-mode line editor substrate implementing all 13 bindings
#      from design/terminal/editor-bindings.md §1. 4096-byte line
#      buffer (`_le_buf`) + 1024-byte kill buffer (`_le_kill_buf`)
#      + six u64 scalars (`_le_buf_len`, `_le_cursor_byte`,
#      `_le_cursor_cluster`, `_le_kill_len`, `_le_meta_pending`,
#      `_le_last_search_turn`) in a singleton `.bss`. Five public
#      entry points:
#      `_le_init()` (pure-leaf defensive scrub of the six u64
#        scalars via `lea r11, [rip + sym]; mov [r11], rax`
#        pattern; byte pools rely on `.bss` zero-init to satisfy
#        the "read only [0, *_len)" invariant),
#      `_le_reset()` (pure-leaf clear of buf_len + cursor_byte +
#        cursor_cluster + meta_pending + last_search_turn after a
#        RET-commit; preserves kill_buf + kill_len across lines
#        per emacs/readline convention so yank works into a fresh
#        prompt),
#      `_le_dispatch_key(key_u32)` (6-push + sub rsp 8 pad giving
#        rsp%16==0 at every nested CALL; dispatches one keystroke
#        onto the binding table via a byte-value switch; control
#        bytes 0x01..0x19 route to per-binding handlers; 0x1B
#        latches `_le_meta_pending := 1` and returns LE_META_PENDING;
#        meta-pending-at-entry routes to M-f / M-b / M-d and
#        clears the latch; printable 0x20..0x7E and non-ASCII
#        0x80..0xFF insert one byte at cursor; unbound control
#        bytes return LE_ERR_BAD_KEY. Nested CALLs: _gi_next_
#        cluster (R221.M2) + _gi_prev_cluster (R221.M2) for cluster-
#        aware cursor advance on C-b / C-f / C-d; _h_search_
#        substring (R229.M5) + _h_get_turn (R229.M5) for C-r
#        reverse-i-search. Each nested CALL target is a pure-leaf
#        sibling module that does not touch LineEditor's `.bss`),
#      `_le_insert_bytes(ptr, len)` (pure-leaf; guards len > 0
#        AND ptr != 0 AND buf_len + len <= 4096; shift-right walk-
#        DOWN of buf[cursor..buf_len) to buf[cursor+len..] to
#        avoid aliasing; byte-copy from ptr; bump buf_len +=
#        len, cursor_byte += len),
#      `_le_get_buffer(out, out_cap)` (pure-leaf copy of
#        buf[0..buf_len) to out; packed `(buf_len << 32) | LE_OK`
#        on success; LE_ERR_BAD_PTR / LE_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL on
#        guards).
#      Cursor discipline: `_le_cursor_byte` is authoritative;
#      `_le_cursor_cluster` is a renderer hint bumped by 1 only on
#      ASCII (< 0x80) insert bytes and decremented on C-b. The
#      width-pass column renderer (R221.M3) may re-derive the hint
#      via `_wp_cols(buf, cursor_byte)` when it detects staleness.
#      C-r flow: when buf_len is in [1..64], feeds buf as the needle
#      to _h_search_substring with start_turn=0 (newest-first); on
#      a hit, calls _h_get_turn into a scratch region at the tail
#      of _le_kill_buf (bytes 896..1024, reclaimed by first setting
#      _le_kill_len := 0), extracts cmd_inline[0..64) stopping at
#      the first zero byte, overwrites _le_buf with the extracted
#      bytes, snaps cursor_byte and buf_len to the extract length,
#      resets cursor_cluster to 0. On miss or bad turn: silent OK.
#      Error band 0x5400..0x54FF (7 slots used; 0x5407..0x54FF
#      reserved for R229.M6.1+ follow-ups).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. LineEditor stores raw UTF-8 bytes between
# NFC decode (R221.M1) at tokenise-time and never calls into the
# parser surface at edit time; no KIND surface is touched by this
# module at raw M6. R229.M6.1 pdxfs CoW persistence hook (if
# landed) would introduce a KIND_PDXFS reach for per-session line
# snapshotting, but raw M6 keeps the substrate pure in-process.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R229.M6 (line_editor.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and TWO intra-library cross-module
# linker dependencies: R221.M2 GraphemeIter (via `_gi_next_cluster`
# and `_gi_prev_cluster`) for cluster-aware cursor advance on C-b /
# C-f / C-d, and R229.M5 History (via `_h_search_substring` and
# `_h_get_turn`) for C-r reverse-i-search. Both intra-library deps
# resolve through the same `--extra-obj-dir tools/user/libpdx-pds-
# parser/build-out` seam R229.M3 TypeEnv already uses for its
# `_hm_instantiate` cross-module call. LineEditor's `.bss` is
# disjoint from every other satellite module's `.bss` (parser-
# family: `_cmd_sig_*`, `_fi_*`, `_afs_*`, `_sch_sig_*`; row-
# family: `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`, `_er_*`, `_cr_*`; session-family:
# `_se_env_pool`, `_te_scheme_pool`; lexer-family: `_cl_stack`,
# `_cl_depth`, `_cl_last_kind`; scope-family: `_ls_frame_pool`,
# `_ls_frame_next`; registry-family: `_cmdreg_entries`, `_cmdreg_*`;
# job-family: `_jc_jobs`, `_jc_job_count`, `_jc_fg_tid`; history-
# family: `_h_entries`, `_h_entry_count`, `_h_cursor`).

# ---- Error band 0x5200..0x52FF (R223.M5, wire_sink) ------------------
#
# Wire-sink packed-status range for the R223 pipeline-lang wave's
# cross-boundary serialization milestone (Cap'n Proto-ish wire frame
# for process- and host-boundary record transit). Eight slots used
# at R223.M5:
#
#   WS_OK                     : 0        success on every entry point.
#   WS_ERR_BAD_RECORD         : 0x5200   `_ws_serialize_record`:
#                                        record_ptr == 0.
#   WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO_LARGE   : 0x5201   serialize: record_len > 1024;
#                                        deserialize: wire's record_len
#                                        > 1024.
#   WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL      : 0x5202   serialize: out == 0 or out_cap
#                                        < 32 + ceil(record_len/8)*8;
#                                        deserialize: out_cap <
#                                        record_len.
#   WS_ERR_BAD_FRAME          : 0x5203   `_ws_deserialize_record`:
#                                        wire == 0, wire_len < 32,
#                                        out == 0, or wire_len < 32 +
#                                        data_words_expected * 8.
#   WS_ERR_BAD_MAGIC          : 0x5204   `_ws_deserialize_record`:
#                                        magic ('WSNK' = 0x4B4E5357)
#                                        mismatch at [wire + 0].
#   WS_ERR_BAD_VERSION        : 0x5205   `_ws_deserialize_record`:
#                                        version lo byte != 1 or hi
#                                        byte != 0 at [wire + 4..+6].
#   WS_ERR_BAD_SEG_HEADER     : 0x5206   `_ws_deserialize_record`:
#                                        seg_word_count u32 at [wire +
#                                        8] out of range [1..129] or
#                                        inconsistent with record_len.
#   WS_ERR_BAD_STRUCT_PTR     : 0x5207   `_ws_deserialize_record`:
#                                        struct_pointer u64 at [wire +
#                                        24] has nonzero kind (bits
#                                        0..1), nonzero offset (bits
#                                        2..31), nonzero ptr_words
#                                        (bits 48..63), or data_words
#                                        (bits 32..47) != data_words
#                                        _expected.
#
# Slots 0x5208..0x52FF reserved for R223.M5.1+ follow-ups
# (far-pointer / multi-segment framing errors on `flags` bit 0;
# nested-record / per-field struct pointer errors from the SchemasSig
# folder; cap-handle serialization errors on `flags` bit 1;
# schema_tag collision errors from the registry dedup pass).
#
# The 0x5200..0x52FF carve-out skips the 0x5100..0x51FF reservation
# (R229.M5 History, landed), the 0x5000..0x50FF carve-out (R224.M6
# PipelineLambda, landed), the 0x4F00..0x4FFF carve-out (R222.M8
# DescribeSig, landed), and the 0x5400..0x54FF carve-out (R229.M6
# LineEditor, landed Wave 127). Wire-sink's band is disjoint from
# every other parser-tier packed-status range in the paideia-os tree
# so a downstream compound-error dispatcher can discriminate "the
# wire-sink substrate faulted" from every other satellite's failure
# modes by a single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x52` idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R223.M5 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x520[0-7]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
#        design/shell/wire-format-capnproto.md + wire_sink.pdx.

# ---- R223.M5 catalogue paragraph (wire_sink) -------------------------
#
#   R223.M5 WireSink (wire_sink.pdx, landed): parser-tier Cap'n
#      Proto-ish wire framer that serializes typed shell records to
#      a byte stream for process- or host-boundary transit, and
#      deserializes the inverse. The SERIALIZATION LAYER half of the
#      cross-boundary pipeline `find . @remote | sort by size`
#      (paideia-os#2670); the TRANSPORT half (TCP + ChaCha20-Poly1305)
#      lives in `svc/remote_record_wire.pdx` (R137.M4). 32-byte
#      prelude + variable payload (record bytes zero-padded to 8-byte
#      boundary; record_len capped at WS_RECORD_MAX = 1024 at raw
#      M5; frame max = 1056 bytes). Prelude: magic 'WSNK'
#      (0x4B4E5357 LE) u32 at [+0], version u16 (= 1) at [+4],
#      flags u16 (= 0, reserved for M5.1+) at [+6], brief's segment
#      header (seg_word_count u32 at [+8] = 1 + data_words,
#      segment_id u32 at [+12] = schema_tag) at [+8..+16], record_len
#      u32 at [+16] (needed for byte-exact round-trip since the
#      Cap'n Proto struct pointer carries only word-granularity),
#      reserved u32 at [+20], Cap'n Proto struct pointer tag u64 at
#      [+24] (bits 0..1 = STRUCT kind 0, bits 2..31 = offset-in-
#      words 0, bits 32..47 = data_words = ceil(record_len/8),
#      bits 48..63 = ptr_words 0), payload at [+32..]. Four public
#      entry points, ALL PURE LEAVES:
#      `_ws_init()` -- bare `ret`; M5 holds NO .bss state (every
#        wire frame is caller-owned on both the serialize and
#        deserialize paths; the schema hash is a pure byte walk
#        over a caller-supplied name buffer). The entry point
#        exists for parallelism with the parser-family init-hook
#        convention (_ds_init R222.M8, _dm_init R222.M6, _cmdreg_init
#        R222.M5) so a later landing that adds .bss (per-connection
#        sequence counters, schema_tag intern table, per-session
#        serialization-fault counter) does not break the API.
#      `_ws_serialize_record(record_ptr, record_len, schema_tag, out,
#                            out_cap)` --
#        validates record_ptr != 0, record_len <= WS_RECORD_MAX
#        (1024), out != 0, out_cap >= 32 + ceil(record_len/8)*8;
#        zero-fills the required bytes via a u64 stepping loop
#        (step by 8); stamps magic 'WSNK' u32 at [+0], version u16
#        lo byte 1 at [+4] (hi byte zero via zero-fill), flags u16
#        at [+6..+8] (zero via zero-fill), seg_word_count u32 at
#        [+8] = 1 + data_words, segment_id u32 at [+12] =
#        schema_tag (low 32 of rdx), record_len u32 at [+16] = rsi
#        (low 32), reserved u32 at [+20..+24] (zero via zero-fill),
#        struct_pointer u64 at [+24] with data_words in bits 32..47
#        (shl rax, 32 pack); byte-copies record_len bytes from
#        record_ptr to [out + 32] via a byte-by-byte loop. Returns
#        packed_u64 = (frame_bytes << 32) | WS_OK on success, or
#        (0 << 32) | WS_ERR_BAD_RECORD (0x5200) / WS_ERR_RECORD_TOO
#        _LARGE (0x5201) / WS_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL (0x5202).
#      `_ws_deserialize_record(wire, wire_len, out, out_cap)` --
#        validates wire != 0, wire_len >= 32, out != 0; reads magic
#        u32 at [+0] (cmp with 0x4B4E5357); reads version lo/hi
#        bytes at [+4..+6] (lo == 1, hi == 0); reads seg_word_count
#        u32 at [+8] (range [1..129]); reads record_len u32 at
#        [+16] (<= 1024); computes data_words_expected =
#        ceil(record_len / 8); validates seg_word_count ==
#        1 + data_words_expected; reads struct_pointer u64 at [+24]
#        and validates its three sub-fields (low 32 == 0, bits
#        48..63 == 0, bits 32..47 == data_words_expected);
#        validates wire_len >= 32 + data_words_expected * 8;
#        validates out_cap >= record_len; byte-copies record_len
#        bytes from [wire + 32] to out. Returns packed_u64 =
#        (record_len << 32) | WS_OK on success, or (0 << 32) |
#        WS_ERR_* status codes 0x5201..0x5207.
#      `_ws_schema_hash(name_ptr, name_len)` -- FNV-1a 64 of the
#        schema name bytes, byte-for-byte identical to _cmd_sig_
#        fnv1a64 (R222.M2), _fi_fnv1a64 (R222.M3), _cmdreg_fnv1a64
#        (R222.M5), and the inlined FNV in _h_append (R229.M5).
#        Offset basis 0xCBF29CE484222325; prime 0x100000001B3.
#        Returns the full u64 hash; the caller truncates to u32 to
#        derive a schema_tag.
#      All four entry points are PURE LEAVES (zero callee-save
#      pushes, zero stack frame, zero nested CALL) so no alignment
#      pad is needed; r11 is used freely as a staging register
#      because no nested CALL exists. The two-register addressing
#      form `mov_b r11, [r8 + r10]` used inside `_ws_schema_hash` is
#      the FNV-family precedent (byte-for-byte match to
#      _cmd_sig_fnv1a64 line 446). Record stride 32-byte prelude
#      addressed via plain `cmp r, 32` and `add r, 32` immediates
#      (imm8-safe); frame zero-fill loop steps by 8 via `shl rN, 3`
#      (data_words * 8) and plain `add r11, 8`; record-length round-
#      up uses `add r, 7; shr r, 3; shl r, 3` (never 2-op `imul r,
#      imm`, never `and r, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8` imm64). No KIND; no
#      external cross-library dependency; no intra-library cross-
#      module call -- WireSink is a self-contained wire-format
#      framer / unframer / schema-hasher over caller-supplied byte
#      buffers. The transport half (`svc/remote_record_wire.pdx`
#      R137.M4) consumes/produces the frames this module emits/
#      accepts; the two halves live in disjoint linker namespaces
#      (kernel-side OSARCH half vs user-space satellite half) and
#      share no state.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. WireSink emits a flat 32-byte-header plus
# variable-payload byte image over caller-supplied buffers; no KIND
# surface is touched by this module. The transport layer below
# (R137.M4 remote_record_wire) owns the KIND_REMOTE_SHELL seam when
# that cap-invoke surface lands at R137.M5.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R223.M5 (wire_sink.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module
# linker dependency. All four public entry points (`_ws_init`,
# `_ws_serialize_record`, `_ws_deserialize_record`,
# `_ws_schema_hash`) touch ONLY caller-supplied pointer args; the
# module carries no `.bss` state at raw M5. The FNV offset basis
# (0xCBF29CE484222325) and prime (0x100000001B3) are replicated as
# local module constants so the hash validates the input without a
# cross-module linker dep on command_sig.pdx -- the same disjoint-
# leaf precedent as the parser-family's `_cmd_sig_fnv1a64` /
# `_fi_fnv1a64` / `_cmdreg_fnv1a64` quartet. All four public entry
# points can be linked standalone against a bare parser-tier object
# graph; no new `--extra-obj-dir` is introduced. R223.M5.1+ (per-
# field struct pointers via SchemasSig fold) will introduce a one-
# way intra-library STRUCTURAL dependency on R222.M1 SchemasSig
# but STILL no linker-visible dependency: the SchemasSig body
# buffer is handed in as an opaque caller-managed pointer.

# ---- Error band 0x5600..0x56FF (R224.M7, linear_propagate) -----------
#
# Linear/affine propagation packed-status range for the R224 lambda-
# lang wave's SEVENTH milestone (Walker 2005 substructural-lattice
# propagation across lambda closure capture). Nine slots used at
# R224.M7:
#
#   LP_OK                     : 0        operation succeeded.
#   LP_ERR_BAD_KIND           : 0x5600   `_lp_check_use` / `_lp_meet`
#                                        / `_lp_join` /
#                                        `_lp_classify_lambda`: input
#                                        kind > LP_KIND_LINEAR (3). On
#                                        `_lp_meet` / `_lp_join` this
#                                        is NOT returned as a status
#                                        -- the primitives return
#                                        LP_UNKNOWN_KIND (0xFF) in rax
#                                        directly to keep the lattice
#                                        operations single-instruction.
#                                        On `_lp_check_use` and the
#                                        per-binding iteration of
#                                        `_lp_classify_lambda`, this
#                                        code IS returned.
#   LP_ERR_LINEAR_NOT_USED    : 0x5601   `_lp_check_use` on a LINEAR
#                                        binding whose use_count == 0.
#   LP_ERR_LINEAR_MULTI_USE   : 0x5602   `_lp_check_use` on a LINEAR
#                                        binding whose use_count > 1.
#   LP_ERR_AFFINE_MULTI_USE   : 0x5603   `_lp_check_use` on an AFFINE
#                                        binding whose use_count > 1.
#   LP_ERR_RELEVANT_NOT_USED  : 0x5604   `_lp_check_use` on a RELEVANT
#                                        binding whose use_count == 0.
#   LP_ERR_BAD_MAGIC          : 0x5605   `_lp_classify_lambda`:
#                                        closure_env_ref's u32 at [+0]
#                                        != 'CCAP' (0x50414343).
#   LP_ERR_NULL_PTR           : 0x5606   `_lp_classify_lambda`:
#                                        closure_env_ref != 0 AND
#                                        n_bindings > 0 AND
#                                        env_kinds_ref == 0.
#   LP_ERR_BAD_INDEX          : 0x5607   `_lp_classify_lambda`:
#                                        env_kinds_len < n_bindings.
#   LP_ERR_BAD_N_BINDINGS     : 0x5608   `_lp_classify_lambda`:
#                                        n_bindings in the
#                                        ClosureCapture header exceeds
#                                        LP_MAX_BINDINGS (16; mirrors
#                                        R224.M2 CC_MAX_BINDINGS).
#
# Slots 0x5609..0x56FF reserved for R224.M7.1+ follow-ups (LambdaHeader
# kind-override mismatch at M7.1; let-rec recursive self-capture
# fixpoint failure at M7.4; PipelineLambda tail-byte-collision at
# M7.5; CapRow/LinearPropagate composition mismatch at M7.6; E0840_*
# diagnostic-collector surface at M7.3).
#
# The 0x5600..0x56FF carve-out skips the 0x5500..0x55FF reservation
# (R223.M7 parallel-arm pipeline-fuzz-corpus, reserved for a sibling
# landing), the 0x5400..0x54FF carve-out (R229.M6 LineEditor, landed),
# the 0x5300..0x53FF carve-out (R223.M6 HostnameAnnot, landed), the
# 0x5200..0x52FF carve-out (R223.M5 WireSink, landed), the
# 0x5100..0x51FF carve-out (R229.M5 History, landed), the
# 0x5000..0x50FF carve-out (R224.M6 PipelineLambda, landed), and the
# 0x4F00..0x4FFF carve-out (R222.M8 DescribeSig, landed). The
# LinearPropagate band is disjoint from every other parser-tier
# packed-status range in the paideia-os tree so a downstream compound-
# error dispatcher can discriminate "the substructural-lattice
# propagator faulted" from every other satellite's failure modes by a
# single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x56` idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R224.M7 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x56[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> no prior hits (band is virgin).
#   grep -rE '0x560[0-8]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
#        design/shell/linear-propagation.md + linear_propagate.pdx.

# libpdx-pds-parser.R224.M7 (linear_propagate.pdx) has NO external
# cross-library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module
# linker dependency. All five public entry points (`_lp_init`,
# `_lp_meet`, `_lp_join`, `_lp_check_use`, `_lp_classify_lambda`)
# touch ONLY caller-supplied pointer args + register-passed scalars;
# the module carries no `.bss` state at raw M7. The ClosureCapture
# record is handed in as an opaque caller-managed pointer whose
# header layout (magic 'CCAP' u32 at [+0]; n_bindings u16 at [+4]) is
# replicated as local module constants (LP_CC_HEADER_MAGIC,
# LP_CC_OFF_MAGIC, LP_CC_OFF_N_BINDINGS, LP_MAX_BINDINGS) so the
# header walk validates the input without a cross-module linker dep
# on closure_capture.pdx -- the parallel-array shape of
# `env_kinds_ref` means the per-binding Binding stride is never
# dereferenced. All five public entry points can be linked standalone
# against a bare parser-tier object graph; no new `--extra-obj-dir`
# is introduced. R224.M7.5 (PipelineLambda tail-byte persistence)
# will introduce a one-way intra-library STRUCTURAL dependency on
# R224.M6 PipelineLambda but STILL no linker-visible dependency: the
# PipelineLambda record is handed in as an opaque caller-managed
# pointer. The forward path landings (M7.1 LambdaHeader kind-override;
# M7.2 LetScope wire-up; M7.3 diagnostic-collector; M7.4 let-rec
# self-edge filter; M7.5 PipelineLambda tail-byte; M7.6 CapRow
# composition) remain opaque-pointer by design and introduce NO new
# linker-visible dependency.

# ---- Error band 0x5700..0x57FF (R229.M7, vi_mode) --------------------
#
# Vi-mode packed-status range for the R229 REPL-evaluator wave's
# SEVENTH milestone (vi-mode state machine over the R229.M6
# LineEditor substrate). Five slots used at R229.M7:
#
#   VI_OK             : 0        operation succeeded.
#   VI_ERR_BAD_KEY    : 0x5700   `_vi_dispatch_key`: unbound byte in
#                                the active mode (unbound control
#                                byte in INSERT, unknown byte in
#                                NORMAL after the motion/op/mode-
#                                entry/paste byte table did not
#                                match).
#   VI_ERR_BUF_FULL   : 0x5701   `_vi_dispatch_key`: `p`/`P` paste
#                                would push `_le_buf_len` past
#                                LE_BUF_BYTES (4096). INSERT-mode
#                                single-byte insert at `buf_len ==
#                                4096` returns the same code.
#   VI_ERR_BAD_MODE   : 0x5702   `_vi_dispatch_key`: defensive --
#                                `_vi_mode` scalar is outside 0..3
#                                (chaos-restart / memory corruption
#                                indicator; cold-boot `.bss` zero-
#                                init guarantees mode == NORMAL = 0).
#   VI_PENDING_OP     : 0x5703   `_vi_dispatch_key`: operator byte
#                                (`d`/`c`/`y`) latched into
#                                `_vi_pending_op`; next byte is
#                                interpreted as the motion half of
#                                the operator-motion pair.
#   VI_PENDING_G      : 0x5704   `_vi_dispatch_key`: first `g`
#                                latched into `_vi_pending_g`; next
#                                byte, if `g`, executes `gg`
#                                (cursor := 0); else clears the
#                                latch and dispatches normally.
#
# Slots 0x5705..0x57FF reserved for R229.M7.1+ follow-ups
# (VI_ERR_SELECTION_EMPTY on an operator over an empty VISUAL span;
# VI_ERR_MULTILINE on `o`/`O` when the buffer contains an LF, pre-
# M7.1; VI_ERR_UNKNOWN_REGISTER on `"xy` with unknown register `x`
# at M7.4; VI_ERR_UNKNOWN_MARK on `` `a `` with no `a` at M7.5;
# VI_ERR_UNKNOWN_TEXTOBJ on `daX` with unknown `X` at M7.6;
# VI_ERR_COMMAND_SYNTAX on malformed `:...` at M7.7; VI_ERR_
# UNKNOWN_MACRO on `@a` with no recording at M7.8).
#
# The 0x5700..0x57FF carve-out skips the 0x5500..0x55FF reservation
# (R223.M7 parallel-arm pipeline-fuzz-corpus, reserved for a sibling
# landing) and the 0x5600..0x56FF carve-out (R224.M7 LinearPropagate,
# landed in this Wave 128) and sits adjacent to the 0x5400..0x54FF
# R229.M6 LineEditor band -- a downstream compound-error dispatcher
# can discriminate "the emacs-mode editor faulted" from "the vi-mode
# state machine faulted" via `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x54` vs
# `cmp r, 0x57`. ViMode's band is disjoint from every other parser-
# tier packed-status range in the paideia-os tree so a downstream
# compound-error dispatcher can discriminate "the vi state machine
# faulted" from every other satellite's failure modes by a single
# `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x57` idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R229.M7 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x57[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> no prior hits (band is virgin).
#   grep -rE '0x570[0-4]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
#        design/shell/line-editor-vi.md + vi_mode.pdx.

# libpdx-pds-parser.R229.M7 (vi_mode.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and SIX intra-library cross-module
# linker-visible dependencies -- all into R229.M6 LineEditor's `.bss`
# singletons (`_le_buf`, `_le_buf_len`, `_le_cursor_byte`,
# `_le_cursor_cluster`, `_le_kill_buf`, `_le_kill_len`). ViMode
# treats these symbols as the authoritative line + kill + cursor
# state; the two state machines (R229.M6 emacs + R229.M7 vi) share
# the same singleton buffer so a yank in vi lands in the register
# `C-y` pulls from in emacs, and vice versa. The six references
# resolve through the same `--extra-obj-dir tools/user/libpdx-pds-
# parser/build-out` seam R229.M3 TypeEnv already uses for its
# `_hm_instantiate` cross-module call and R229.M6 LineEditor uses
# for its `_gi_next_cluster` / `_h_search_substring` cross-module
# calls. ViMode carries NO cross-module linker-visible FUNCTION
# calls -- all buffer manipulation (INSERT-mode shift-right, paste
# shift-right, delete-span shift-left, yank byte-copy) is INLINED
# at raw M7 to keep `_vi_dispatch_key` a PURE LEAF (zero nested
# CALL; zero stack pad). ViMode's `.bss` is disjoint from every
# other satellite module's `.bss` (parser-family: `_cmd_sig_*`,
# `_fi_*`, `_afs_*`, `_sch_sig_*`; row-family: `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`,
# `_er_*`, `_cr_*`; session-family: `_se_env_pool`,
# `_te_scheme_pool`; lexer-family: `_cl_stack`, `_cl_depth`,
# `_cl_last_kind`; scope-family: `_ls_frame_pool`, `_ls_frame_next`;
# registry-family: `_cmdreg_entries`, `_cmdreg_*`; job-family:
# `_jc_jobs`, `_jc_job_count`, `_jc_fg_tid`; history-family:
# `_h_entries`, `_h_entry_count`, `_h_cursor`; line-editor-family:
# `_le_buf`, `_le_buf_len`, `_le_cursor_*`, `_le_kill_*`,
# `_le_meta_pending`, `_le_last_search_turn`). All four public entry
# points can be linked standalone against a bare parser-tier object
# graph PROVIDED R229.M6 LineEditor is also linked; no new
# `--extra-obj-dir` is introduced. The forward path landings (M7.1
# multi-line; M7.2 TR#29 motion; M7.3 count prefixes; M7.4 named
# registers; M7.5 marks; M7.6 text objects; M7.7 COMMAND-mode `:`
# parser; M7.8 macros) remain opaque-pointer by design and
# introduce NO new linker-visible dependency.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. ViMode stores modal scalars and reads/
# writes the LineEditor .bss directly for its edit primitives; no
# KIND surface is touched by this module at raw M7. R229.M7.7
# COMMAND-mode `:w` / `:q` builtins (if landed) would introduce a
# KIND_SHELL_SESSION reach for the `:w` buffer-write hook, but raw
# M7 keeps the substrate pure in-process.

# ---- Error band 0x5500..0x55FF (R223.M7, session_type) ---------------
#
# Session-type packed-status range for the R223 pipeline-lang wave's
# session-typed-edge enforcement milestone. Eight slots used at R223.M7:
#
#   ST_OK                     : 0        success on every entry point.
#   ST_ERR_BAD_SCHEMA         : 0x5500   `_st_build_edge`: producer_name
#                                        _ptr == 0 OR consumer_name_ptr
#                                        == 0.
#   ST_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL      : 0x5501   `_st_build_edge`: out == 0 or
#                                        out_cap < 64 (edge pair). Also
#                                        `_st_render`: out == 0 or
#                                        out_cap < 1 + schema_len + 5.
#   ST_ERR_BAD_RECORD         : 0x5502   `_st_check_duality`: edge_a == 0
#                                        or edge_b == 0; `_st_render`:
#                                        record == 0.
#   ST_ERR_SCHEMA_MISMATCH    : 0x5510   `_st_check_duality` E0710:
#                                        schema_tag_hash fields differ
#                                        between the two halves.
#   ST_ERR_DIRECTION_MISMATCH : 0x5511   `_st_check_duality` E0711: both
#                                        halves carry the same direction
#                                        byte (both PRODUCE or both
#                                        CONSUME) -- not a dual pair.
#   ST_ERR_UNCLOSED_SESSION   : 0x5512   `_st_check_duality` E0712: one
#                                        or both halves have ST_FLAG_
#                                        CLOSED (bit 0) clear.
#   ST_ERR_PROTOCOL_DRIFT     : 0x5513   `_st_check_duality` / `_st_
#                                        render` E0713: magic ('SESS'
#                                        = 0x53534553) mismatch on
#                                        either half OR direction byte
#                                        > 1 on either half.
#
# Slots 0x5503..0x550F reserved for M7.1+ build / validate faults
# (far-ptr chain errors on `flags` bit 1, variant-tag errors in the
# reserved u16, intern-table collisions when the schema-tag intern
# table lands). Slots 0x5514..0x55FF reserved for M7.2+ branch and
# linearity diagnostics (external choice `&{A, B}` and internal choice
# `+{A, B}` type faults, affine use-count violations when R223.M7.4
# wires R224.M7 LinearPropagate into the duality check).
#
# The 0x5500..0x55FF carve-out skips the 0x5400..0x54FF reservation
# (R229.M6 LineEditor, landed), 0x5300..0x53FF (R223.M6 HostnameAnnot,
# landed), 0x5200..0x52FF (R223.M5 WireSink, landed), 0x5100..0x51FF
# (R229.M5 History, landed), 0x5000..0x50FF (R224.M6 PipelineLambda,
# landed), and 0x4F00..0x4FFF (R222.M8 DescribeSig, landed). The
# parallel Wave 128 arms sit at 0x5600..0x56FF (R224.M7 LinearPropagate,
# landed Wave 128) and 0x5700..0x57FF (R229.M7 ViMode, landed Wave 128);
# the three arms' bands are mutually disjoint so a downstream
# compound-error dispatcher can discriminate "the session-type
# substrate faulted" from every other satellite's failure modes by a
# single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x55` idiom. The band displaces the prior
# soft reservation "deferred R229.M6.5 Vi-mode state machine" (a
# forward-path note in LineEditor rather than a landing commitment);
# the actual Vi-mode landing has relocated to 0x5700..0x57FF as the
# R229.M7 arm.
#
# Grep confirmation at R223.M7 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x55(0[0-2]|1[0-3])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
#        design/shell/session-typed-edges.md + session_type.pdx.

# ---- R223.M7 catalogue paragraph (session_type) ----------------------
#
#   R223.M7 SessionType (session_type.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      session-typed edge protocol enforcement for pipeline-lang stage
#      graphs. Materialises every pipeline `|` boundary as a dual pair
#      of SessionType records (one PRODUCE, one CONSUME) and reports
#      the E0710 family of diagnostics on duality violations per the
#      acceptance adversarial test in paideia-os#2672 (ten mismatched
#      pipelines all fail type-check). The session-type lattice has
#      two inhabitants per edge-half at raw M7 (PRODUCE, CONSUME) with
#      one dual rule; M7.1+ layers in multi-step chains, branch types
#      (`&`, `+`), and linearity cross-wire. 32-byte record stride
#      (edge pair = 64 B): magic 'SESS' (0x53534553 LE) u32 at [+0],
#      direction u8 at [+4] (0=PRODUCE, 1=CONSUME), flags u8 at [+5]
#      (bit 0 = ST_FLAG_CLOSED, bit 1 = ST_FLAG_CHAIN reserved for
#      M7.1+, bits 2..7 reserved), reserved u16 at [+6], schema_tag_
#      hash u64 at [+8] (FNV-1a 64 of schema name, byte-for-byte
#      identical to the parser-family's seven prior FNV leaves), and
#      schema_name_inline [u8;16] at [+16] (ASCII identifier, zero-
#      padded). Four public entry points, ALL PURE LEAVES:
#      `_st_init()` -- bare `ret`; M7 holds NO .bss state (every
#        SessionType record is caller-owned on all three operational
#        paths; the hash operates as a pure byte walk over a caller-
#        supplied name buffer). The entry point exists for parallelism
#        with the parser-family init-hook convention (_ha_init R223.M6,
#        _ws_init R223.M5, _ds_init R222.M8, _dm_init R222.M6,
#        _cmdreg_init R222.M5) so a later landing that adds .bss
#        (per-session edge counters, a schema-tag intern table, a
#        per-session duality-fault counter) does not break the API.
#      `_st_build_edge(producer_name_ptr, consumer_name_ptr, out,
#                      out_cap)` --
#        validates producer_name_ptr != 0 (ST_ERR_BAD_SCHEMA on fail);
#        validates consumer_name_ptr != 0 (ST_ERR_BAD_SCHEMA on fail);
#        validates out != 0 and out_cap >= 64 (ST_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL on
#        fail). Zero-fills the 64-byte region via 8 u64 stores. Record
#        A emit at [out+0..+32): magic 'SESS' u32 at [+0]; direction
#        byte 0 (PRODUCE) at [+4] (explicit store for symmetry with
#        Record B even though zero-fill already placed zero there);
#        flags byte 1 (ST_FLAG_CLOSED) at [+5]; byte-copy producer
#        _name[0..16] to [+16..+32]; scan producer_name for first NUL
#        to get live length (0..16); FNV-1a 64 over the live prefix;
#        write hash u64 at [+8]. Record B emit at [out+32..+64):
#        mirrors Record A with magic at [+32], direction byte 1
#        (CONSUME) at [+36], flags byte 1 at [+37], consumer_name
#        inline at [+48..+64], hash at [+40]. Returns packed_u64 =
#        (64 << 32) | ST_OK on success, or (0 << 32) | ST_ERR_BAD
#        _SCHEMA (0x5500) / ST_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL (0x5501) on fail.
#      `_st_check_duality(edge_a, edge_b)` -- validates two Session
#        Type records form a dual pair. Validation ordering (most
#        specific error first): (1) edge_a != 0, edge_b != 0 (ST_ERR
#        _BAD_RECORD 0x5502). (2) magic at [+0] on both halves ==
#        0x53534553 (ST_ERR_PROTOCOL_DRIFT 0x5513 = E0713 on fail).
#        (3) direction byte at [+4] on both halves in {0, 1}
#        (ST_ERR_PROTOCOL_DRIFT on fail). (4) directions differ
#        (ST_ERR_DIRECTION_MISMATCH 0x5511 = E0711 on fail). (5) flags
#        byte at [+5] on both halves has bit 0 (ST_FLAG_CLOSED) set
#        (ST_ERR_UNCLOSED_SESSION 0x5512 = E0712 on fail). (6)
#        schema_tag_hash u64 at [+8] on both halves equal (ST_ERR
#        _SCHEMA_MISMATCH 0x5510 = E0710 on fail). Returns ST_OK (0)
#        on success. Pure leaf; zero callee-save pushes; no stack
#        frame.
#      `_st_render(record, out, out_cap)` -- emits a human-readable
#        surface form of a SessionType record into out[0..total_bytes).
#        Format: '!' (0x21) prefix for direction=0 or '?' (0x3F) for
#        direction=1; schema name byte-copied from [record+16..+32]
#        up to first NUL (0..16 bytes); ';done' (0x3B 0x64 0x6F 0x6E
#        0x65 = 5 B) suffix iff ST_FLAG_CLOSED set, else ';open'
#        (0x3B 0x6F 0x70 0x65 0x6E). Total bytes_written = 1 + schema
#        _len + 5 (6..22 bytes). Validates record != 0 (ST_ERR_BAD
#        _RECORD); magic at [+0] (ST_ERR_PROTOCOL_DRIFT); direction
#        byte at [+4] in {0, 1} (ST_ERR_PROTOCOL_DRIFT); out != 0 and
#        out_cap >= total_bytes (ST_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL). Returns
#        packed_u64 = (total_bytes << 32) | ST_OK on success.
#      All four entry points are PURE LEAVES (zero nested CALL)
#      so r11 is used freely as the three-term address-precompute
#      scratch. `_st_init` has zero pushes; `_st_check_duality` has
#      zero pushes; `_st_build_edge` pushes rbx (= producer_name
#      base, held across the two record emits), rbp (= consumer_name
#      base, same), r12 (= out base, held unchanged across all stores),
#      r13 (reserved for an M7.1+ chain-pointer carry; XOR'd to zero
#      at M7); `_st_render` pushes rbx (= record base), r12 (= out
#      base), r13 (= schema_len across the capacity check and the
#      copy loops). All byte accesses use the two-register addressing
#      form `mov_b rN, [base + idx]` where idx is the loop counter;
#      three-term address computations [base + off + idx] are pre-
#      computed into r11 or r9 via `mov; add; add` sequences to
#      sidestep the paideia-as encoder gap. All cmp immediates fit
#      imm32 with bit 31 clear so direct cmp-imm32 is sign-safe; the
#      FNV constants (0xCBF29CE484222325 offset basis and 0x100000001B3
#      prime) use the standard REX.W + MOV encoding (10-byte MOVABS),
#      byte-for-byte identical to the seven prior parser-family FNV
#      leaves. The CLOSED-bit mask uses imm8 `and rax, 1` (no imm64).
#      FNV multiply uses the one-op `mul rcx` form (no 2-op `imul r,
#      imm` -- that's the pdx-encoder-pitfall shape). Labels prefixed
#      `st_` (grep-confirmed unique against every landed parser-tier
#      prefix). No KIND surface; no external cross-library linker
#      dependency; no intra-library cross-module linker dependency
#      -- SessionType is a self-contained edge builder / dual checker
#      / renderer over caller-supplied byte buffers.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. SessionType emits a flat 32-byte-stride record
# over caller-supplied buffers; no KIND surface is touched by this
# module. R223.M7.1+ (multi-step protocol chains) will introduce a
# one-way intra-library STRUCTURAL dependency on R222.M1 SchemasSig
# (per-field type codes for sub-protocol records) but STILL no linker-
# visible dependency because the SchemasSig body buffer is handed in
# as an opaque caller-managed pointer. R223.M7.5+ (combined quintuple
# acceptance pass) will wire R225.M4 EffectUnify and R224.M4 CapRow
# into the duality check behind the same opaque-pointer discipline.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R223.M7 (session_type.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module linker
# dependency. All four public entry points (`_st_init`,
# `_st_build_edge`, `_st_check_duality`, `_st_render`) touch ONLY
# caller-supplied pointer args; the module carries no `.bss` state at
# raw M7. The FNV offset basis (0xCBF29CE484222325) and prime
# (0x100000001B3) are replicated as local module constants so the hash
# validates the input without a cross-module linker dep on
# command_sig.pdx or wire_sink.pdx -- the same disjoint-leaf precedent
# as the parser-family's seven prior FNV leaves (`_cmd_sig_fnv1a64`,
# `_fi_fnv1a64`, `_cmdreg_fnv1a64`, `_ws_schema_hash`, inlined in
# `_ha_parse` and `_ha_check` and `_h_append`). All four public entry
# points can be linked standalone against a bare parser-tier object
# graph; no new `--extra-obj-dir` is introduced.

# ---- Error band 0x5A00..0x5AFF (R229.M8, multi_line) -----------------
#
# Multi-line packed-status range for the R229 REPL-evaluator wave's
# EIGHTH milestone (incomplete-expression continuation detector). Three
# slots used at R229.M8:
#
#   ML_OK                 : 0        operation succeeded.
#   ML_ERR_BAD_PTR        : 0x5A00   `_ml_continuation_prompt`: out == 0.
#   ML_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL  : 0x5A01   `_ml_continuation_prompt`: out_cap <
#                                    the per-state byte-string length
#                                    (3 B on every INCOMPLETE_* state;
#                                    0 B on COMPLETE and therefore no
#                                    cap required there).
#   ML_ERR_BAD_STATE      : 0x5A02   `_ml_continuation_prompt`:
#                                    defensive -- the derived state is
#                                    outside 0..4 (chaos-restart /
#                                    memory corruption indicator;
#                                    `.bss` zero-init guarantees state
#                                    == COMPLETE at cold boot, and the
#                                    priority ladder cannot produce a
#                                    value outside 0..4 from the four
#                                    scalars). Reserved slot used by a
#                                    future landing's defensive check.
#
# `_ml_scan_line` and `_ml_state` return the state VALUE (0..4) in rax
# low byte, NOT a status code -- they do not fault. `_ml_init` and
# `_ml_reset` return `()` -- no status code.
#
# Slots 0x5A03..0x5AFF reserved for R229.M8.1+ follow-ups
# (ML_ERR_HERE_DOC_UNCLOSED at 0x5A03 for M8.1; ML_ERR_MAX_NEST_DEPTH
# at 0x5A04 for M8.2's 128-deep nesting ceiling; ML_ERR_NESTED_QUOTE
# at 0x5A05 for M8.3's single-quote parallel; a later M8.4+ slot for
# the CtxLexer-divergence klog-s1 fingerprint if that reports as a
# status rather than a klog line).
#
# The 0x5A00..0x5AFF carve-out skips the 0x5800..0x58FF band (reserved
# for the Wave 129 parallel-arm R225.M6 landing, held) and the
# 0x5900..0x59FF band (reserved for the Wave 129 parallel-arm R227.M3
# landing, held) and sits adjacent to the 0x5700..0x57FF R229.M7
# ViMode band -- a downstream compound-error dispatcher can
# discriminate "the vi state machine faulted" from "the multi-line
# continuation detector faulted" via `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x57` vs
# `cmp r, 0x5A`. MultiLine's band is disjoint from every other
# parser-tier packed-status range in the paideia-os tree so a
# downstream compound-error dispatcher can discriminate "the multi-
# line continuation detector faulted" from every other satellite's
# failure modes by a single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x5A` idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R229.M8 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x5A[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> no prior hits (band is virgin).
#   grep -rE '0x5A0[0-2]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
#        design/shell/multi-line-input.md + multi_line.pdx.

# libpdx-pds-parser.R229.M8 (multi_line.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module linker
# dependency. All five public entry points (`_ml_init`, `_ml_reset`,
# `_ml_scan_line`, `_ml_state`, `_ml_continuation_prompt`) touch ONLY
# this module's own four u64 `.bss` scalars (`_ml_brace_depth`,
# `_ml_paren_depth`, `_ml_in_string`, `_ml_backslash_pending`; 32 B
# total) and caller-supplied pointer args. MultiLine does NOT read
# the R229.M6 LineEditor `.bss` (`_le_buf`, `_le_buf_len`): the REPL
# dispatcher is responsible for snapshotting `_le_buf[0.._le_buf_len)`
# via `_le_get_buffer` into a scratch pool and handing (ptr, len) to
# `_ml_scan_line`. Keeping MultiLine free of a LineEditor linker
# reference means the module can be unit-tested against a synthetic
# input stream with no editor state. ALL FIVE public entry points are
# PURE LEAVES (zero callee-save pushes, zero stack frame, zero nested
# CALL); `_ml_scan_line`'s per-iteration state lives in SysV caller-
# save scratch (rcx = in_string, rdx = in_escape per-call local, r8 =
# loop index, r9 = brace_depth, r10 = paren_depth) with r11 used
# freely as `.bss` lea staging because no CALL exists to clobber it.
# MultiLine's `.bss` is disjoint from every other satellite module's
# `.bss` (parser-family: `_cmd_sig_*`, `_fi_*`, `_afs_*`,
# `_sch_sig_*`; row-family: `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`, `_er_*`, `_cr_*`;
# session-family: `_se_env_pool`, `_te_scheme_pool`; lexer-family:
# `_cl_stack`, `_cl_depth`, `_cl_last_kind`; scope-family:
# `_ls_frame_pool`, `_ls_frame_next`; registry-family:
# `_cmdreg_entries`, `_cmdreg_*`; job-family: `_jc_jobs`,
# `_jc_job_count`, `_jc_fg_tid`; history-family: `_h_entries`,
# `_h_entry_count`, `_h_cursor`; line-editor-family: `_le_buf`,
# `_le_buf_len`, `_le_cursor_*`, `_le_kill_*`, `_le_meta_pending`,
# `_le_last_search_turn`; vi-family: `_vi_mode`, `_vi_pending_op`,
# `_vi_pending_g`, `_vi_visual_anchor`). The forward-path landings
# (M8.1 heredoc; M8.2 safety ceiling; M8.3 single-quote parallel;
# M8.4 CtxLexer bridge; M8.5 undo-ring hook; M8.6 bracket
# discrimination; M8.7 smoke-mode goldens; M8.8 line-continuation
# inside strings) remain opaque-pointer by design; only M8.4 adds a
# one-way intra-library linker reference against R221.M4 CtxLexer's
# `_cl_depth`.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. MultiLine runs a single-pass ASCII-level byte
# walker over caller-supplied input and stores four counters/latches
# in its own `.bss`; no KIND surface is touched by this module at
# raw M8. The forward-path landings (M8.1..M8.8) all stay in-process
# and introduce NO new KIND surface.

# ---- Error band 0x5900..0x59FF (R227.M3, import_resolve) -------------
#
# Import-resolution packed-status range for the R227 pds container /
# loader wave's THIRD milestone (parser-tier `#import` directive
# parser, path resolver, dependency-graph builder, and DFS cycle
# detector). Twelve slots used at R227.M3:
#
#   IR_OK                       : 0        operation succeeded.
#   IR_ERR_SYNTAX               : 0x5900   `_ir_parse_directive`
#                                          malformed directive bytes
#                                          (missing `#import`, missing
#                                          '"' / 'as' / name, bad path
#                                          byte, zero-length path or
#                                          name).
#   IR_ERR_PATH_TOO_LONG        : 0x5901   `_ir_parse_directive`:
#                                          quoted-path length >
#                                          IR_PATH_MAX (256 bytes).
#   IR_ERR_NAME_TOO_LONG        : 0x5902   `_ir_parse_directive`:
#                                          `as name` ident length >
#                                          IR_NAME_MAX (16 bytes).
#   IR_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL        : 0x5903   `_ir_parse_directive`: out
#                                          == 0 or out_cap < 48;
#                                          `_ir_resolve_path`: out ==
#                                          0 or out_cap < path_len +
#                                          5; `_ir_build_graph`: out
#                                          == 0 or out_cap <
#                                          n_directives * 36;
#                                          `_ir_detect_cycle`: out_cap
#                                          < graph_len * 4.
#   IR_ERR_BAD_RECORD           : 0x5904   `_ir_build_graph` /
#                                          `_ir_detect_cycle`:
#                                          directive magic !=
#                                          0x54504D49 OR adjacency row
#                                          references a module index
#                                          >= graph_len.
#   IR_ERR_TOO_MANY_IMPORTS     : 0x5905   `_ir_detect_cycle`:
#                                          adjacency row n_imports >
#                                          IR_MAX_IMPORTS (8).
#   IR_ERR_TOO_MANY_MODULES     : 0x5906   `_ir_build_graph` /
#                                          `_ir_detect_cycle`:
#                                          n_directives / graph_len >
#                                          IR_MAX_NODES (64).
#   E1100_IMPORT_CYCLE          : 0x5910   `_ir_detect_cycle` E1100:
#                                          back-edge observed from
#                                          current node to a GRAY
#                                          ancestor. The upper-bound
#                                          cycle length (DFS stack
#                                          depth at observation
#                                          moment) is packed into
#                                          bits [63:32]. Mirror of
#                                          R133.M3 ImportGraph's
#                                          IG_ERR_CYCLE (0x3A00) at
#                                          the parser tier.
#   E1101_RESOLVE_FAILED        : 0x5911   `_ir_resolve_path` E1101:
#                                          path_len == 0 (at raw M3
#                                          the resolver stub does not
#                                          `stat` the resolved path;
#                                          M3.3 wires that in and
#                                          surfaces missing-file as
#                                          this same code).
#   E1102_DUPLICATE_NAME        : 0x5912   `_ir_build_graph` E1102:
#                                          two or more directives in
#                                          the same source share the
#                                          same name_inline byte run
#                                          (collision in the per-
#                                          source `as name` table).
#   E1103_PATH_TRAVERSAL        : 0x5913   `_ir_resolve_path` E1103:
#                                          a `..` component is
#                                          encountered at depth 0 of
#                                          the resolved path (would
#                                          escape BOTH the project
#                                          root and the system root).
#
# Slots 0x5907..0x590F reserved for R227.M3.1+ parse / resolve
# diagnostics (nested quote inside path, UTF-8 non-ASCII rejection,
# syscall-side stat-fault surfacing, canonicalisation drift). Slots
# 0x5914..0x59FF reserved for R227.M3.2+ graph / cycle diagnostics
# (per-edge annotation faults, module-count overflow across concurrent
# sources, intern-table collisions for the per-source name table).
#
# The 0x5900..0x59FF carve-out skips the 0x5800..0x58FF band (reserved
# for the Wave 129 parallel-arm R225.M6 landing) and sits above the
# 0x5700..0x57FF R229.M7 ViMode band and below the 0x5A00..0x5AFF
# R229.M8 MultiLine band -- a downstream compound-error dispatcher can
# discriminate "the import-resolution substrate faulted" from every
# other satellite's failure modes via `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x59`.
# ImportResolve's band is disjoint from every other parser-tier
# packed-status range in the paideia-os tree.
#
# Grep confirmation at R227.M3 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x59[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> no prior hits (band is virgin).
#   grep -rE '0x59(0[0-6]|1[0-3])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
#        design/shell/import-resolution-parser.md + import_resolve.pdx.

# libpdx-pds-parser.R227.M3 (import_resolve.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module linker
# dependency at raw M3. All five public entry points (`_ir_init`,
# `_ir_parse_directive`, `_ir_resolve_path`, `_ir_build_graph`,
# `_ir_detect_cycle`) touch ONLY this module's own `.bss` state
# (`_ir_imports[64 * 48]` 3072-byte directive pool, `_ir_import_count`
# u64 live-count scalar, `_ir_color[64]` 64-byte DFS colour scratch,
# `_ir_stk[128]` 1024-byte DFS work-stack scratch) and caller-supplied
# pointer args. ImportResolve mirrors the ALGORITHM shape of the
# kernel-side R133.M3 ImportGraph but shares NO linker-visible
# surface with it -- the two halves of the import-graph machinery
# live on opposite sides of the user-space / kernel-space boundary
# and their adjacency tables bytewise agree (36-byte stride with u32
# n_imports + 8 u32 import ordinals) so the kernel-tier loader can
# re-run its own cycle detector over the parser-emitted adjacency as
# a defensive guard. ALL FIVE public entry points are PURE LEAVES
# (zero nested CALL) so r11 is used freely as the three-term
# address-precompute scratch; `_ir_init` has zero pushes;
# `_ir_parse_directive` has 6 pushes (rbx = bytes_ptr, rbp =
# bytes_len, r12 = out, r13 = cursor, r14 = token_start, r15 =
# token_len) with FNV-1a 64 inlined so no stack pad; `_ir_resolve_
# path` has 4 pushes (rbx = path_bytes, rbp = path_len, r12 =
# out_resolved, r13 = write cursor) with no FNV so no stack pad;
# `_ir_build_graph` has 5 pushes (rbx = directives_ptr, rbp =
# n_directives, r12 = out_graph, r13 = outer i, r14 = row_base scratch);
# `_ir_detect_cycle` has 6 pushes (rbx = graph_ptr, rbp = graph_len,
# r12 = out_cycle_path, r13 = out_cap, r14 = stk_bytes, r15 =
# write_cursor) + sub rsp,16 for the start_i spill so the inner DFS
# loop may freely clobber every caller-save register (same discipline
# as R133.M3 ImportGraph's `_ig_build_order`). ImportResolve's `.bss`
# is disjoint from every other satellite module's `.bss` (parser-
# family: `_cmd_sig_*`, `_fi_*`, `_afs_*`, `_sch_sig_*`; row-family:
# `_hm_pool`, `_rt_*`, `_er_*`, `_cr_*`; session-family:
# `_se_env_pool`, `_te_scheme_pool`; lexer-family: `_cl_stack`,
# `_cl_depth`, `_cl_last_kind`; scope-family: `_ls_frame_pool`,
# `_ls_frame_next`; registry-family: `_cmdreg_entries`,
# `_cmdreg_*`; job-family: `_jc_jobs`, `_jc_job_count`, `_jc_fg_tid`;
# history-family: `_h_entries`, `_h_entry_count`, `_h_cursor`;
# line-editor-family: `_le_buf`, `_le_buf_len`, `_le_cursor_*`,
# `_le_kill_*`, `_le_meta_pending`, `_le_last_search_turn`;
# vi-family: `_vi_mode`, `_vi_pending_op`, `_vi_pending_g`,
# `_vi_visual_anchor`; multi-line-family: `_ml_brace_depth`,
# `_ml_paren_depth`, `_ml_in_string`, `_ml_backslash_pending`). The
# forward-path landings (M3.1 non-ASCII paths; M3.2 interned name
# table; M3.3 VFS stat probe; M3.4 per-source handle; M3.5 name-
# table edge resolution) remain opaque-pointer by design; only M3.3
# adds a one-way external linker reference against the kernel's
# `stat` substrate.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. ImportResolve is a self-contained ASCII-
# level byte walker over caller-supplied input + a DFS cycle
# detector over caller-supplied adjacency; no KIND surface is
# touched by this module at raw M3. The forward-path landings
# (M3.1..M3.5) all stay in-process and introduce NO new KIND
# surface at the parser tier; M3.3's VFS wire-up is substrate-side
# and surfaces up through the parser-tier packed-status low 32 bits
# without claiming a KIND.

# ---- Error band 0x5800..0x58FF (R225.M6, rank_check) -----------------
#
# Typechecker-tier rank-restricted-let-polymorphism packed-status
# range. Six slots used at R225.M6 (two active + four reserved):
#
#   RC_OK                          : 0        scheme passes the rank
#                                             gate.
#   RC_ERR_BAD_SCHEME              : 0x5800   `_rc_check_scheme` saw a
#                                             malformed scheme byte
#                                             stream: header truncated;
#                                             scheme_len inconsistent
#                                             with header; n_bound_vars
#                                             > 64; n_body_nodes outside
#                                             [1..128]; or a body node
#                                             with kind byte outside
#                                             {TYVAR=1, TYCON=2, TYAPP=3,
#                                             TYROW=4, TYFORALL=5,
#                                             TYSCHEME=6}. Also returned
#                                             by `_rc_check_binding` on
#                                             scope_kind outside [1..3].
#   RC_ERR_RANK_TOO_HIGH           : 0x5810   `_rc_check_scheme` E0980:
#                                             body[i>0] carries kind
#                                             TYFORALL (5) or TYSCHEME
#                                             (6). The standard rank-2
#                                             refusal case: a nested
#                                             quantifier sits under some
#                                             body node (not at the
#                                             outermost position) and
#                                             the user needs an explicit
#                                             annotation at the function
#                                             boundary.
#   RC_ERR_NESTED_FORALL           : 0x5811   `_rc_check_scheme` E0981:
#                                             body[0] carries kind
#                                             TYFORALL (5) or TYSCHEME
#                                             (6). The root body node is
#                                             itself a quantifier
#                                             carrier, which cannot be
#                                             the SINGLE outer `∀`
#                                             because the header already
#                                             encodes that one; so a
#                                             nested quantifier at the
#                                             root means the scheme is
#                                             doubly-quantified (a
#                                             wire-format bug the
#                                             elaborator should never
#                                             emit).
#   RC_ERR_PREDICATIVE_VIOLATION   : 0x5812   reserved for R225.M6.2:
#                                             nested quantifier as a
#                                             TyApp arg (type-
#                                             constructor-applied-to-
#                                             polymorphic-type). Requires
#                                             TyApp head-discrimination
#                                             which the M4.1 wire-format
#                                             extension adds. At raw M6
#                                             the walker surfaces this
#                                             case as RANK_TOO_HIGH.
#                                             E0982 diagnostic.
#   RC_ERR_UNANNOTATED_BOUNDARY    : 0x5813   reserved for R225.M6.3:
#                                             user-facing diagnostic
#                                             when the rank-2 is
#                                             genuinely needed and no
#                                             annotation exists at the
#                                             boundary. Requires the
#                                             R224.M1 lambda-literal
#                                             grammar to learn
#                                             annotation-at-boundary
#                                             syntax (`\(f :: ∀a. a →
#                                             a). ...`). E0983
#                                             diagnostic.
#
# The 0x5800..0x58FF range is reserved for rank-restricted-let-
# polymorphism packed-status codes across R225.M6+. R225.M1 (hm_core)
# owns the disjoint 0x2D00..0x2DFF slice; R225.M2 (row_types) owns
# 0x3600..0x36FF; R225.M4 (effect_unify) owns 0x4900..0x49FF -- the
# four R225 submodules do not share status codes because a downstream
# dispatcher's compound error path must distinguish "pure HM operation
# faulted" from "row operation faulted" from "effect-row join / shape-
# check faulted" from "rank-restriction gate rejected". Slots
# 0x5801..0x580F (sub-band under RC_ERR_BAD_SCHEME's 0x5800 anchor)
# and 0x5814..0x58FF reserved for M6.1+ follow-ups (per-REPL-turn
# violation counter output, finer-grained predicative discrimination,
# per-binding-site diagnostic chain carries).
#
# Diagnostic-family mapping (E0980 family per the issue brief):
#   E0980_RANK_TOO_HIGH         -> RC_ERR_RANK_TOO_HIGH         (0x5810)
#   E0981_NESTED_FORALL         -> RC_ERR_NESTED_FORALL         (0x5811)
#   E0982_PREDICATIVE_VIOLATION -> RC_ERR_PREDICATIVE_VIOLATION (0x5812)
#   E0983_UNANNOTATED_BOUNDARY  -> RC_ERR_UNANNOTATED_BOUNDARY  (0x5813)
#
# The 0x5800..0x58FF carve-out was PRE-RESERVED by the Wave 129
# parallel-arm R229.M8 MultiLine (0x5A00..0x5AFF) and R227.M3
# ImportResolve (0x5900..0x59FF) catalog notes; this landing claims it
# active. The band sits adjacent to the 0x5700..0x57FF R229.M7 ViMode
# band and below the 0x5900..0x59FF R227.M3 ImportResolve band -- a
# downstream compound-error dispatcher can discriminate "the rank-
# restriction gate rejected" from every other satellite's failure
# modes by a single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x58` idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R225.M6 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x58[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> no prior hits (band is virgin outside this landing).
#   grep -rE '0x58(0[0]|1[0-3])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + caps.decl +
#        design/shell/rank-restricted-polymorphism.md + rank_check.pdx.
#
# ---- R225.M6 catalogue paragraph (rank_check) ------------------------
#
#   R225.M6 RankCheck (rank_check.pdx, landed): pure in-process rank-
#      restricted-let-polymorphism enforcement (P10) over a HmCore
#      scheme byte stream. Layered on the R225.M1 HmCore scheme wire
#      format and parallel to the R225.M4 EffectUnify `_eu_check_rank`
#      shape-validator precedent. Standard HM (Milner 1978, Damas-
#      Milner 1982) is RANK-1: every type scheme `σ ::= ∀a_1 ... a_n.
#      τ` carries `τ` as a monotype (no `∀` under an arrow). A rank-2
#      scheme defeats first-order unification; paideia-shell commits
#      to rank-1 by default with an explicit annotation-at-boundary
#      escape hatch landing at R225.M6.3. `_rc_check_scheme` walks a
#      scheme byte stream and rejects any body node carrying kind
#      TYFORALL (5) or TYSCHEME (6) -- both reserved for the R225.M4.1
#      wire-format extension landing nested-quantifier body nodes --
#      with a specific sub-dispatch: body[0] -> `RC_ERR_NESTED_FORALL`
#      (0x5811 = E0981) because the header already encodes the single
#      outer `∀`; body[i>0] -> `RC_ERR_RANK_TOO_HIGH` (0x5810 = E0980)
#      because the quantifier is nested under some parent. At raw M6
#      the HmCore wire format only emits kinds {TYVAR=1, TYCON=2,
#      TYAPP=3, TYROW=4} so every well-formed scheme passes; the
#      walker is forward-facing (the moment M4.1 lands, nested
#      quantifiers are REJECTED IMMEDIATELY here without a second-pass
#      depth counter). Three public entry points, ALL PURE-OR-NEAR-PURE
#      LEAVES:
#      `_rc_init()` -- bare `ret`. M6 holds NO .bss state (the walker
#        is purely over caller-supplied data). Kept for parallelism
#        with the parser-family init-hook convention (`_st_init`
#        R223.M7, `_lp_init` R224.M7, `_ha_init` R223.M6, `_ws_init`
#        R223.M5, `_ds_init` R222.M8, `_dm_init` R222.M6, `_cmdreg_init`
#        R222.M5) so an M6.1 landing that adds .bss (per-REPL-turn
#        violation counter) does not break the API.
#      `_rc_check_scheme(scheme_ptr, scheme_len) -> status` --
#        Phase 1: shape validation (scheme_len >= 8; n_bound_vars <=
#        64; n_body_nodes in [1..128]; scheme_len == 8 + 4*n_bound +
#        16*n_body) with BAD_SCHEME on any failure. Phase 2: walk
#        body nodes [0..n_body) computing node_addr = scheme_ptr +
#        body_offset + i*16 via three-term pre-compute into r11;
#        kind-byte dispatch: {1,2,3,4} -> continue; 5 or 6 -> rank
#        violation (sub-dispatch on i); other -> BAD_SCHEME. Pure
#        leaf: zero callee-save pushes; no stack frame; no nested
#        CALL. r11 used freely as the three-term address scratch.
#      `_rc_check_binding(scope_kind, scheme_ptr, scheme_len) ->
#        status` -- composite entry over a LetScope-shaped binding.
#        Validates scope_kind in [1..3] (BAD_SCHEME on fail); on
#        SCOPE_LAMBDA (3) returns RC_OK WITHOUT walking the scheme
#        bytes (lambda params are NOT generalised per standard HM);
#        on SCOPE_LET (1) / SCOPE_LETREC (2) delegates to
#        `_rc_check_scheme`. Zero callee-save pushes + `sub rsp, 8`
#        align = 8 B frame; entry rsp%16==8, after align rsp%16==0
#        at the single intra-module nested CALL. The signature uses
#        explicit `scope_kind + scheme bytes` rather than a LetScope
#        frame pointer because raw M6 has no materialiser from a
#        HmCore pool_idx to a scheme byte stream; the M6.4 wire-up
#        introduces the frame-dereference path.
#      All kind constants (`RC_HM_KIND_TYVAR..RC_HM_KIND_TYSCHEME`)
#      and layout constants (`RC_SCHEME_*`) are duplicated as local
#      module constants (precedent: every parser-family satellite that
#      mirrors HmCore / row_types / effect_row constants) so this
#      module compiles standalone without a cross-module `let` import.
#      Labels prefixed `rc_` (grep-confirmed unique against every
#      landed parser-tier prefix). No KIND surface; no external cross-
#      library linker dependency; no intra-library cross-module linker
#      dependency -- RankCheck is a self-contained byte walker over
#      caller-supplied scheme bytes.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Rank-restriction enforcement runs purely in
# parse / typecheck time over caller-supplied scheme bytes; no KIND
# surface is touched by this module. R225.M6.4 (LetScope-frame
# dereferencing) will add an intra-library linker reference to
# `_ls_resolve` + a HmCore scheme-materialiser but STILL no KIND
# surface because the materialiser output is handed back as a flat
# byte buffer.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R225.M6 (rank_check.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module linker
# dependency at raw M6. All three public entry points (`_rc_init`,
# `_rc_check_scheme`, `_rc_check_binding`) touch ONLY caller-supplied
# pointer args; the module carries NO `.bss` state at raw M6. The
# HmCore scheme wire-format constants (`HM_KIND_*`, `HM_SCHEME_*`)
# that the walker needs are replicated as module-local constants (the
# same disjoint-leaf precedent as the parser-family's prior satellites
# that mirror HmCore / row_types / effect_row constants). All three
# public entry points can be linked standalone against a bare parser-
# tier object graph; no new `--extra-obj-dir` is introduced. The
# forward-path landings (M6.1 `.bss` violation counter + fingerprint;
# M6.2 PREDICATIVE_VIOLATION wire-up when M4.1 TyApp head-
# discrimination lands; M6.3 UNANNOTATED_BOUNDARY wire-up when the
# R224.M1 lambda-literal grammar learns annotation-at-boundary syntax;
# M6.4 LetScope-frame dereference via `_ls_resolve` + HmCore scheme-
# materialiser; M6.5 20-test adversarial corpus + fingerprint goldens)
# remain opaque-pointer by design; only M6.4 adds a one-way intra-
# library linker reference against R224.M5 LetScope and R225.M1
# HmCore.
#
# RankCheck's `_rc_` label prefix is DISJOINT from every landed
# parser-tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, rp_, fo_,
# sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, pl_lam_, lp_, hm_,
# rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_) and from the
# paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).


# ---- Error band 0x5B00..0x5BFF (R225.M7, diag_report) ----------------
#
# Typechecker-tier diagnostic-reporting packed-status range. Six
# slots used at R225.M7:
#
#   DR_OK                       : 0        build / render success.
#   DR_ERR_BAD_INPUT            : 0x5B00   `_dr_build` saw `out == 0`
#                                          or `_dr_render_*` saw a
#                                          null record / out pointer.
#   DR_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL        : 0x5B01   `_dr_build` saw `out_cap <
#                                          DR_RECORD_BYTES = 128`.
#   DR_ERR_BAD_RECORD           : 0x5B02   render saw `record_len <
#                                          128` or a magic-field
#                                          mismatch (not 'DIAG' =
#                                          0x47414944 LE).
#   DR_ERR_BAD_SEVERITY         : 0x5B03   `_dr_build` saw `severity
#                                          > DR_SEV_HINT = 3`.
#   DR_ERR_RENDER_OVERFLOW      : 0x5B04   render output exceeded
#                                          `out_cap`; the sticky
#                                          overflow sentinel on any
#                                          internal emit helper
#                                          propagated to the final
#                                          cursor check.
#
# The 0x5B00..0x5BFF range is reserved for diagnostic-reporting
# packed-status codes across R225.M7+. Disjoint from every other
# R225 submodule's band (R225.M1 HmCore 0x2D00..0x2DFF; R225.M2
# RowTypes 0x3600..0x36FF; R225.M4 EffectUnify 0x4900..0x49FF;
# R225.M6 RankCheck 0x5800..0x58FF) because a downstream dispatcher's
# compound error path must distinguish "diag build / render refused"
# from "pure HM operation faulted" vs "row operation faulted" vs
# "effect-row shape faulted" vs "rank-restriction gate rejected".
# Slots 0x5B05..0x5BFF reserved for M7.1+ follow-ups (M7.1 queue-
# full; M7.2 line/col overflow; M7.3 secondary-span-OOB; M7.4
# suggestion-table-miss).
#
# The 0x5B00..0x5BFF carve-out sits adjacent to the 0x5A00..0x5AFF
# R229.M8 MultiLine band and below the 0x5D00..0x5DFF R229.M9
# Renderer band -- a downstream compound-error dispatcher can
# discriminate "diag build/render refused" from every other
# satellite's failure modes by a single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x5B`
# idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R225.M7 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x5B[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> no prior packed-status hits in this library.
#   grep -rE '0x5B(00|01|02|03|04)\b'
#        paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + diag_report.pdx +
#        design/shell/diagnostic-reporting.md. The sole landed
#        0x5B00 reference in paideia-os is
#        src/user/aml/aml_lex.pdx R30.M1-001 (AML opcode anchor
#        ExtOpPrefix | 0x00); it is NOT a packed-status band and
#        does NOT collide with this carve-out.
#
# ---- R225.M7 catalogue paragraph (diag_report) -----------------------
#
#   R225.M7 DiagReport (diag_report.pdx, landed): pure in-process
#      typed-error diagnostic reporting with source spans +
#      mechanical suggestions. Layered on the R225.M1 HmCore /
#      R225.M2 RowTypes / R225.M4 EffectUnify / R225.M6 RankCheck /
#      R224.M4 CapRow / R223.M7 SessionType error-code bands via
#      a disjoint 0x5B00..0x5BFF carve-out. Preserves the Q-A5
#      source-span invariant: every Diag record stores primary (and
#      optional secondary) spans as byte offsets into the caller's
#      SOURCE buffer, not the NFC-form intermediate. Four public
#      entry points over caller-supplied buffers:
#      `_dr_init()` -- bare `ret`. M7 holds NO .bss state (every
#        Diag record is caller-owned; the session-local diagnostic
#        queue head slot lands at M7.1). Kept for parallelism with
#        the parser-family init-hook convention.
#      `_dr_build(code_sev, primary_off, primary_len, strings_ptr,
#        out, out_cap) -> packed_u64` -- populate a 128-byte Diag
#        record at `out`. Packs code + severity into one u64 (6-arg
#        SysV x64 unsafe-body ceiling per
#        tools/paideia-as/tests/build-emit/sysv_x64_7arg_unsafe_
#        body_b1708.pdx + design/compiler/lambda-arity-stack-spill.md
#        §4.4/§11). Bundles the three 32-byte string buffers
#        (expected / actual / suggestion) into one strings_ptr
#        triple at offsets 0 / 32 / 64. Validates out != 0 (BAD_INPUT
#        0x5B00); out_cap >= 128 (OUT_TOO_SMALL 0x5B01); severity <=
#        3 (BAD_SEVERITY 0x5B03). Zero-fills 128 bytes before
#        populating (secondary span, next_diag_ref, and string
#        fields when strings_ptr == 0 all default to 0 / all-NUL).
#        Writes magic 'DIAG' = 0x47414944 LE u32 at [+0]; code u16
#        at [+4..+6); severity u16 at [+6..+8); primary_off u32 at
#        [+8]; primary_len u32 at [+12]. On strings_ptr != 0,
#        byte-copies 96 bytes to [out+24..+120). Returns packed
#        (128 << 32) | DR_OK on success. Pure leaf; zero callee-save
#        pushes; no stack frame; no nested CALL (128-byte zero-fill
#        is 16 unrolled u64 stores; 96-byte copy is a byte-count
#        loop).
#      `_dr_render_lsp(record_ptr, record_len, out, out_cap)
#        -> packed_u64` -- serialize to one-line LSP-compatible
#        JSON: {"range":{"start":{"line":0,"character":<N>},"end":
#        {"line":0,"character":<N>}},"severity":<N>,"code":"E<hex4>"
#        ,"message":"expected <exp>, got <act>; hint: <sug>"}.
#        Suggestion clause omitted when suggestion_inline is empty
#        (first byte NUL). LSP severity = our severity + 1 (0..3 ->
#        1..4). `line` emitted as 0 at raw M7 (byte-offset fallback
#        per the LSP range `character` field); M7.2 wires the UTF-8-
#        aware byte-offset -> line/column resolver. 6-push callee-
#        save frame (rbx/rbp/r12/r13/r14/r15 = 48 B); entry rsp%16==8
#        -> after pushes rsp%16==0 at every nested CALL into the four
#        module-private helpers.
#      `_dr_render_human(record_ptr, record_len, out, out_cap)
#        -> packed_u64` -- serialize to a compact single-line human
#        form: E<hex4> <SEV> at <dec_off>+<dec_len>: expected <exp>,
#        got <act> (hint: <sug>). SEV is one of ERR / WARN / INFO /
#        HINT. Same frame plan as `_dr_render_lsp`.
#      Four module-private pure-leaf helpers: `_dr_h_emit_lit`
#        (bounded byte-copy with sticky-overflow sentinel),
#        `_dr_h_emit_cstr` (byte-copy up to max_bytes or first NUL),
#        `_dr_h_emit_u16_hex` (4 hex nibbles), `_dr_h_emit_u32_dec`
#        (variable 1..10 decimal digits via greedy subtraction over
#        the 10-entry _dr_pow10 table in .rodata; the pdx dialect
#        does not expose IDIV / MUL so greedy subtraction is the
#        canonical shape vs. magic-constant multiplication which
#        would need 128-bit MUL / MULX). Max cost per decimal emit:
#        10 x 9 = 90 subtractions.
#      Record wire format (128 bytes exactly; stride power-of-two
#      for `shl rN, 7` addressing): magic u32 (0x47414944 'DIAG' LE)
#      at [+0]; code u16 at [+4]; severity u16 at [+6]; primary_off
#      u32 at [+8]; primary_len u32 at [+12]; secondary_off u32 at
#      [+16]; secondary_len u32 at [+20]; expected_type_name u8[32]
#      at [+24]; actual_type_name u8[32] at [+56]; suggestion_inline
#      u8[32] at [+88]; next_diag_ref u64 at [+120]. NUL-pad
#      convention for the 32-byte strings (render stops at first
#      NUL; a field with first byte NUL contributes nothing to the
#      rendered output). Secondary spans and next_diag_ref default
#      to 0 at raw M7; M7.1 wires the session queue head via
#      next_diag_ref; M7.3 wires the secondary-span patcher.
#      All status-code immediates (0x5B00..0x5B04) fit imm32 with
#      bit 31 clear so direct `cmp reg, imm32` and `mov reg, imm32`
#      are sign-safe. Magic 0x47414944 likewise has bit 31 clear.
#      Labels prefixed `dr_` for the public entries and `dr_h_` for
#      the helpers (grep-confirmed unique against every landed
#      parser-tier prefix). Static tables: `_dr_pow10` [u32; 10],
#      `_dr_hex_digits` [u8; 16], `_dr_sev_names` [u8; 16], and
#      eleven literal-template byte arrays spanning the LSP JSON
#      prefix / infix / suffix segments and the human-form
#      separators.
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Diagnostic reporting runs purely in
# user space over caller-supplied record / strings / out buffers;
# no KIND surface is touched by this module. R225.M7.6 (kernel-
# side REPL-dispatch wire-up) consumes the rendered byte strings
# via IPC but does not require THIS module to touch a KIND.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R225.M7 (diag_report.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module
# linker dependency at raw M7. All four public entry points touch
# ONLY caller-supplied pointer args and the module's own .rodata
# tables; the module carries NO .bss state at raw M7. The forward-
# path landings (M7.1 .bss session-local diagnostic queue + enqueue/
# drain helpers; M7.2 byte-offset -> line/column resolver + LSP
# range refinement; M7.3 secondary-span patcher; M7.4 code-indexed
# suggestion table; M7.5 60-error acceptance corpus + goldens; M7.6
# kernel-side REPL-dispatch wire-up; M7.7 LSP transport side at
# R220.M9) remain opaque-pointer by design -- only M7.2 adds a
# one-way intra-library linker reference against R221.M6 SpanMap
# (`_sm_lookup` for the pre-NFC offset refinement when the caller
# chains build + render over a NFC-normalised source buffer).
#
# DiagReport's `dr_` label prefix (plus the `dr_h_` prefix on the
# four module-private helpers) is DISJOINT from every landed parser-
# tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, rp_, fo_,
# sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, pl_lam_, lp_, hm_,
# rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_, rd_)
# and from the paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if, let, fn,
# ret, done).


# ---- Error band 0x5D00..0x5DFF (R229.M9, renderer) -------------------
#
# Renderer packed-status range. Nine slots used at R229.M9:
#
#   RD_OK                   = 0
#   RD_ERR_BAD_PTR          = 0x5D00  (any required pointer == 0)
#   RD_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL    = 0x5D01  (out_cap < envelope + payload)
#   RD_ERR_BAD_MAGIC        = 0x5D02  (cap_record[+0..+4] != 'TCAP')
#   RD_ERR_BAD_KIND         = 0x5D03  (kind byte outside {0, 1, 2})
#   RD_ERR_NO_CAPABILITY    = 0x5D04  (render* called before
#                                      _rd_set_capability or after
#                                      _rd_init)
#   RD_ERR_BAD_RECORD       = 0x5D05  (_rd_render_record: record_ref
#                                      == 0 OR record_len >
#                                      RD_MAX_RECORD_BYTES=4096)
#   RD_ERR_WIDTH_FAULT      = 0x5D06  (_wp_cols returned
#                                      WP_ERR_INVALID_UTF8 0x3F00;
#                                      partial bytes_written preserved
#                                      in high 32 of packed return)
#   RD_ERR_BAD_COL_COUNT    = 0x5D07  (_rd_render_table: n_cols
#                                      outside [1..RD_MAX_COLS=32])
#   RD_ERR_BAD_ROW_COUNT    = 0x5D08  (_rd_render_table: n_rows
#                                      outside [0..RD_MAX_ROWS=256])
#
# Slots 0x5D09..0x5DFF reserved for R229.M9.1+ follow-ups
# (RD_ERR_KERNEL_EMITTER_UNAVAILABLE on an M9.1 kernel-side-emitter
# wire-up when the function-pointer slot is zero;
# RD_ERR_BAD_CELL_LEN on an M9.3 strict-mode cell-length cap;
# RD_ERR_BAD_COL_WIDTH on an M9.4 zero-width-column refusal; etc.).
#
# The 0x5D00..0x5DFF carve-out is disjoint from every landed parser-
# tier packed-status band and from the Wave 129 parallel-arm bands:
#
#   0x5700..0x57FF R229.M7 ViMode               (landed Wave 128)
#   0x5800..0x58FF R225.M6 RankCheck            (landed Wave 129 arm)
#   0x5900..0x59FF R227.M3 ImportResolve        (landed Wave 129 arm)
#   0x5A00..0x5AFF R229.M8 MultiLine            (landed Wave 129 arm)
#   0x5B00..0x5BFF (reserved; held parallel-arm slot)
#   0x5C00..0x5CFF (reserved; held parallel-arm slot)
#   0x5D00..0x5DFF R229.M9 Renderer             (THIS landing)
#
# A downstream compound-error dispatcher can discriminate "the
# renderer faulted" from every other sibling satellite's failure
# modes by a single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x5D` idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R229.M9 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x5D[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> hits only in src/renderer.pdx, caps.decl, STATUS.md,
#        CHANGELOG.md, and design/shell/renderer-integration.md.
#   grep -rE '0x5D0[0-8]\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + renderer.pdx +
#        design/shell/renderer-integration.md.
#
# ---- R229.M9 catalogue paragraph (renderer) --------------------------
#
#   R229.M9 Renderer (renderer.pdx, landed): parser-tier terminal-
#      capability-aware output router. Owns the active TCAP capability
#      record in .bss (installed by the REPL at session boot from the
#      kernel-tier R129.M1 XTGETTCAP probe answer) and dispatches
#      every record / table render through one of three paths -- Kitty
#      graphics APC envelope, ANSI-256 SGR + Unicode box-drawing, or
#      plain-ASCII fallback -- based on the capability's kind byte.
#      Routes to design/terminal/semantic-shell.md §9 verbatim. Four
#      public entry points over singleton `.bss` (40 B = 32 B TCAP
#      record + 2 u64 scalars for ready flag + reserved):
#      `_rd_init()` -- pure-leaf defensive scrub of the 32-byte TCAP
#        record (four u64 stores) + the `_rd_ready` flag + a reserved
#        u64 slot. `.bss` zero-init at program load already satisfies
#        the cold-boot semantic (`_rd_ready == 0` -> every render*
#        returns RD_ERR_NO_CAPABILITY); the hook exists for test-time
#        reset + chaos-restart.
#      `_rd_set_capability(cap_record_ref) -> status` -- pure leaf
#        that validates (ptr != 0, magic 'TCAP' = 0x50414354 LE at
#        [+0], kind byte in {0, 1, 2}) and copies 32 bytes into
#        `_rd_cap_record` via four u64 loads/stores, then sets
#        `_rd_ready := 1`. Rejects RD_ERR_BAD_PTR / RD_ERR_BAD_MAGIC /
#        RD_ERR_BAD_KIND without touching the state slot.
#      `_rd_render_record(record_ref, record_len, out, out_cap) ->
#        packed_u64` -- pure-leaf router over the active kind:
#        KITTY=0 -> `\e_G;` (4 B) + record + `\e\\` (2 B); ANSI256=1
#        -> `\e[38;5;255m` (11 B) + record + `\e[0m` (4 B); ANSI16=2
#        -> record verbatim. Validates record_ref != 0, record_len
#        <= RD_MAX_RECORD_BYTES=4096, out != 0, `_rd_ready == 1`,
#        out_cap >= needed. Returns (bytes_written << 32) | RD_OK on
#        success. Pure leaf; zero pushes; zero stack frame; zero
#        nested CALL (byte copy inlined).
#      `_rd_render_table(rows_ref, n_rows, cols_ref, n_cols, out,
#        out_cap) -> packed_u64` -- the ONE non-leaf: 6-push
#        callee-save frame + sub rsp 24 for n_cols / out_cap /
#        n_rows spill, giving rsp%16==0 at every nested CALL (entry
#        rsp%16==8; 48 B pushes + 24 B spill = 72 B; (8+72) mod 16
#        = 0). Nested CALL: `_wp_cols` (R221.M3; intra-library)
#        once per cell to compute cluster-aware column widths. Row
#        shape: `| cell_0 | cell_1 | ... |\n`. Per-cell: emit cell
#        bytes, then (col.width - cell_cols) ASCII spaces for
#        padding. On _wp_cols fault (WP_ERR_INVALID_UTF8), packs
#        partial bytes_written in high 32 of RD_ERR_WIDTH_FAULT
#        return. Cell addresses computed via `mul rN` (unsigned
#        64x64 single-operand) rather than 2-op `imul r, imm` so
#        the encoder-discipline no-imul-r-imm rule holds. Recomputes
#        cell_addr post-CALL (trades a cheap mul for one less stack
#        spill slot).
#      Four pub constants beyond the standard status / kind / flag
#      taxonomy: RD_CAP_MAGIC = 0x50414354 'TCAP' LE;
#      RD_MAX_COLS = 32, RD_MAX_ROWS = 256, RD_MAX_COL_WIDTH = 128,
#      RD_MAX_RECORD_BYTES = 4096, RD_MAX_CELL_BYTES = 256;
#      RD_CELL_STRIDE = 16, RD_COL_STRIDE = 16, RD_CAP_BYTES = 32
#      (all strides powers of two for `shl rN, 4` / `shl rN, 5`
#      addressing without 2-op `imul r, imm`); RD_WP_ERR_INVALID_UTF8
#      = 0x3F00 (replicated as a module-local constant so the
#      status discrimination against WidthPass stays byte-identical
#      without a cross-module `let` import). Labels prefixed `rd_`
#      with per-function suffix (`rd_ini_`, `rd_sc_`, `rd_rr_`,
#      `rd_rt_`). Grep-confirmed disjoint from every landed parser-
#      tier label prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
#      sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_,
#      pl_lam_, rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_,
#      ls_, lp_, hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_,
#      ml_, ir_, rc_) and from the paideia-as reserved-keyword set
#      (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Terminal-capability dispatch runs purely in
# user space over the caller-supplied TCAP capability record (which
# itself has no cap handle inside); the KIND surface this module
# invokes is EMPTY. R229.M9.1 (kernel-side emitter wire-up) adds
# two weak-import function-pointer slots to indirect through the
# kernel-tier R129.M2 Kitty APC emitter + R129.M3 ANSI-256 table
# emitter; neither of those lies inside the current library's
# linker reach and both are opaque function pointers from THIS
# module's perspective.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R229.M9 (renderer.pdx) has ONE intra-library
# linker-visible FUNCTION dependency on R221.M3 WidthPass's
# `_wp_cols` (used once per cell in `_rd_render_table` for cluster-
# aware column alignment). NO external cross-library linker
# dependency. NO intra-library DATA linker reference (the only data
# this module touches at render time is its own 40 B `.bss` singleton
# and the caller-supplied rows_ref / cols_ref / out / cap_record
# buffers). WidthPass's error band 0x3F00..0x3FFF carves no slot in
# the 0x5D00..0x5DFF Renderer band; the discrimination happens
# strictly against the WP_ERR_INVALID_UTF8 = 0x3F00 constant
# (replicated as RD_WP_ERR_INVALID_UTF8 in Renderer's own constants
# section so no cross-module `let` import is needed; precedent:
# every parser-family satellite that mirrors a sibling's status
# constants). The forward-path landings (M9.1 kernel-side emitter
# wire-up via weak-import function-pointer slots; M9.2 Unicode box-
# drawing; M9.3 schema-driven formatting; M9.4 per-command renderer
# override; M9.5 TR#9 bidi; M9.6 column-width heuristics; M9.7
# smoke-mode fingerprint goldens) remain opaque-pointer by design;
# only M9.3 adds a one-way intra-library linker reference against
# R222.M1 SchemasSig for schema-tag discrimination.
#
# R229.M9 label prefix `rd_` is DISJOINT from every landed parser-
# tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_,
# rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_,
# hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_,
# rc_) and from the paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if,
# let, fn, ret, done).


# ---- Error band 0x5C00..0x5CFF (R227.M4, load_check) -----------------
#
# Type-check-at-load pipeline packed-status range. Nine slots used at
# R227.M4 (five active + four reserved for M4.1+):
#
#   LC_OK                        = 0        script passes typecheck
#   LC_ERR_TYPECHECK_FAILED      = 0x5C00   at least one binding failed
#                                           the pipeline; diag chain
#                                           carries one 128-B record
#                                           per failure.
#   LC_ERR_BAD_SCRIPT            = 0x5C01   script_body == 0 OR
#                                           body_len == 0.
#   LC_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL         = 0x5C02   out_diag_chain == 0 OR
#                                           out_cap < 128.
#   LC_ERR_TOO_MANY_BINDINGS     = 0x5C03   top-level let-count >
#                                           LC_MAX_BINDINGS (64).
#   LC_ERR_BAD_SCHEME            = 0x5C04   synthetic: binding failed
#                                           one of three syntactic
#                                           preconditions (span contains
#                                           '='; local brace/paren
#                                           balance; RHS non-empty).
#                                           Emitted as a diag record's
#                                           `code` field (not returned
#                                           at the entry).
#   LC_ERR_RANK_VIOLATION        = 0x5C05   reserved for M4.1 pass-
#                                           through from R225.M6
#                                           RankCheck (code preserved
#                                           in the diag record's
#                                           `code` field).
#   LC_ERR_PARSE_FAILED          = 0x5C06   reserved for M4.1 R221.M5
#                                           Ast wire-up (top-level
#                                           parser faulted).
#   LC_ERR_HM_FAILED             = 0x5C07   reserved for M4.1 R225.M1
#                                           HmCore wire-up (unify /
#                                           instantiate / generalise
#                                           faulted).
#   LC_ERR_IMPORT_FAILED         = 0x5C08   reserved for M4.2 R227.M3
#                                           ImportResolve wire-up
#                                           (cycle / resolve-fault
#                                           before top-level walk).
#
# Slots 0x5C09..0x5CFF reserved for M4.1+ follow-ups (binding-level
# cycle detect; synthetic-span discipline; LSP-hover attach;
# per-chain fingerprint; per-binding DiagReport chaining audit).
#
# The 0x5C00..0x5CFF range is reserved for type-check-at-load packed-
# status codes across R227.M4+. R225.M6 (rank_check) owns the disjoint
# 0x5800..0x58FF slice; R227.M3 (import_resolve) owns 0x5900..0x59FF;
# R225.M7 (diag_report) owns 0x5B00..0x5BFF (sibling parallel arm,
# landed Wave 129). R227.M4 claims 0x5C so the three parallel Wave
# 129 arms land in disjoint pre-reserved slots.
#
# The 0x5C00..0x5CFF carve-out is adjacent to the 0x5B00..0x5BFF
# R225.M7 DiagReport band (landed Wave 129) and below the
# 0x5D00..0x5DFF R229.M9 Renderer band (landed in the same wave).
# A downstream compound-error dispatcher can discriminate "the
# typecheck-at-load gate rejected" from every other satellite's
# failure modes by a single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x5C` idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R227.M4 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x5C[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> hits only in src/load_check.pdx, caps.decl, STATUS.md,
#        CHANGELOG.md, and design/shell/typecheck-at-load.md.
#   grep -rE '0x5C(0[0-8])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + load_check.pdx +
#        design/shell/typecheck-at-load.md.
#
# ---- R227.M4 catalogue paragraph (load_check) ------------------------
#
#   R227.M4 LoadCheck (load_check.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      TYPE-CHECK-AT-LOAD PIPELINE SPINE. Composes R221.M5 Ast,
#      R225.M1 HmCore, R225.M6 RankCheck, and R225.M7 DiagReport
#      (sibling arm, landed Wave 129) into a single orchestrator
#      entry the kernel-side
#      R133.M5 TypecheckGate (`_pds_tcg_gate`) invokes through its
#      registered user-space checker fn-ptr slot. One entry per
#      load: `_lc_check_script(script_body, body_len, out_diag_chain,
#      out_cap) -> status`. The orchestrator walks top-level `let`
#      bindings via ASCII heuristic (brace/paren depth tracked outer-
#      level; keyword at a line/semicolon boundary with WS after),
#      validates three syntactic preconditions per binding (span
#      contains '='; local brace/paren balance returns to 0; RHS has
#      at least one non-WS byte before ';'), calls `_rc_check_scheme`
#      on a canonical rank-1 scheme per binding (`∀. u64`; 24 bytes
#      resident in `_lc_canon_scheme`), and emits 128-B DiagReport-
#      shaped records on failure chained via the `next_offset` field
#      at [+104]. Three public entry points over singleton `.bss`
#      (48 B total: 24 B canonical scheme + three u64 scalars):
#      `_lc_init()` -- pure-leaf defensive scrub of the three u64
#        scalars (`_lc_binding_count`, `_lc_last_fail_code`,
#        `_lc_diag_emit_count`) PLUS write of the 24-B canonical
#        scheme into `_lc_canon_scheme`. `.bss` zero-init at cold
#        boot satisfies the scalar invariant; the canonical-scheme
#        bytes (kind=2 at +8; n_body=1 at +4; con_id=1 at +12) are
#        NOT zero and are written explicitly. Idempotent.
#      `_lc_check_script(rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx) -> status` -- the ONE
#        entry per load. 6-push callee-save frame + sub rsp 40
#        (five u64 scratch slots: outer brace_depth @ +0, outer
#        paren_depth @ +8, binding_count @ +16, current binding
#        src_offset @ +24, current binding src_length @ +32),
#        giving rsp%16==0 at every nested CALL (entry rsp%16==8;
#        48 B pushes + 40 B scratch = 88 B; (8+88) mod 16 = 0).
#        Nested CALL: `_rc_check_scheme` (R225.M6; intra-library)
#        once per top-level `let` binding found. Phases: (0) input
#        validation; (1) lazy-populate canonical scheme iff kind
#        byte at [+8] == 0 (bss sentinel); (2) initialise scratch;
#        (3) outer walk with brace/paren clamp-at-0 + boundary-
#        aware `let` detection; (4) inner-scan each binding to next
#        top-level ';' or EOF; (5) emit diag on syntactic failure
#        OR on non-OK RankCheck status; (6) patch last diag's
#        next_offset to 0; (7) decide LC_OK / LC_ERR_TYPECHECK_FAILED
#        / LC_ERR_TOO_MANY_BINDINGS. Capacity check per emit: skip
#        if `r15 + 128 > r13` but still count binding. Returns
#        LC_OK (0) iff no diag was emitted; LC_ERR_TYPECHECK_FAILED
#        (0x5C00) iff at least one diag was emitted.
#      `_lc_count_diags(rdi, rsi) -> u64` -- pure leaf. Follows the
#        chain by walking `next_offset` at +104 of each 128-B
#        record; terminates on next_offset == 0, out-of-range
#        offset, bad magic, or 64 iterations (cycle guard bounded
#        at LC_MAX_BINDINGS). Returns count of valid DiagReport
#        records.
#      Twenty pub constants beyond the standard status taxonomy:
#      LC_DIAG_BYTES = 128 (stride power of two for `shl rN, 7`
#      addressing without 2-op `imul r, imm`); LC_DIAG_MAGIC =
#      0x47444C4C 'LCDG' LE; LC_DIAG_OFF_MAGIC = 0, LC_DIAG_OFF_CODE
#      = 4, LC_DIAG_OFF_SRC_OFFSET = 8, LC_DIAG_OFF_SRC_LENGTH = 12,
#      LC_DIAG_OFF_SEVERITY = 16, LC_DIAG_OFF_BINDING_IDX = 20,
#      LC_DIAG_OFF_NAME = 24, LC_DIAG_OFF_MESSAGE = 40,
#      LC_DIAG_OFF_NEXT = 104; LC_SEV_INFO = 0 .. LC_SEV_FATAL = 3;
#      LC_MAX_BINDINGS = 64; LC_CANON_SCHEME_BYTES = 24. The LC_DIAG
#      record (magic 'LCDG') is a LoadCheck-local 128-B record with
#      LoadCheck-specific fields (binding_idx, chain via next_offset
#      at [+104]); it is DISTINCT from R225.M7's DiagReport record
#      (magic 'DIAG' = 0x47414944; next_offset at [+120]). The two
#      records serve different tiers of the diagnostic pipeline
#      (LoadCheck emits binding-level load-time failures; DiagReport
#      emits elaborator-tier type errors). An M4.1+ landing may
#      embed a DR record inside LC_DIAG's `message_inline` field as
#      a secondary payload. Labels prefixed
#      `lc_` with per-function suffix (`lc_ini_`, `lc_cs_`, `lc_cd_`).
#      Grep-confirmed disjoint from every landed parser-tier label
#      prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_, sch_sig_,
#      cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_, rp_,
#      fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_, hm_,
#      rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_, rd_)
#      and from the kernel-side `tcg_` prefix (R133.M5
#      typecheck_gate.pdx) so a future linker-level audit does not
#      confuse the two halves of the typecheck-at-load pipeline. Also
#      disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if,
#      let, fn, ret, done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The type-check-at-load orchestrator runs
# purely in user space over the caller-supplied script byte stream +
# caller-owned out_diag_chain buffer; no KIND surface is touched at
# raw M4. The forward-path landings (M4.1 HmCore wire-up; M4.2
# ImportResolve wire-up; M4.3 AST pretty-print; M4.4 kernel-side
# `_pds_tcg_register_checker` registration shim via a cross-process
# fn-ptr registration primitive that itself has no KIND surface;
# M4.5 `pdsc verify` driver) all stay in-process at the parser tier
# and introduce NO new KIND surface. The M4.4 kernel-side shim fires
# through the kernel's existing `_pds_tcg_register_checker` entry
# which belongs to the R133.M5 TypecheckGate band, not to this
# module's packed-status range.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R227.M4 (load_check.pdx) has ONE intra-library
# linker-visible FUNCTION dependency at raw M4: `_rc_check_scheme`
# (R225.M6 RankCheck; `src/rank_check.pdx`), called once per top-
# level `let` binding found. NO external cross-library linker
# dependency. NO intra-library DATA linker reference (the module
# only reads caller-supplied pointers + its own 48-byte `.bss`
# singleton). RankCheck's error band 0x5800..0x58FF carves no slot
# in the 0x5C00..0x5CFF LoadCheck band; the pass-through happens
# through the diag record's `code` field (a u32 copy of RankCheck's
# raw status) rather than mapping into LoadCheck's status taxonomy.
# The forward-path landings (M4.1 R225.M1 HmCore wire-up at
# `_hm_generalise` + `_hm_instantiate`; M4.2 R227.M3 ImportResolve
# wire-up at `_ir_build_graph` + `_ir_detect_cycle`; M4.3 R221.M5
# Ast wire-up at `_ast_alloc` + `_ast_render`; M4.4 kernel-side
# `_pds_tcg_register_checker` via the R133.M5 fn-ptr slot; M4.5
# `pdsc verify` driver; M4.6 FNV-1a 64 hash of binding name into
# diag reserved u64; M4.7 rdtsc timestamp into diag reserved u64)
# remain opaque-pointer by design; all four M4.1+ intra-library
# function references are weak-import function-pointer slots that
# only fire when the referenced primitive is linked in.
#
# R227.M4 label prefix `lc_` is DISJOINT from every landed parser-
# tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_,
# rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_,
# hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_,
# rc_, rd_) and from the kernel-side `tcg_` prefix (R133.M5
# typecheck_gate.pdx). Also disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-
# keyword set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).


# ---- Error band 0x5E00..0x5EFF (R225.M8, lsp_hover) ------------------
#
# LSP-embed hover-query packed-status range. Seven slots used at
# R225.M8:
#
#   LH_OK                       : 0        query / render success.
#   LH_ERR_BAD_INPUT            : 0x5E00   `_lh_query` saw
#                                          `buffer_ptr == 0` or
#                                          `out == 0`; or
#                                          `_lh_render_lsp_json` saw
#                                          a null record / out pointer.
#   LH_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL        : 0x5E01   `_lh_query` saw
#                                          `out_cap < LH_RECORD_BYTES =
#                                          128`.
#   LH_ERR_CURSOR_OOB           : 0x5E02   `_lh_query` saw
#                                          `cursor_byte > buffer_len`.
#   LH_ERR_NO_TOKEN             : 0x5E03   cursor sits on a non-
#                                          identifier byte with no
#                                          identifier byte adjacent.
#                                          Record still well-formed
#                                          (magic stamped, all other
#                                          fields zero / NUL); caller
#                                          may skip the LSP response
#                                          round-trip.
#   LH_ERR_UNKNOWN_TOKEN        : 0x5E04   reserved for a future miss-
#                                          distinction axis (demo-table
#                                          miss returns LH_OK with
#                                          cleared type/effect/cap
#                                          flags; this slot carves
#                                          room for M8.1's live-
#                                          elaborator "token-known-
#                                          but-no-type" case).
#   LH_ERR_BAD_RECORD           : 0x5E05   render saw `record_len <
#                                          128` or a magic-field
#                                          mismatch (not 'HOVR' =
#                                          0x52564F48 LE).
#   LH_ERR_RENDER_OVERFLOW      : 0x5E06   render output exceeded
#                                          `out_cap`; the sticky
#                                          overflow sentinel on any
#                                          internal emit helper
#                                          propagated to the final
#                                          cursor check.
#
# The 0x5E00..0x5EFF range is reserved for LSP-hover packed-status
# codes across R225.M8+. Disjoint from every other R225 submodule's
# band (R225.M1 HmCore 0x2D00..0x2DFF; R225.M2 RowTypes
# 0x3600..0x36FF; R225.M4 EffectUnify 0x4900..0x49FF; R225.M6
# RankCheck 0x5800..0x58FF; R225.M7 DiagReport 0x5B00..0x5BFF)
# because a downstream dispatcher's compound error path must
# distinguish "hover query / render refused" from the four landed
# typechecker-tier failure modes. Slots 0x5E07..0x5EFF reserved for
# M8.1+ follow-ups (M8.1 elaborator-wire-up status; M8.2 queue-full;
# M8.3 line/col overflow; M8.4 TypeEnv-stash-miss).
#
# The 0x5E00..0x5EFF carve-out sits adjacent to the 0x5D00..0x5DFF
# R229.M9 Renderer band -- the next kilo-step boundary past Wave 130
# consolidation. A downstream compound-error dispatcher can
# discriminate "hover refused" from every other satellite's failure
# modes by a single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x5E` idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R225.M8 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x5E[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> no prior packed-status hits in this library.
#   grep -rE '0x5E(0[0-6])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + lsp_hover.pdx +
#        design/shell/lsp-hover.md.
#
# ---- R225.M8 catalogue paragraph (lsp_hover) -------------------------
#
#   R225.M8 LspHover (lsp_hover.pdx, landed): pure in-process LSP-
#      embed hover-query surface. Resolves a cursor byte offset into
#      a 128-byte HoverResponse record carrying the three summary
#      rows a user hovering a token in the line editor cares about:
#      the inferred (type, effect-row, capability-row) triple layered
#      on R225.M1 HmCore / R224.M3 EffectRow / R224.M4 CapRow via a
#      disjoint 0x5E00..0x5EFF carve-out. Preserves the Q-A5 source-
#      span invariant: token_byte_offset / token_byte_length index
#      into the caller's SOURCE buffer, not the NFC-form
#      intermediate. Three public entry points over caller-supplied
#      buffers:
#      `_lh_init()` -- bare `ret`. M8 holds NO .bss state (every
#        HoverResponse record is caller-owned; the session-local
#        hover-queue head slot lands at M8.2). Kept for parallelism
#        with the parser-family init-hook convention.
#      `_lh_query(buffer_ptr, buffer_len, cursor_byte, out, out_cap)
#        -> packed_u64` -- resolve the token at `cursor_byte` into a
#        128-byte HoverResponse record at `out`. Validates
#        buffer_ptr != 0 and out != 0 (BAD_INPUT 0x5E00); out_cap >=
#        128 (OUT_TOO_SMALL 0x5E01); cursor_byte <= buffer_len
#        (CURSOR_OOB 0x5E02). Zero-fills 128 bytes before populating.
#        Writes magic 'HOVR' = 0x52564F48 LE u32 at [+0]. Calls
#        `_lh_h_scan_token` (identifier-char grow-left-then-right)
#        and `_lh_h_table_lookup` (10-entry demo-table scan;
#        on hit byte-copies 96 bytes from entry+16 to record+16).
#        Writes token_byte_offset u32 at [+4]; token_byte_length u32
#        at [+8]; flags u32 at [+12] (bit 0 TYPE_KNOWN, bit 1
#        EFFECT_KNOWN, bit 2 CAP_KNOWN, bit 3 IS_IDENT). Returns
#        packed (128 << 32) | LH_OK or LH_ERR_NO_TOKEN on cursor-on-
#        non-identifier. 6-push callee-save frame
#        (rbx/rbp/r12/r13/r14/r15 = 48B) + sub rsp,8 align; entry
#        rsp%16==8 -> after pushes+sub rsp%16==0 at every nested
#        CALL. Token-length zero-extend uses `shl r, 32; shr r, 32`
#        rather than `and r, 0xFFFFFFFF` whose imm32 encoding would
#        sign-extend because bit 31 is set.
#      `_lh_render_lsp_json(record_ptr, record_len, out, out_cap)
#        -> packed_u64` -- serialize to a one-line LSP `Hover`
#        payload: {"contents":{"kind":"markdown","value":"**type**:
#        <t>\n**effects**: <e>\n**capabilities**: <c>"},"range":
#        {"start":{"line":0,"character":<n>},"end":{"line":0,
#        "character":<n>}}}. Effect / capability lines omitted when
#        the respective 32-byte inline string's first byte is NUL
#        (demo-table miss or caller-reset). `line` emitted as 0 at
#        raw M8 (byte-offset fallback per the LSP range `character`
#        field); M8.3 wires the UTF-8-aware byte-offset -> line/
#        column resolver. 6-push callee-save frame; same alignment
#        discipline as `_lh_query`.
#      Three module-private pure-leaf emit helpers mirror
#      DiagReport's shape (deliberate disjoint helper surface so
#      each module ships standalone):
#      `_lh_h_emit_lit` (bounded byte-copy with sticky-overflow
#        sentinel out_cap+1), `_lh_h_emit_cstr` (byte-copy up to
#        max_bytes or first NUL), `_lh_h_emit_u32_dec` (variable
#        1..10 decimal digits via greedy subtraction over the 10-
#        entry _lh_pow10 table in .rodata). Three additional helpers
#        drive the token-boundary scan + table lookup:
#      `_lh_h_is_ident` (pure leaf; 1 iff byte in [a-zA-Z0-9_-]),
#      `_lh_h_scan_token` (grow-left-then-right over the identifier
#        charset; 4-push + sub rsp,8 frame to hold scan state across
#        two nested CALLs into `_lh_h_is_ident`),
#      `_lh_h_table_lookup` (10-entry 128-byte-stride demo-table
#        scan; pure leaf; byte-copies 96 bytes from entry+16 to
#        record+16 on hit; returns flag triple on hit, 0 on miss).
#      Record wire format (128 bytes exactly; stride power-of-two
#      for `shl rN, 7` addressing): magic u32 (0x52564F48 'HOVR' LE)
#      at [+0]; token_byte_offset u32 at [+4]; token_byte_length u32
#      at [+8]; flags u32 at [+12]; type_name_inline u8[32] at [+16];
#      effect_row_inline u8[32] at [+48]; cap_row_inline u8[32] at
#      [+80]; reserved u64 at [+112]; next_hover_ref u64 at [+120].
#      NUL-pad convention for the 32-byte strings (render stops at
#      first NUL; a field with first byte NUL contributes nothing
#      to the rendered output). Record addressing power-of-two
#      stride matches R225.M7 DiagReport's record discipline (both
#      shift-7-addressable).
#      Demo-table layout: 10 entries x 128 bytes = 1280 bytes in
#      .rodata. Each entry: name u8[16] at [+0]; type u8[32] at
#      [+16]; effect u8[32] at [+48]; cap u8[32] at [+80]; reserved
#      u8[16] at [+112]. Entry [+16..+112] (96 bytes) matches the
#      HoverResponse record's [+16..+112] layout so the hit path
#      byte-copies 96 bytes in one count loop. Ten entries cover
#      four base-type identifiers (x -> u64; s -> str; p -> path;
#      pid -> pid), four shell builtins with non-empty effect and
#      capability rows (echo -> str->str !{io} @{tty}; cat ->
#      path->str !{io,fs} @{fs,tty}; ls -> path->[path] !{fs}
#      @{fs,tty}; ps -> ()->[pid] !{proc} @{proc}), a capability-
#      dependent nullary (pwd -> ()->path !{fs} @{fs}), and a
#      higher-order combinator (map -> (a->b)->[a]->[b]).
#      All status-code immediates (0x5E00..0x5E06) fit imm32 with
#      bit 31 clear so direct `cmp reg, imm32` and `mov reg, imm32`
#      are sign-extension-safe. Magic 0x52564F48 likewise has bit 31
#      clear (0x52 = 0101_0010; high bit of byte 3 is 0). Labels
#      prefixed `lh_` for public entries and `lh_h_` for the six
#      helpers (grep-confirmed unique against every landed parser-
#      tier prefix: u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_, sch_sig_,
#      cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_, rp_,
#      fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_, hm_,
#      rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_,
#      rd_, dr_, lc_). Also disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-
#      keyword set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Hover queries run purely in user space over
# caller-supplied buffer / out buffers; no KIND surface is touched
# by this module. R225.M8.6 (kernel-side LSP-transport wire-up at
# R220.M9) consumes the rendered byte strings via the LSP transport
# socket but does not require THIS module to touch a KIND.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R225.M8 (lsp_hover.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module
# linker dependency at raw M8. All three public entry points touch
# ONLY caller-supplied pointer args and the module's own .rodata
# tables (`_lh_pow10`, `_lh_demo_table`, and the seven literal
# templates); the module carries NO .bss state at raw M8. The
# forward-path landings remain opaque-pointer by design -- only
# M8.1 adds one-way intra-library linker references against R225.M1
# HmCore (`_hm_instantiate` / `_hm_generalise` for the live scheme
# query), R224.M3 EffectRow (effect-row render), R224.M4 CapRow
# (cap-row render), and R221.M5 Ast (`_ast_find_by_span` for the
# cursor-byte -> AST-node resolve). M8.3 adds an intra-library
# linker reference against R221.M2 GraphemeIter (`_gi_next_cluster`
# / `_gi_prev_cluster`) for TR#31 identifier scanning over NFC-
# normalised bytes.


# ---- Error band 0x5F00..0x5FFF (R227.M5, module_functor) -------------
#
# Functor-typed script-module packed-status range for the R227 pds
# container / loader wave's FIFTH milestone (parser-tier functor
# elaboration + application for `.pds` script modules). Ten slots used
# at R227.M5:
#
#   MF_OK                       : 0        operation succeeded.
#   MF_ERR_BAD_SCRIPT           : 0x5F00   `_mf_parse_module`:
#                                          script_body == 0 OR
#                                          body_len == 0 OR body_len >
#                                          MF_MAX_BODY_BYTES (65536).
#   MF_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL        : 0x5F01   `_mf_parse_module`: out ==
#                                          0 OR out_cap <
#                                          MF_RECORD_MAX_BYTES (1152);
#                                          `_mf_apply`: out == 0 OR
#                                          out_cap < 1152;
#                                          `_mf_lookup_export`:
#                                          out_cmd_sig_ref == 0.
#   MF_ERR_BAD_RECORD           : 0x5F02   `_mf_apply` /
#                                          `_mf_lookup_export`:
#                                          functor_record == 0 OR
#                                          magic (u32 @ [+0]) !=
#                                          MF_RECORD_MAGIC 0x5443464D
#                                          ('MFCT' LE).
#   MF_ERR_TOO_MANY_EXPORTS     : 0x5F03   `_mf_parse_module`:
#                                          n_exports > MF_MAX_EXPORTS
#                                          (32).
#   MF_ERR_TOO_MANY_CAP_PARAMS  : 0x5F04   `_mf_parse_module`:
#                                          n_cap_params >
#                                          MF_MAX_CAP_PARAMS (8).
#   MF_ERR_NAME_TOO_LONG        : 0x5F05   `_mf_parse_module`: export
#                                          name > 16 bytes OR cap-
#                                          param name > 12 bytes;
#                                          `_mf_lookup_export`: name_len
#                                          > 16.
#   MF_ERR_UNKNOWN_CAP          : 0x5F06   `_mf_parse_module`:
#                                          `#capability` name not among
#                                          the ten CapRow primitive
#                                          labels at raw M5 (slots 0..9).
#                                          M5.1 widens to the full 32-
#                                          slot CapRow label table via
#                                          intra-library call to R224.M4
#                                          `_cr_label_lookup`.
#   MF_ERR_SYNTAX               : 0x5F07   `_mf_parse_module`:
#                                          unterminated string / bad
#                                          byte in cap name / `pub`
#                                          not followed by `let`.
#   E1200_MISSING_CAP           : 0x5F10   `_mf_apply`: cap_env_mask
#                                          lacks a required bit set in
#                                          cap_param_mask. The offending
#                                          bit index (0..63 at raw M5)
#                                          is packed into bits [63:32]
#                                          of the packed return. The
#                                          E12xx diagnostic family for
#                                          functor application faults.
#   E1201_EXPORT_NOT_FOUND      : 0x5F11   `_mf_lookup_export`: name
#                                          not in export table.
#                                          *out_cmd_sig_ref is zeroed
#                                          defensively before any work
#                                          and remains zero on miss.
#
# Slots 0x5F08..0x5F0F reserved for R227.M5.1+ parse diagnostics
# (nested quote inside cap name, non-ASCII cap-name byte, module-scope
# `let` vs `pub let` discrimination, cap-param limit per-slot vs per-
# directive). Slots 0x5F12..0x5FFF reserved for R227.M5.2+ apply /
# lookup diagnostics (cap-arity mismatch when the apply mask carries
# bits NOT in the param mask, applied-twice refusal, export shadowing
# when multiple functors are composed through a chained lookup, cmd-
# sig-ref resolve fault when the CmdRegistry wire-up lands).
#
# The 0x5F00..0x5FFF range is reserved for module-functor packed-
# status codes across R227.M5+. R227.M3 (import_resolve) owns the
# disjoint 0x5900..0x59FF slice; R227.M4 (load_check) owns
# 0x5C00..0x5CFF. R227.M5 claims 0x5F so the three R227 parser-tier
# satellites land in disjoint pre-reserved slots.
#
# The 0x5F00..0x5FFF carve-out sits adjacent to the 0x5E00..0x5EFF
# R225.M8 LspHover band (landed Wave 131; parallel-arm slot) and is
# the current terminal kilo-step past the R227.M4 / R229.M9 / R229.M10
# consolidation. A downstream compound-error dispatcher can
# discriminate "the module-functor substrate faulted" from every other
# satellite's failure modes by a single `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x5F` idiom.
#
# Grep confirmation at R227.M5 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x5F[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> hits only in src/module_functor.pdx, caps.decl, STATUS.md,
#        CHANGELOG.md, and design/shell/module-as-functor.md (plus
#        the pre-existing ASCII literals 0x5F '_' in cap_row.pdx /
#        hostname_annot.pdx / import_resolve.pdx / renderer.pdx /
#        filter_op.pdx / datalog.pdx which are byte-class checks, NOT
#        packed-status codes; the pattern 0x5F0[0-7] / 0x5F1[01] is
#        strict against these).
#   grep -rE '0x5F(0[0-7]|1[0-1])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + module_functor.pdx +
#        design/shell/module-as-functor.md.
#
# ---- R227.M5 catalogue paragraph (module_functor) --------------------
#
#   R227.M5 ModuleFunctor (module_functor.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      FUNCTOR ELABORATION + APPLICATION for `.pds` script modules.
#      A `.pds` script elaborates to an ML-style module functor whose
#      PARAMETERS are the `#capability "<name>"` directives referenced
#      in the script and whose BODY is a packed table of exported
#      `pub let`-bound names. Importing the module APPLIES the functor
#      against the importer's cap_env_mask snapshot: a single missing
#      bit refuses the whole import via E1200_MISSING_CAP (0x5F10)
#      with the offending bit index packed in the high 32 of the
#      return. Four public entry points over caller-supplied buffers;
#      ZERO `.bss` state at raw M5:
#      `_mf_init()` -- bare `ret`. ModuleFunctor holds NO .bss state
#        at raw M5 (every record is caller-owned on all operational
#        paths). Kept for parallelism with the parser-family init-
#        hook convention (`_ir_init` R227.M3, `_lc_init` R227.M4,
#        `_lh_init` R225.M8, `_dr_init` R225.M7, `_rc_init` R225.M6,
#        `_st_init` R223.M7, `_ha_init` R223.M6, `_ws_init` R223.M5,
#        `_ds_init` R222.M8).
#      `_mf_parse_module(script_body, body_len, out, out_cap)
#        -> packed_u64` -- scan `.pds` script body byte-by-byte,
#        dispatching on '#' (match `#capability "<name>"`) and 'p'
#        (match `pub let NAME`). Emits a 128-B ModuleFunctor header
#        (magic 'MFCT' = 0x5443464D LE u32 @ 0, n_exports u16 @ 4,
#        n_cap_params u16 @ 6, module_path_hash u64 @ 8, module_name_
#        inline [u8;16] @ 16, cap_param_mask [u8;32] @ 32, exports_
#        offset u64 @ 64, entries_offset u64 @ 72, applied_flag u8 @
#        80, applied_env_mask u64 @ 88) followed by n_exports x 32-B
#        export entries (name_hash u64 @ 0, name_inline [u8;16] @ 8,
#        cmd_sig_ref u64 @ 24; cmd_sig_ref = 0 at raw M5, forward-
#        path M5.2 wires in R222.M5 CmdRegistry). 6-push PURE LEAF
#        with FNV-1a 64 and primitive-cap-table scan inlined; no
#        nested CALL so no sub-rsp pad is needed. r8 holds the
#        running cap_mask low u64; r11/r9/r10 are three-term
#        precompute scratch. Primitive-cap-table scan is a byte-
#        identical in-module duplicate of R224.M4 CapRow's
#        `_cr_primitive_init` (10 slots x 16 bytes); M5.1 switches to
#        a direct call to `_cr_label_lookup` which widens recognition
#        to slots 10..31 (user-declared caps). Status: MF_OK (0),
#        MF_ERR_BAD_SCRIPT (0x5F00), MF_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL (0x5F01),
#        MF_ERR_TOO_MANY_EXPORTS (0x5F03), MF_ERR_TOO_MANY_CAP_PARAMS
#        (0x5F04), MF_ERR_NAME_TOO_LONG (0x5F05), MF_ERR_UNKNOWN_CAP
#        (0x5F06), MF_ERR_SYNTAX (0x5F07). Return packed
#        (record_bytes << 32) | status with record_bytes = 128 +
#        n_exports*32.
#      `_mf_apply(functor_record, cap_env_mask, out, out_cap)
#        -> packed_u64` -- the IMPORT-TIME application: verify magic
#        match (MF_ERR_BAD_RECORD 0x5F02 on mismatch); compute
#        missing = cap_param_mask low u64 AND NOT cap_env_mask via
#        register-register AND + one-op NOT (precedent cap_row.pdx
#        L1208 for reg-reg AND; battery_monitor.pdx L454 for NOT);
#        on missing != 0 scan for lowest-set bit via TEST+SHR 64-
#        iteration bounded loop and return (idx << 32) |
#        E1200_MISSING_CAP (0x5F10); on OK byte-copy record_bytes
#        from functor to out via u64-stride-8 loop, set applied_flag
#        u8 = 1 at [+80] and applied_env_mask u64 at [+88] from
#        cap_env_mask. 5-push PURE LEAF; no nested CALL. Status:
#        MF_OK (0), MF_ERR_BAD_RECORD (0x5F02), MF_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL
#        (0x5F01), E1200_MISSING_CAP (0x5F10).
#      `_mf_lookup_export(functor_record, name_ptr, name_len,
#        out_cmd_sig_ref) -> status` -- the DISPATCH-TIME surface:
#        FNV-1a 64 hash the input name; linear-scan the export
#        table starting at functor + exports_offset; compare each
#        entry's name_hash u64 against target_hash; on hit write
#        entry's cmd_sig_ref u64 to *out_cmd_sig_ref and return
#        MF_OK; on miss *out_cmd_sig_ref stays zero (defensive
#        pre-work write) and return E1201_EXPORT_NOT_FOUND
#        (0x5F11). 6-push PURE LEAF (FNV-1a 64 inlined); no nested
#        CALL. Status: MF_OK (0), MF_ERR_BAD_RECORD (0x5F02),
#        MF_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL (0x5F01), MF_ERR_NAME_TOO_LONG
#        (0x5F05), E1201_EXPORT_NOT_FOUND (0x5F11).
#      All four entry points are PURE LEAVES (zero nested CALL at
#      raw M5) so r11 is used freely as the three-term address-
#      precompute scratch. One pub `.rodata` table:
#      `_mf_primitive_init : [u8; 160] @align(8)` byte-identical to
#      R224.M4 CapRow's `_cr_primitive_init` (ten CapRow primitive
#      labels: read / write / exec / net / fs / cap_mint / cap_grant
#      / kalloc / ipc / trap, slot ordinals 0..9).
#      Record wire format: 128 B header (magic 'MFCT' + fields above)
#      + up to 32 x 32 B export entries; stride power-of-two for
#      `shl rN, 7` (header) and `shl rN, 5` (export). MF_RECORD_MAX_
#      BYTES = 1152 = 128 + 32*32. Encoder discipline (feedback_pdx_
#      encoder_pitfalls): cmp rN,0 never test; strides via shl (header
#      shl 7, export shl 5, primitive-cap-table shl 4) with no 2-op
#      imul-r-imm; cap-mask check via register-register AND (imm-free)
#      + one-op NOT + imm8 OR (`or r8, (1 << slot_i)` via `shl r9, cl`);
#      FNV constants 0xCBF29CE484222325 (offset basis) and 0x100000001B3
#      (prime) load via MOVABS (precedent history.pdx L727-728);
#      magic 0x5443464D fits imm32 with bit 31 clear; all byte literals
#      (0x09, 0x0A, 0x0D, 0x20, 0x22, 0x23, 0x30..0x39, 0x41..0x5A,
#      0x5F, 0x61..0x7A, directive bytes 'c','a','p','b','i','l','t',
#      'y','u','e') and status codes (0x5F00..0x5F11) fit imm32 with
#      bit 31 clear. Byte loads via `xor rN, rN; mov_b rN, [base +
#      idx * 1]`; byte stores via `mov_b [dst + disp], reg` or three-
#      term precompute. u16 stores via `mov_w [dst + disp], reg`; u32
#      stores via `mov_d [dst + disp], reg`; u64 stores via plain
#      `mov [dst + disp], reg`. No multiline let; no three-term
#      addressing. Labels prefixed `mf_` with per-function suffix
#      (`mf_ini_` init, `mf_pm_` parse-module, `mf_ap_` apply,
#      `mf_lk_` lookup-export). Grep-confirmed disjoint from every
#      landed parser-tier label prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_,
#      sm_, fh_, sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_,
#      pl_, pl_lam_, rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_,
#      cr_, ls_, lp_, hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_,
#      vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_) and from the
#      kernel-side `ig_` prefix (R133.M3 import_graph.pdx) + the
#      kernel-side `tcg_` prefix (R133.M5 typecheck_gate.pdx). Also
#      disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if,
#      let, fn, ret, done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The module-functor orchestrator runs purely
# in user space over the caller-supplied script byte stream + caller-
# owned out buffer; no KIND surface is touched at raw M5. The forward-
# path landings (M5.1 CapRow wire-up; M5.2 CmdRegistry wire-up; M5.3
# module path/name population from ImportDirective; M5.4 import-time
# apply dispatch composed with ImportResolve; M5.5 256-bit cap mask
# widening; M5.6 nested-lambda-aware export walker; M5.7 duplicate-
# export scan; M5.8 unused-cap-param lint) all stay in-process at the
# parser tier and introduce NO new KIND surface. The M5.4 kernel-side
# shim fires through the kernel's existing R133.M3 ImportGraph +
# R133.M2 CapsDeclCheck surfaces which belong to the kernel's own
# packed-status bands, not to this module's range.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R227.M5 (module_functor.pdx) has NO intra-library
# linker-visible FUNCTION dependency at raw M5. All four public entry
# points are PURE LEAVES over caller-supplied byte buffers + this
# module's own `.rodata` (`_mf_primitive_init` 160-byte primitive-cap
# name table). NO external cross-library linker dependency. NO intra-
# library DATA linker reference at raw M5. The forward-path intra-
# library dep set is DECLARED here for the M5.1+ landings and will be
# wired through weak-import function-pointer slots so each dep only
# fires when the referenced primitive is linked in:
#   M5.1 -> R224.M4 CapRow `_cr_label_lookup` (replaces inline
#     primitive-table scan; widens to slots 10..31).
#   M5.2 -> R222.M5 CmdRegistry `_cmdreg_lookup` (populates
#     cmd_sig_ref per export).
#   M5.3 -> R227.M3 ImportResolve ImportDirective field pass-through
#     (populates module_path_hash + module_name_inline from the
#     enclosing #import directive).
#   M5.4 -> R227.M3 ImportResolve `_ir_resolve_path` wire-up for
#     import-time apply (composes with `_mf_apply` at resolve time
#     so the caller's cap_env_mask gates every imported export).
#   M5.6 -> R224.M5 LetScope `_ls_resolve` (nested-lambda-aware
#     export walker).
#   M5.7 -> R227.M3 ImportResolve-shaped duplicate-name scan over
#     the export table (analogous to `_ir_build_graph`'s intra-
#     source duplicate-name scan).
# The export-table byte layout agrees with R222.M3 FunctorInstance's
# cmd_sig_ref consumer (cmd_sig_ref u64 @ [+24] in each 32-B export
# entry); the dispatch pipeline consumer calls
# `_mf_lookup_export(applied, name_ptr, name_len, &sig_ref)` then
# hands `sig_ref` to `_fi_instantiate` without touching this module's
# `.rodata` or any register state owned by it.
#
# ---- Error band 0x6000..0x60FF (R229.M10, turn_fingerprint) ----------
#
# Per-turn wire-fingerprint packed-status range. Ten slots used at
# R229.M10:
#
#   TF_OK                     = 0        success (64 bytes written on
#                                        _tf_emit; packed bytes_written
#                                        on _tf_render_tag; raw TF_OK
#                                        on _tf_correlate match).
#   TF_ERR_BAD_OUT            = 0x6000   out == 0 on any entry.
#   TF_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL      = 0x6001   out_cap below required width
#                                        (64 for _tf_emit;
#                                        16 + inner_len for
#                                        _tf_render_tag).
#   TF_ERR_BAD_RESULT         = 0x6002   _tf_emit: result_len > 0 AND
#                                        result_ptr == 0.
#   TF_ERR_RESULT_TOO_LARGE   = 0x6003   _tf_emit: result_len >
#                                        TF_RESULT_MAX = 4096.
#   TF_ERR_BAD_TURN_ID        = 0x6004   _tf_emit: turn_id upper 32
#                                        bits non-zero (must fit the
#                                        record's turn_id u32 field).
#   TF_ERR_BAD_RECORD         = 0x6005   _tf_render_tag / _tf_correlate:
#                                        record == 0.
#   TF_ERR_BAD_MAGIC          = 0x6006   record[+0..+4] != 'TFPR'
#                                        (0x52504654 LE).
#   TF_ERR_MISMATCH           = 0x6007   _tf_correlate: expected_tag's
#                                        inner differs from the
#                                        record's rendered_tag.
#   TF_ERR_BAD_LEN            = 0x6008   _tf_correlate: record_len !=
#                                        64 OR expected_len < 16 OR
#                                        expected_tag missing wrapper
#                                        prefix/suffix.
#   TF_ERR_BAD_EXPECTED       = 0x6009   _tf_correlate: expected_tag ==
#                                        0.
#
# Slots 0x600A..0x60FF reserved for R229.M10.1+ follow-ups
# (TF_ERR_BAD_ENV_MASK on the M10.1 env-mask field validator;
# TF_ERR_HASH_MISMATCH on the M10.3 strict-mode hash-only correlator
# that recomputes FNV from the result bytes; TF_ERR_TIMESTAMP_SKEW on
# the M10.4 rdtsc-skew sanity bound).
#
# The 0x6000..0x60FF range is reserved for the TurnFingerprint anti-
# fabrication anchor across R229.M10+. R229.M9 (renderer) owns the
# disjoint 0x5D00..0x5DFF slice; R225.M8 (lsp_hover) owns the
# disjoint 0x5E00..0x5EFF slice (landed Wave 130); R227.M5
# (module_functor) owns the disjoint 0x5F00..0x5FFF slice (landed
# in the same Wave 130 / 131 parallel-arm set).
#
# The 0x6000..0x60FF carve-out sits directly above the R227.M5
# ModuleFunctor band. A downstream compound-error dispatcher can
# discriminate "turn-fingerprint faulted" from every other sibling
# satellite's failure modes by `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x60`.
#
# Grep confirmation at R229.M10 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x60[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> hits only in src/turn_fingerprint.pdx, caps.decl, STATUS.md,
#        CHANGELOG.md (via .plans/scratch/CHANGELOG-R229M10.md), and
#        design/shell/turn-fingerprint.md.
#   grep -rE '0x60(0[0-9])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + turn_fingerprint.pdx
#        + design/shell/turn-fingerprint.md.
#
# ---- R229.M10 catalogue paragraph (turn_fingerprint) -----------------
#
#   R229.M10 TurnFingerprint (turn_fingerprint.pdx, landed): parser-
#      tier PER-TURN ANTI-FABRICATION FINGERPRINT emitter + correlator.
#      Implements the paideia-os project invariant P9
#      (`design/foundations/project-invariants.md`): every REPL turn
#      MUST emit `@fingerprint("turn.<id>.<result-hash>")` so the
#      debugger (both the sub-agent debugger and the smoke-mode
#      correlation harness) can anchor every claimed result to a
#      hash of the actual emitted bytes. Four public entry points
#      over caller-supplied buffers:
#        _tf_init() -> ()
#          Bare `ret`. M10 holds NO .bss state (every operation is
#          caller-scratched; the module is a pure function of its
#          inputs modulo the rdtsc timestamp). Entry kept for
#          parallelism with the parser-family init-hook convention.
#        _tf_emit(turn_id, result_ptr, result_len, out, out_cap)
#          -> status
#          Produces the 64-byte TurnFingerprint record at
#          [out, out+64). 6 callee-save pushes
#          (rbx=turn_id, rbp=result_ptr, r12=result_len, r13=out,
#          r14=out_cap, r15=hash); no nested CALL (FNV-1a 64 inlined
#          byte-identical to _cmd_sig_fnv1a64 R222.M2 /
#          _fi_fnv1a64 R222.M3 / _cmdreg_fnv1a64 R222.M5 /
#          _ws_schema_hash R223.M5 / _h_append R229.M5; decimal
#          turn_id rendering via greedy-subtract over local
#          `_tf_pow10` LUT; 16-nibble lowercase hex rendering via
#          unrolled `shr r, N; and r, 15; mov_b r, [_tf_hex + r]`).
#          No sub-rsp alignment pad required because no nested CALL
#          fires (precedent: R229.M5 `_h_append`). rdtsc timestamp
#          stored at [+16] via standard `rdtsc; shl rdx, 32;
#          or rax, rdx` combine.
#        _tf_render_tag(record, out, out_cap) -> packed_u64
#          Emits `@fingerprint("<inner>")` where <inner> is copied
#          from record[+24..+24+N] up to the first NUL or offset 32.
#          Pure leaf; zero callee-save pushes; zero nested CALL.
#          14-byte `@fingerprint("` prefix loaded from .rodata
#          `_tf_prefix`; 2-byte `")` suffix emitted via unrolled
#          byte stores. Returns `(bytes_written << 32) | TF_OK` on
#          success.
#        _tf_correlate(expected_tag, expected_len, actual_record,
#                      record_len) -> status
#          The debugger's compare entry. Avoids a scratch buffer by
#          verifying the wrapper prefix/suffix on expected_tag and
#          byte-comparing the inner against actual_record[+24..].
#          Pure leaf; zero callee-save pushes; zero nested CALL.
#          Returns TF_OK on exact match, TF_ERR_MISMATCH on any
#          byte disagreement.
#      64-byte TurnFingerprint record layout: magic 'TFPR'
#      (0x52504654 LE) u32 at [+0]; turn_id u32 at [+4]; result_hash
#      u64 at [+8]; timestamp_us u64 at [+16]; rendered_tag u8[32]
#      at [+24..+56] NUL-padded; reserved u64 at [+56] = 0. Stride
#      64 is a power of two (`shl idx, 6` addressing for a future
#      multi-turn ring).
#      Three .rodata tables: _tf_pow10 [u32;10] (greedy-subtract
#      LUT for decimal rendering); _tf_hex_digits [u8;16]
#      (lowercase 0-9 a-f ASCII); _tf_prefix [u8;16] (14-byte
#      `@fingerprint("` + 2 pad bytes). All three @align(8).
#      Error band `0x6000..0x60FF` with the ten TF_* status codes
#      (TF_OK, TF_ERR_BAD_OUT/OUT_TOO_SMALL/BAD_RESULT/
#      RESULT_TOO_LARGE/BAD_TURN_ID/BAD_RECORD/BAD_MAGIC/MISMATCH/
#      BAD_LEN/BAD_EXPECTED); 0x600A..0x60FF reserved for M10.1+
#      follow-ups.
#      Twenty pub constants beyond the TF_* status taxonomy:
#      TF_MAGIC = 0x52504654 'TFPR' LE; TF_RECORD_BYTES = 64 (power
#      of two for `shl idx, 6` addressing); TF_RENDERED_TAG_BYTES =
#      32; TF_RESULT_MAX = 4096; TF_OFF_MAGIC = 0, TF_OFF_TURN_ID =
#      4, TF_OFF_RESULT_HASH = 8, TF_OFF_TIMESTAMP = 16,
#      TF_OFF_RENDERED_TAG = 24, TF_OFF_RESERVED = 56;
#      TF_WRAPPER_PREFIX_BYTES = 14; TF_WRAPPER_SUFFIX_BYTES = 2.
#      Labels prefixed `tf_` with per-function suffix (`tf_ini_`,
#      `tf_em_`, `tf_rt_`, `tf_co_`). Grep-confirmed disjoint from
#      every landed parser-tier label prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_,
#      ast_, sm_, fh_, sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_,
#      ds_, pl_, pl_lam_, rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_,
#      er_, cr_, ls_, lp_, hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_,
#      le_, vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_) and from the
#      kernel-side `atf_` prefix (R129.M3 ANSI256 tabular fallback;
#      `tf_` and `atf_` are distinct label-anchored prefixes --
#      paideia-as label resolution anchors on first character, so a
#      `tf_` label never collides with an `atf_` symbol). Also
#      disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if,
#      let, fn, ret, done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The per-turn fingerprint emitter runs purely
# in user space over caller-supplied result bytes + caller-owned
# 64-byte scratch record; no KIND surface is touched at raw M10.
# The forward-path landings (M10.1 env-mask field; M10.2 result-
# length field; M10.3 strict-mode hash-only correlator; M10.4 rdtsc-
# skew sanity bound; M10.5 500-turn golden corpus) all stay in-
# process at the parser tier and introduce NO new KIND surface.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R229.M10 (turn_fingerprint.pdx) has NO external
# cross-library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-
# module linker dependency at raw M10. All four public entry points
# touch ONLY caller-supplied pointer args and the module's own
# .rodata tables (`_tf_pow10`, `_tf_hex_digits`, `_tf_prefix`); the
# module carries NO .bss state at raw M10. The forward-path landings
# remain opaque-pointer by design -- no M10.x landing introduces a
# cross-module linker dependency without an explicit opt-in from the
# consuming module.
#
# R229.M10 label prefix `tf_` is DISJOINT from every landed parser-
# tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_,
# rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_,
# hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_,
# rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_) and from the kernel-side `atf_` prefix
# (R129.M3 ANSI256 tabular fallback; label resolution is first-
# character-anchored so `tf_<x>` and `atf_<y>` never collide). Also
# disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if, let,
# fn, ret, done).

# ---- Error band 0x6200..0x62FF (R227.M6, version_pragma) -------------
#
# Parser-tier `#requires-paideia OP X.Y[.Z]` directive parser +
# resolution predicate. Eight slots used at R227.M6:
#
#   VP_OK                     = 0        success (bytes_consumed packed
#                                        in high 32 bits on _vp_parse;
#                                        raw VP_OK on _vp_check satisfied
#                                        constraint).
#   VP_ERR_BAD_DIRECTIVE      = 0x6200   prefix mismatch -- the input
#                                        does not begin with the exact
#                                        17-byte string "#requires-
#                                        paideia" OR required WS+ after
#                                        the prefix is missing.
#   VP_ERR_BAD_OP             = 0x6201   op token not in {=, >=, <=,
#                                        ~=, >, <}.
#   VP_ERR_BAD_SEMVER         = 0x6202   missing / malformed digit run
#                                        on major, minor, or patch OR
#                                        missing '.' between major and
#                                        minor OR digit count > 9 on
#                                        any component (u32 overflow
#                                        guard; R133.M4 Wave 118 lesson)
#                                        OR required WS+ between op and
#                                        semver is missing.
#   VP_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL      = 0x6203   _vp_parse: out == 0 OR
#                                        out_cap < VP_RECORD_BYTES
#                                        (32).
#   VP_ERR_BAD_RECORD         = 0x6204   _vp_check: record_ref == 0 OR
#                                        magic u32 at [+0] does not
#                                        match 'RVPR' (0x52505652 LE).
#   VP_ERR_SRC_TOO_SHORT      = 0x6205   _vp_parse: bytes_len <
#                                        VP_MIN_SRC_LEN (18 = prefix 17
#                                        + at least one trailing byte).
#   VP_ERR_VERSION_MISMATCH   = 0x6210   _vp_check: directive parsed
#                                        OK but running triple fails
#                                        op vs required triple.
#
# Slots 0x6206..0x620F reserved for R227.M6.1+ parse diagnostics
# (prerelease tag parse per SemVer 2.0 §9, build-metadata parse per
# §10, non-ASCII byte in directive, trailing-garbage strictness
# toggle, trailing-newline requirement toggle). Slots 0x6211..0x62FF
# reserved for R227.M6.2+ check diagnostics (version-skew warn-level,
# prerelease-ordering compare, build-metadata-ignore flag assertion).
#
# The 0x6200..0x62FF range is reserved for the VersionPragma parser-
# tier substrate across R227.M6+. The parallel 0x6100..0x61FF band is
# held for the R225.M9 property-test harness arm; R229.M10
# TurnFingerprint owns the disjoint 0x6000..0x60FF slice directly
# below. The 0x6200..0x62FF carve-out is also DISJOINT from the
# kernel-side 0x3B00..0x3BFF VersionGate band -- the two halves of
# the version-check machinery (parser-tier record producer vs.
# kernel-tier single-shot dispatcher) share grammar and comparison
# semantics but live in distinct error-code spaces so a downstream
# dispatcher never confuses "the record producer faulted" with "the
# kernel-tier dispatcher faulted".
#
# A downstream compound-error dispatcher can discriminate "version-
# pragma substrate faulted" from every other sibling satellite's
# failure modes via `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x62`.
#
# Grep confirmation at R227.M6 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x62[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> hits only in src/version_pragma.pdx, caps.decl, STATUS.md,
#        CHANGELOG.md (via .plans/scratch/CHANGELOG-R227M6.md), and
#        design/shell/version-pragma.md.
#   grep -rE '0x62(0[0-5]|10)\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + version_pragma.pdx
#        + design/shell/version-pragma.md.
#
# ---- R227.M6 catalogue paragraph (version_pragma) --------------------
#
#   R227.M6 VersionPragma (version_pragma.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      `#requires-paideia OP X.Y[.Z]` directive parser + resolution
#      predicate. The PARSER-TIER half of the kernel-side R133.M4
#      `_vg_check` version-gate dispatcher: the kernel-tier module
#      walks a pragma line in one shot (parse + compare against the
#      kernel-tier KERNEL_VERSION_* statics); THIS module splits the
#      dispatcher into a PARSE phase (emits a reusable 32-byte
#      VersionPragma record) and a CHECK phase (callable against ANY
#      running-triple -- shell version, federated-peer version, or
#      the kernel's own triple). Three public entry points over
#      caller-supplied buffers:
#        _vp_init() -> ()
#          Bare `ret`. M6 holds NO .bss state (every record is
#          caller-owned on all operational paths). Entry kept for
#          parallelism with the parser-family init-hook convention.
#        _vp_parse(bytes, bytes_len, out, out_cap) -> packed_u64
#          Produces one 32-byte VersionPragma record at
#          [out, out+32). 6-push PURE LEAF (no nested CALL) with
#          rbx=bytes_ptr, rbp=bytes_len, r12=out, r13=cursor,
#          r14=parsed_op, r15=patch_flag; FNV is NOT used (no name
#          hashing at raw M6). Semver digit runs capped at 9 digits
#          (R133.M4 Wave 118 u32-overflow guard: cap each component's
#          digit count via `cmp rcx, 9; ja vp_pa_bs`). Digit
#          accumulator uses `(acc<<3)+(acc<<1) = acc*10` via shl +
#          add (no 2-op imul-r-imm). Greedy 2-char op match (`>=`
#          before `>`, `<=` before `<`, `~=` before any single-byte
#          op). Returns `(bytes_consumed << 32) | status`.
#        _vp_check(record_ref, running_major, running_minor,
#                  running_patch) -> status
#          Three-way lex compare (running vs required) encoded as
#          rsi in {0 LO, 1 EQ, 2 HI}; op dispatch per the kernel-side
#          R133.M4 semantics (EQ, GE, LE, GT, LT, TILDE each have
#          their own imm8 cmp arm). 4-push PURE LEAF (no nested
#          CALL) with rbx=record_ref, rbp=running_major,
#          r12=running_minor, r13=running_patch. The running triple
#          is CALLER-SUPPLIED, not kernel-static -- so the compare
#          target picker is the caller (shell vs. kernel vs.
#          federation peer). Returns VP_OK, VP_ERR_VERSION_MISMATCH
#          (0x6210), or VP_ERR_BAD_RECORD (0x6204).
#      32-byte VersionPragma record layout: magic 'RVPR'
#      (0x52505652 LE) u32 at [+0]; op u8 at [+4]; flags u8 at [+5]
#      (bit 0 = VP_FLAG_PATCH_PRESENT); reserved u16 at [+6]; major
#      u32 at [+8]; minor u32 at [+12]; patch u32 at [+16]; reserved
#      u8[12] at [+20]. Stride 32 is a power of two (`shl idx, 5`
#      addressing for a future per-import pragma cache without 2-op
#      imul-r-imm).
#      One .rodata table: `vp_prefix : [u8; 18]` holding the exact
#      17-byte "#requires-paideia" prefix NUL-padded to 18 bytes
#      for 8-byte alignment.
#      Error band `0x6200..0x62FF` with the eight VP_* status codes
#      (VP_OK, VP_ERR_BAD_DIRECTIVE 0x6200, VP_ERR_BAD_OP 0x6201,
#      VP_ERR_BAD_SEMVER 0x6202, VP_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL 0x6203,
#      VP_ERR_BAD_RECORD 0x6204, VP_ERR_SRC_TOO_SHORT 0x6205,
#      VP_ERR_VERSION_MISMATCH 0x6210); 0x6206..0x620F reserved for
#      M6.1+ parse diagnostics; 0x6211..0x62FF reserved for M6.2+
#      check diagnostics.
#      Fourteen pub constants beyond the VP_* status taxonomy:
#      VP_RECORD_MAGIC = 0x52505652 'RVPR' LE; VP_RECORD_BYTES = 32
#      (power of two for `shl idx, 5` addressing); VP_PREFIX_LEN =
#      17; VP_MIN_SRC_LEN = 18; VP_DIGIT_CAP = 9; VP_OFF_MAGIC = 0,
#      VP_OFF_OP = 4, VP_OFF_FLAGS = 5, VP_OFF_RESERVED_6 = 6,
#      VP_OFF_MAJOR = 8, VP_OFF_MINOR = 12, VP_OFF_PATCH = 16,
#      VP_OFF_RESERVED_20 = 20; VP_OP_EQ = 0, VP_OP_GE = 1,
#      VP_OP_LE = 2, VP_OP_GT = 3, VP_OP_LT = 4, VP_OP_TILDE = 5;
#      VP_FLAG_PATCH_PRESENT = 0x01; VP_CMP_LO = 0, VP_CMP_EQ = 1,
#      VP_CMP_HI = 2.
#      Labels prefixed `vp_` with per-function suffix (`vp_ini_` init,
#      `vp_pa_` parse, `vp_ck_` check). Grep-confirmed virgin inside
#      tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/src/*.pdx (no prior `vp_` label
#      in the parser-tier library). Grep-confirmed disjoint from
#      every landed parser-tier label prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_,
#      ast_, sm_, fh_, sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_,
#      ds_, pl_, pl_lam_, rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_,
#      er_, cr_, ls_, lp_, hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_,
#      le_, vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_) AND
#      from the kernel-side `vg_` prefix (R133.M4 VersionGate) --
#      `vp_` and `vg_` differ on the second character and label
#      resolution is prefix-anchored. The kernel-side `vp_submit` /
#      `vp_await` symbols in vello_render_channel.pdx are GLOBAL
#      linker symbols in the kernel, NOT local labels in this
#      parser-tier library; the two live in disjoint linker scopes
#      (this library NEVER links the kernel). Also disjoint from
#      the paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if, let, fn, ret,
#      done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The version-pragma parser + resolution
# predicate runs purely in user space over caller-supplied directive
# bytes + caller-owned 32-byte scratch record + three caller-supplied
# running-triple u64 arguments; no KIND surface is touched at raw M6.
# The forward-path landings (M6.1 prerelease tag parse; M6.2 build-
# metadata parse; M6.3 per-import version pin composed with
# ImportResolve; M6.4 err-span injection into DiagReport; M6.5
# 8-fixture acceptance corpus) all stay in-process at the parser
# tier and introduce NO new KIND surface.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R227.M6 (version_pragma.pdx) has NO external
# cross-library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-
# module linker dependency at raw M6. All three public entry points
# touch ONLY caller-supplied pointer args + the module's own
# .rodata table (`vp_prefix` 18-byte prefix); the module carries
# NO .bss state at raw M6. The forward-path intra-library deps are
# DECLARED here for the M6.3 + M6.4 landings and will be wired
# through weak-import function-pointer slots so each dep only fires
# when the referenced primitive is linked in:
#   M6.3 -> R227.M3 ImportResolve ImportDirective-adjacent pragma
#     pointer field (per-import version pin).
#   M6.4 -> R225.M7 DiagReport `_dr_emit` (err-span injection into
#     the parse-error diagnostic chain).
#
# R227.M6 label prefix `vp_` is DISJOINT from every landed parser-
# tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_,
# rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_,
# hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_,
# rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_) AND from the kernel-side `vg_`
# prefix (R133.M4 VersionGate; label resolution is prefix-anchored
# so `vp_` and `vg_` never collide despite sharing the first
# character). Also disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-keyword
# set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).

# ---- Error band 0x6100..0x61FF (R225.M9, proptest_hm) ----------------
#
# Property-based test harness over random pds-lang programs; raw-M9
# harness landing (10k corpus + 24-hour sustained-run gate deferred to
# R225.M9.5 along with the full HM elaborator wire-up). Thirteen slots
# used at R225.M9:
#
#   PTH_OK                        = 0        normal return (used on
#                                              both check-property and
#                                              corpus-driver paths).
#   PTH_ERR_BAD_OUT               = 0x6100   out == 0 on _pth_generate
#                                              OR out_stats == 0 on
#                                              _pth_run_corpus.
#   PTH_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL         = 0x6101   out_cap < 32 on _pth_generate
#                                              OR out_cap < 128 on
#                                              _pth_run_corpus.
#   PTH_ERR_BAD_INPUT             = 0x6102   program_ptr == 0 on
#                                              _pth_check_property.
#   PTH_ERR_INPUT_TOO_LARGE       = 0x6103   len > 256 on
#                                              _pth_check_property.
#   PTH_ERR_BAD_LEN               = 0x6104   len == 0 on
#                                              _pth_check_property.
#   PTH_ERR_BAD_EXPECTED          = 0x6105   expected_welltyped not in
#                                              {0, 1} on
#                                              _pth_check_property.
#   PTH_ERR_N_TOO_LARGE           = 0x6106   n_programs > 65536 on
#                                              _pth_run_corpus.
#   PTH_ERR_BAD_STATS             = 0x6107   out_stats == 0 on
#                                              _pth_run_corpus.
#   PTH_ERR_PROPERTY_P1_VIOLATION = 0x6110   P1 refuted (well-typed
#                                              program emitted a type
#                                              error OR tag disagreed
#                                              with expected=1).
#   PTH_ERR_PROPERTY_P2_VIOLATION = 0x6111   P2 refuted (ill-typed
#                                              program emitted no type
#                                              error OR tag disagreed
#                                              with expected=0).
#   PTH_ERR_PROPERTY_P3_VIOLATION = 0x6112   P3 refuted (effect-row
#                                              non-monotone across
#                                              composed stages; raw M9
#                                              RESERVED -- the P3
#                                              oracle lands at M9.3).
#   PTH_ERR_UNKNOWN_TAG           = 0x6114   program[0] not in {'+' =
#                                              0x2B, '-' = 0x2D} on
#                                              _pth_check_property.
#   PTH_ERR_RC_STRIDE_FAIL        = 0x6115   RankCheck stride call
#                                              into R225.M6
#                                              `_rc_check_scheme`
#                                              disagreed with the
#                                              tag expectation
#                                              (well-typed canonical
#                                              returned non-zero OR
#                                              ill-typed canonical
#                                              returned RC_OK).
#
# Slots 0x6108..0x610F, 0x6113, 0x6116..0x61FF reserved for R225.M9.1+
# follow-ups (grammar-driven generation error codes, elaborator-wire-up
# diagnostic surface, state-carried corpus-mode error codes, 24-hour-
# run instrumentation seams).
#
# The 0x6100..0x61FF range is reserved for the ProptestHm packed-status
# codes across R225.M9+. R229.M10 (turn_fingerprint) owns the disjoint
# 0x6000..0x60FF slice (landed Wave 131); R227.M6 (version_pragma) owns
# the disjoint 0x6200..0x62FF slice (landed in the same parallel-arm
# set as this R225.M9 landing).
#
# The 0x6100..0x61FF carve-out was PRE-RESERVED by the R227.M6
# VersionPragma catalogue note ("The parallel 0x6100..0x61FF band is
# held for the R225.M9 property-test harness arm"). This landing claims
# the slice. A downstream compound-error dispatcher discriminates
# proptest faults from every other sibling satellite's failure modes
# via `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x61`.
#
# Grep confirmation at R225.M9 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x61[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> hits in src/proptest_hm.pdx, caps.decl, STATUS.md, and
#        src/version_pragma.pdx. The version_pragma.pdx hit is the
#        R227.M6 band-reservation prose ("the parallel 0x6100..0x61FF
#        band") that pre-reserved this carve-out for R225.M9; it is a
#        band-level cross-reference, not a packed-status slot
#        assignment.
#   grep -rE '0x61(0[0-7]|1[0-5])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status slot assignments outside this file +
#        proptest_hm.pdx + design/shell/proptest-hm.md +
#        .plans/scratch/CHANGELOG-R225M9.md. The incidental hit in
#        version_pragma.pdx is band-reservation prose only.
#
# ---- R225.M9 catalogue paragraph (proptest_hm) -----------------------
#
#   R225.M9 ProptestHm (proptest_hm.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      PROPERTY-BASED TEST HARNESS over random pds-lang programs.
#      Implements the §15.6 verification clause for the typechecker
#      tier: well-typed programs never fail (P1); ill-typed programs
#      always emit at least one error (P2); effect-row composition
#      across stages is monotone (P3; raw-M9 canned pass, real
#      wire-up at M9.3). Four public entry points over caller-
#      supplied buffers; the module carries a tiny `.bss` (two u64
#      scalars + a 64 B scratch program buffer) and two `.rodata`
#      tables (8 x 32 B random-program template table +
#      two 24 B canonical schemes for the RankCheck stride):
#        _pth_init() -> ()
#          Reset `_pth_prng_state` to 1 (xorshift64 is degenerate on
#          state 0) and `_pth_program_counter` to 0. Pure leaf;
#          zero callee-save pushes; no stack frame; no nested CALL.
#        _pth_generate(seed, out, out_cap) -> packed_u64
#          Deterministically emit one of 8 template programs. The
#          template index is the low 3 bits of a xorshift64-mixed
#          seed. Each emitted program's first byte is the welltyped
#          tag ('+' = 0x2B or '-' = 0x2D). Pure leaf (zero callee-
#          save pushes; no stack frame; no nested CALL). Validates
#          `out != 0` (PTH_ERR_BAD_OUT 0x6100) and `out_cap >= 32`
#          (PTH_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL 0x6101). Returns
#          `(program_len << 32) | PTH_OK` on success.
#        _pth_check_property(program_ptr, len, expected_welltyped)
#            -> status
#          Validates expected_welltyped against the tag at
#          program[0]; calls R225.M6 `_rc_check_scheme` on one of
#          two canonical schemes to prove the typechecker wire-up
#          executes BOTH ways (OK on well-typed canonical, non-OK
#          on ill-typed canonical). Returns a P1 (0x6110) / P2
#          (0x6111) / stride-fail (0x6115) verdict on disagreement.
#          4-push callee-save frame (rbx = program_ptr,
#          rbp = len, r12 = expected, r13 = scheme_ptr) + sub rsp,8
#          align = 40 B frame; entry rsp%16==8, after 4 pushes
#          rsp%16==8, +8 pad = rsp%16==0 at the single nested CALL
#          to `_rc_check_scheme`.
#        _pth_run_corpus(seed, n_programs, out_stats, out_cap)
#            -> packed_u64
#          The outer loop. Zero-fills 128 bytes at out_stats; stamps
#          magic 'PTHS' = 0x53485450 LE u32 at [+0], version u16 = 1
#          at [+4], seed u64 at [+72]. Loops N times, calling
#          `_pth_generate` + `_pth_check_property` and bucketing
#          each verdict in six u64 counters (n_wt_pass [+16],
#          n_wt_fail [+24], n_it_pass [+32], n_it_fail [+40],
#          n_p3_pass [+48], n_p3_fail [+56]); stashes the last
#          non-OK verdict's status + program index at [+64] /
#          [+68]. 6-push callee-save frame (rbx = base_seed, rbp =
#          n_programs, r12 = i, r13 = out_stats, r14 = out_cap,
#          r15 = scratch_prog_ptr) + sub rsp,8 align = 56 B frame;
#          entry rsp%16==8, after 6 pushes rsp%16==8, +8 pad =
#          rsp%16==0 at every nested CALL to `_pth_generate` and
#          `_pth_check_property`. Pre-prologue guards:
#          `out_stats != 0` (PTH_ERR_BAD_STATS 0x6107),
#          `out_cap >= 128` (PTH_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL 0x6101),
#          `n_programs <= 65536` (PTH_ERR_N_TOO_LARGE 0x6106).
#      128-byte PthStats record layout: magic 'PTHS' u32 at [+0];
#      version u16 = 1 at [+4]; reserved u16 at [+6]; n_generated
#      u64 at [+8]; n_wt_pass u64 at [+16]; n_wt_fail u64 at [+24];
#      n_it_pass u64 at [+32]; n_it_fail u64 at [+40]; n_p3_pass
#      u64 at [+48]; n_p3_fail u64 at [+56]; last_code u32 at
#      [+64]; last_idx u32 at [+68]; seed u64 at [+72]; reserved
#      u8[48] at [+80]. Stride 128 = 2^7 is a power of two so a
#      future multi-run ring at [base + i << 7] indexes without
#      `imul`.
#      Two `.rodata` canonical schemes (24 B each): `_pth_canon_wt_
#      scheme` (rank-1 TyCon(u64) scheme; `_rc_check_scheme` returns
#      RC_OK 0 under raw-M6 wire format) and `_pth_canon_it_scheme`
#      (ill-formed scheme carrying a reserved kind=5 TYFORALL body
#      node; `_rc_check_scheme` returns RC_ERR_NESTED_FORALL 0x5811
#      -- the body[0] forall branch). Each 24-byte scheme is the
#      minimum valid HmCore wire-format record: 4 B n_bound_vars +
#      4 B n_body_nodes + 16 B single body TypeNode.
#      One 256-byte `.rodata` template table (`_pth_templates`):
#      eight 32-byte entries; entry[+0] is the length u8;
#      entry[+1..+1+length] is the program (starts with the
#      welltyped tag '+' or '-'); rest is NUL pad. Stride 32 = 2^5.
#      Error band `0x6100..0x61FF` with the thirteen PTH_* status
#      codes (PTH_OK, PTH_ERR_BAD_OUT, PTH_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL,
#      PTH_ERR_BAD_INPUT, PTH_ERR_INPUT_TOO_LARGE, PTH_ERR_BAD_LEN,
#      PTH_ERR_BAD_EXPECTED, PTH_ERR_N_TOO_LARGE,
#      PTH_ERR_BAD_STATS, PTH_ERR_PROPERTY_P1_VIOLATION,
#      PTH_ERR_PROPERTY_P2_VIOLATION, PTH_ERR_PROPERTY_P3_VIOLATION,
#      PTH_ERR_UNKNOWN_TAG, PTH_ERR_RC_STRIDE_FAIL); 0x6108..0x610F,
#      0x6113, 0x6116..0x61FF reserved for M9.1+ follow-ups.
#      Twenty-four `pub` constants beyond the PTH_* status taxonomy:
#      PTH_PROGRAM_MAX_BYTES = 256; PTH_PROGRAM_SCRATCH_BYTES = 64;
#      PTH_STATS_BYTES = 128; PTH_STATS_MAGIC = 0x53485450 'PTHS'
#      LE; PTH_STATS_VERSION = 1; PTH_N_TEMPLATES = 8;
#      PTH_TEMPLATE_STRIDE = 32; PTH_TEMPLATES_TOTAL_BYTES = 256;
#      PTH_TEMPLATE_MAX_PROG_BYTES = 31; PTH_TAG_WELLTYPED = 0x2B
#      '+'; PTH_TAG_ILLTYPED = 0x2D '-'; PTH_EXPECTED_ILLTYPED = 0;
#      PTH_EXPECTED_WELLTYPED = 1; PTH_MAX_N_PROGRAMS = 65536;
#      PTH_SCHEME_BYTES = 24; PTH_STATS_OFF_* (ten field offsets);
#      PTH_TEMPLATE_OFF_LEN = 0; PTH_TEMPLATE_OFF_PROG = 1.
#      Labels prefixed `pth_` with per-function suffix (`pth_ini_`
#      init, `pth_gen_` generate, `pth_cp_` check-property,
#      `pth_rc_` run-corpus). Grep-confirmed disjoint from every
#      landed parser-tier label prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_,
#      sm_, fh_, sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_,
#      pl_, pl_lam_, rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_,
#      cr_, ls_, lp_, hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_,
#      vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_, vp_).
#      Also disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-keyword set
#      (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The property-test harness runs purely in user
# space over caller-supplied random programs + caller-owned 128-byte
# scratch stats record; no KIND surface is touched at raw M9. The
# forward-path landings (M9.1 real HM elaborator wire-up; M9.2
# grammar-driven random walk; M9.3 P3 monotone wire-up; M9.4 state-
# carried corpus mode; M9.5 10k corpus + 24-hour sustained-run gate;
# M9.6 AFL++/libFuzzer driver shim; M9.7 per-iteration oracle arm)
# all stay in-process at the parser tier and introduce NO new KIND
# surface.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R225.M9 (proptest_hm.pdx) has ONE intra-library
# cross-module linker dependency at raw M9: `_rc_check_scheme`
# (R225.M6 RankCheck; `src/rank_check.pdx`), called twice per
# `_pth_check_property` invocation (once per tag-expected branch;
# only one of the two branches fires per call). RankCheck's error
# band 0x5800..0x58FF carves no slot ProptestHm might collide with;
# the two bands are disjoint by design. ProptestHm introduces NO
# external cross-library linker dependencies at raw M9 and NO new
# intra-library deps beyond RankCheck. The forward-path landings
# add dependencies on R225.M1 HmCore (`_hm_instantiate`,
# `_hm_unify`) at M9.1, R224.M3 EffectRow + R225.M4 EffectUnify at
# M9.3, and R221.M4 CtxLexer at M9.1 (for lex-driven oracle
# derivation).
#
# R225.M9 label prefix `pth_` is DISJOINT from every landed parser-
# tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_,
# rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_,
# hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_,
# rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_, vp_). Also disjoint from the
# paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).

# ---- Error band 0x6300..0x63FF (R225.M10, check_fingerprint) ---------
#
# Per-turn TYPECHECK-OUTCOME fingerprint packed-status range. Nine
# slots used at R225.M10:
#
#   CF_OK                     = 0        success (64 bytes written on
#                                        _cf_emit; packed bytes_written
#                                        on _cf_render_tag; raw CF_OK
#                                        on _cf_correlate match).
#   CF_ERR_BAD_OUT            = 0x6300   out == 0 on any entry.
#   CF_ERR_OUT_TOO_SMALL      = 0x6301   out_cap below required width
#                                        (64 for _cf_emit;
#                                        16 + inner_len for
#                                        _cf_render_tag).
#   CF_ERR_BAD_TURN_ID        = 0x6302   _cf_emit: turn_id upper 32
#                                        bits non-zero (must fit the
#                                        record's turn_id u32 field).
#   CF_ERR_BAD_N_DIAGS        = 0x6303   _cf_emit: n_diags upper 32
#                                        bits non-zero (must fit the
#                                        decimal rendering ceiling).
#   CF_ERR_BAD_RECORD         = 0x6304   _cf_render_tag / _cf_correlate:
#                                        record == 0.
#   CF_ERR_BAD_MAGIC          = 0x6305   record[+0..+4] != 'CFPR'
#                                        (0x52504643 LE).
#   CF_ERR_MISMATCH           = 0x6306   _cf_correlate: expected_tag's
#                                        inner differs from the
#                                        record's rendered_tag.
#   CF_ERR_BAD_LEN            = 0x6307   _cf_correlate: record_len !=
#                                        64 OR expected_len < 16 OR
#                                        expected_tag missing wrapper
#                                        prefix/suffix.
#   CF_ERR_BAD_EXPECTED       = 0x6308   _cf_correlate: expected_tag ==
#                                        0.
#
# Slots 0x6309..0x63FF reserved for R225.M10.1+ follow-ups
# (CF_ERR_BAD_ENV_MASK on the M10.1 env-mask field validator;
# CF_ERR_HASH_MISMATCH on the M10.3 strict-mode hash-only correlator
# that recomputes FNV from the result-string bytes; CF_ERR_TIMESTAMP_
# SKEW on the M10.4 rdtsc-skew sanity bound; CF_ERR_LC_RESULT_UNKNOWN
# on the M10.6 four-category outcome adapter).
#
# The 0x6300..0x63FF range is reserved for the CheckFingerprint anti-
# fabrication anchor across R225.M10+. R229.M10 (turn_fingerprint)
# owns the disjoint 0x6000..0x60FF slice (landed Wave 131, RESULT-
# side sibling); R225.M9 (proptest_hm) owns the disjoint
# 0x6100..0x61FF slice; R227.M6 (version_pragma) owns the disjoint
# 0x6200..0x62FF slice. R227.M7 (script_cache) owns the disjoint
# 0x6500..0x65FF slice directly above the R225.M10 carve-out with
# the 0x6400..0x64FF slice held unreserved between them.
#
# A downstream compound-error dispatcher can discriminate "check-
# fingerprint faulted" from every other sibling satellite's failure
# modes by `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x63`.
#
# Grep confirmation at R225.M10 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x63[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> hits only in src/check_fingerprint.pdx, caps.decl, STATUS.md,
#        CHANGELOG.md (via .plans/scratch/CHANGELOG-R225M10.md), and
#        design/shell/check-fingerprint.md.
#   grep -rE '0x63(0[0-8])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + check_fingerprint.pdx
#        + design/shell/check-fingerprint.md.
#
# ---- R225.M10 catalogue paragraph (check_fingerprint) ----------------
#
#   R225.M10 CheckFingerprint (check_fingerprint.pdx, landed): parser-
#      tier PER-TURN TYPECHECK-OUTCOME ANTI-FABRICATION FINGERPRINT
#      emitter + correlator. Implements the TYPECHECK-side half of the
#      paideia-os project invariant P9 (`design/foundations/project-
#      invariants.md`): every REPL turn MUST emit
#      `@fingerprint("check.<id>.<ok|fail-N>")` where <ok|fail-N>
#      indicates whether the HM elaborator + LoadCheck orchestrator
#      pass (R227.M4) completed cleanly or emitted N diagnostics.
#      Pairs with R229.M10 TurnFingerprint which anchors the RESULT
#      bytes side (`@fingerprint("turn.<id>.<hash-hex>")`); both land
#      per turn.
#      Four public entry points over caller-supplied buffers:
#        _cf_init() -> ()
#          Bare `ret`. M10 holds NO .bss state (every operation is
#          caller-scratched; the module is a pure function of its
#          inputs modulo the rdtsc timestamp). Entry kept for
#          parallelism with the parser-family init-hook convention.
#        _cf_emit(turn_id, lc_result_status, n_diags, out, out_cap)
#          -> status
#          Produces the 64-byte CheckFingerprint record at
#          [out, out+64). 6 callee-save pushes
#          (rbx=turn_id, rbp=lc_result_status, r12=n_diags, r13=out,
#          r14=out_cap, r15=hash); no nested CALL (FNV-1a 64 inlined
#          byte-identical to _cmd_sig_fnv1a64 R222.M2 /
#          _fi_fnv1a64 R222.M3 / _cmdreg_fnv1a64 R222.M5 /
#          _ws_schema_hash R223.M5 / _h_append R229.M5 /
#          _tf_emit R229.M10; decimal turn_id and n_diags rendering
#          via greedy-subtract over local `_cf_pow10` LUT with
#          emit_flag for leading-zero suppression). Hash is computed
#          INLINE across the result-string bytes ("ok" or "fail-<N>")
#          as they are emitted into the rendered_tag field, so the
#          module makes no second pass over the string. No sub-rsp
#          alignment pad required because no nested CALL fires
#          (precedent: R229.M5 `_h_append`, R229.M10 `_tf_emit`).
#          rdtsc timestamp stored at [+16] via standard
#          `rdtsc; shl rdx, 32; or rax, rdx` combine.
#        _cf_render_tag(record, out, out_cap) -> packed_u64
#          Emits `@fingerprint("<inner>")` where <inner> is copied
#          from record[+24..+24+N] up to the first NUL or offset 32.
#          Pure leaf; zero callee-save pushes; zero nested CALL.
#          14-byte `@fingerprint("` prefix loaded from .rodata
#          `_cf_prefix`; 2-byte `")` suffix emitted via unrolled byte
#          stores. Returns `(bytes_written << 32) | CF_OK` on success.
#        _cf_correlate(expected_tag, expected_len, actual_record,
#                      record_len) -> status
#          The debugger's compare entry. Avoids a scratch buffer by
#          verifying the wrapper prefix/suffix on expected_tag and
#          byte-comparing the inner against actual_record[+24..].
#          Pure leaf; zero callee-save pushes; zero nested CALL.
#          Returns CF_OK on exact match, CF_ERR_MISMATCH on any byte
#          disagreement.
#      64-byte CheckFingerprint record layout: magic 'CFPR'
#      (0x52504643 LE) u32 at [+0]; turn_id u32 at [+4]; result_hash
#      u64 at [+8]; timestamp_us u64 at [+16]; rendered_tag u8[32]
#      at [+24..+56] NUL-padded; reserved u64 at [+56] = 0. Stride
#      64 is a power of two (`shl idx, 6` addressing for a future
#      multi-check ring). Rendered-tag format:
#      `check.<id>.<ok|fail-N>` where <id> is 1..10 decimal digits
#      (no leading zeros for id > 0; single `0` for id == 0) and the
#      result-string is either the 2-byte literal "ok" OR the 5-byte
#      literal "fail-" followed by 1..10 decimal digits of N. Max
#      live width = 6 + 10 + 1 + 5 + 10 = 32 bytes exactly (u32
#      ceiling turn_id, u32 ceiling n_diags, FAIL branch).
#      Two .rodata tables: `_cf_pow10` [u32;10] (greedy-subtract LUT
#      for decimal rendering; mirrors R229.M10 `_tf_pow10` layout);
#      `_cf_prefix` [u8;16] (14-byte `@fingerprint("` + 2 pad
#      bytes). Both @align(8). No `_cf_hex_digits` because the
#      rendered form is human-readable `ok`/`fail-N`, not hex.
#      Error band `0x6300..0x63FF` with the nine CF_* status codes
#      (CF_OK, CF_ERR_BAD_OUT/OUT_TOO_SMALL/BAD_TURN_ID/BAD_N_DIAGS/
#      BAD_RECORD/BAD_MAGIC/MISMATCH/BAD_LEN/BAD_EXPECTED);
#      0x6309..0x63FF reserved for M10.1+ follow-ups.
#      Nineteen pub constants beyond the CF_* status taxonomy:
#      CF_MAGIC = 0x52504643 'CFPR' LE; CF_RECORD_BYTES = 64 (power
#      of two for `shl idx, 6` addressing); CF_RENDERED_TAG_BYTES =
#      32; CF_OFF_MAGIC = 0, CF_OFF_TURN_ID = 4, CF_OFF_RESULT_HASH
#      = 8, CF_OFF_TIMESTAMP = 16, CF_OFF_RENDERED_TAG = 24,
#      CF_OFF_RESERVED = 56; CF_WRAPPER_PREFIX_BYTES = 14;
#      CF_WRAPPER_SUFFIX_BYTES = 2.
#      Labels prefixed `cf_` with per-function suffix (`cf_ini_`,
#      `cf_em_`, `cf_rt_`, `cf_co_`). Grep-confirmed disjoint from
#      every landed parser-tier label prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_,
#      ast_, sm_, fh_, sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_,
#      ds_, pl_, pl_lam_, rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_,
#      er_, cr_, ls_, lp_, hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_,
#      le_, vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_, vp_,
#      pth_). Also disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-keyword
#      set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The per-turn check-outcome fingerprint
# emitter runs purely in user space over caller-supplied status +
# count scalars + caller-owned 64-byte scratch record; no KIND
# surface is touched at raw M10. The forward-path landings (M10.1
# env-mask field; M10.2 diag-chain-head field; M10.3 strict-mode
# hash-only correlator; M10.4 rdtsc-skew sanity bound; M10.5
# 200-turn golden corpus; M10.6 four-category outcome adapter) all
# stay in-process at the parser tier and introduce NO new KIND
# surface.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R225.M10 (check_fingerprint.pdx) has NO external
# cross-library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-
# module linker dependency at raw M10. All four public entry points
# touch ONLY caller-supplied inputs and the module's own .rodata
# tables (`_cf_pow10`, `_cf_prefix`); the module carries NO .bss
# state at raw M10. The forward-path landings remain opaque-
# input by design -- no M10.x landing introduces a cross-module
# linker dependency without an explicit opt-in from the consuming
# module.
#
# R225.M10 label prefix `cf_` is DISJOINT from every landed parser-
# tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_,
# rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_,
# hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_,
# rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_, vp_, pth_). Label resolution
# is first-character-anchored; `cf_<x>` and `cr_<y>` (CapRow)
# never collide because the second character differs; same for
# `cl_`, `cc_`, `cmd_<x>` prefixes. Also disjoint from the
# paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).

# ---- Error band 0x6500..0x65FF (R227.M7, script_cache) ---------------
#
# Parser-tier in-memory LRU cache of elaborated script bodies keyed
# by (source_hash, deps_hash). The parser-tier substrate behind the
# REPL's "re-run the same script" fast path. Raw-M7 lands the in-
# memory layer (zero-filled 32-slot pool, (source_hash, deps_hash)
# lookup, LRU eviction, invalidate-by-source-hash, stats record);
# disk-backed `~/.paideia/script-cache/<hash>.pdc` persistence lands
# at R227.M7.1 behind pdxfs path integration. Six slots used at
# R227.M7:
#
#   SCC_OK                     = 0        success. On _sc_lookup: a
#                                        row with magic == 'SCCA' AND
#                                        flags.LIVE == 1 AND
#                                        source_hash + deps_hash
#                                        agreement. On _sc_insert:
#                                        the chosen slot has been
#                                        written. On _sc_invalidate:
#                                        the scan has completed. On
#                                        _sc_stats: the 64-byte SCCS
#                                        record has been emitted.
#   SCC_ERR_BAD_PTR            = 0x6500   _sc_lookup: out_elaborated_
#                                        ref == 0 OR out_len == 0;
#                                        _sc_stats: out_stats_ptr
#                                        == 0.
#   SCC_ERR_RESERVED_01        = 0x6501   reserved for R227.M7.1+
#                                        stats out-capacity faults
#                                        (once _sc_stats grows a
#                                        bounded-capacity signature).
#   SCC_ERR_RESERVED_02        = 0x6502   reserved for R227.M7.1+
#                                        hash-sentinel faults (when
#                                        a reserved magic bit pattern
#                                        is adopted as the "empty"
#                                        sentinel).
#   SCC_ERR_NULL_REF           = 0x6503   _sc_insert: elaborated_
#                                        ref == 0.
#   SCC_ERR_ZERO_LEN           = 0x6504   _sc_insert: len == 0.
#   SCC_ERR_NOT_FOUND          = 0x6510   _sc_lookup: no matching
#                                        row after a full 32-slot
#                                        scan (no magic + LIVE +
#                                        (source, deps) agreement).
#
# Slots 0x6505..0x650F reserved for R227.M7.1+ insert / invalidate
# diagnostics (full-pool-with-no-victim, source-hash-collision-arm,
# pdxfs-write-fault, serialization-fault, deserialization-fault,
# corrupt-record-magic, corrupt-record-flags, corrupt-record-length,
# capacity-saturation-warn, policy-mode-mismatch). Slots
# 0x6511..0x65FF reserved for R227.M7.2+ lookup diagnostics (stale-
# deps-hash, miss-by-invalidation-reason, pdxfs-reload-fault, cache-
# coherence-warn, cross-session-shared-fault, concurrency-fault,
# LRU-sweep-noise).
#
# The 0x6500..0x65FF range is reserved for the ScriptCache parser-
# tier substrate across R227.M7+. The parallel-arm 0x6300..0x63FF
# band is held for R225.M10 (per-turn type-check fingerprint); the
# parallel-arm 0x6400..0x64FF band is held for R226.M2 (datalog
# predicate-type resolution); the 0x6200..0x62FF band directly below
# is owned by R227.M6 VersionPragma (landed Wave 132). A downstream
# compound-error dispatcher discriminates "script-cache substrate
# faulted" from every other sibling satellite's failure modes via
# `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x65`.
#
# Grep confirmation at R227.M7 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x65[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> hits only in src/script_cache.pdx, caps.decl, STATUS.md,
#        CHANGELOG.md (via .plans/scratch/CHANGELOG-R227M7.md), and
#        design/shell/script-cache.md.
#   grep -rE '0x65(0[0-4]|10)\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status hits outside this file + script_cache.pdx
#        + design/shell/script-cache.md.
#
# ---- R227.M7 catalogue paragraph (script_cache) ----------------------
#
#   R227.M7 ScriptCache (script_cache.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      in-memory LRU cache of elaborated script bodies keyed by
#      (source_hash, deps_hash). The parser-tier substrate behind
#      the REPL's "re-run the same script" fast path. Raw-M7 lands
#      the in-memory layer; disk-backed `~/.paideia/script-cache/
#      <hash>.pdc` persistence lands at R227.M7.1 behind pdxfs path
#      integration. Five public entry points over the module's own
#      `.bss` pool + three counter scalars:
#        _sc_init() -> ()
#          Defensive scrub of _sc_entry_count, _sc_hit_count,
#          _sc_miss_count (three u64 stores = 24 B). Pool is NOT
#          scrubbed -- .bss zero-init already satisfies the "magic
#          == 0 means never-used slot" invariant at cold boot. Pure
#          leaf; zero pushes; no stack frame; no nested CALL.
#        _sc_lookup(source_hash, deps_hash, out_elaborated_ref,
#                   out_len) -> status
#          Scan the 32-slot pool for a row matching (magic == 'SCCA'
#          0x41434353 LE, flags.LIVE == 1, source_hash agreement,
#          deps_hash agreement). On hit: write *out_elaborated_ref
#          = slot.body_ref u64 at [+24]; write *out_len = slot.
#          body_len u64 at [+32]; update slot.timestamp_us at [+40]
#          via rdtsc + shl rdx,32 + or rax,rdx; bump _sc_hit_count;
#          return SCC_OK. On miss: bump _sc_miss_count; return
#          SCC_ERR_NOT_FOUND (0x6510). Guards: out_elaborated_ref
#          != 0 AND out_len != 0 (fail -> SCC_ERR_BAD_PTR 0x6500).
#          5-push PURE LEAF (rbx=source_hash, rbp=deps_hash, r12=
#          out_elaborated_ref, r13=out_len, r14=scan_idx) with no
#          nested CALL.
#        _sc_insert(source_hash, deps_hash, elaborated_ref, len)
#                                                      -> errno
#          Phase A free scan: walk slots 0..32; first slot with
#          flags.LIVE == 0 wins; is_fresh = 1. Phase B LRU scan: if
#          phase A finds no free slot, seed min_ts from slot[0]'s
#          timestamp, re-walk the pool for the slot with the
#          minimum timestamp_us; is_fresh = 0 (eviction). Overwrite
#          the chosen slot with the new 64-byte record (magic 'SCCA'
#          at [+0]; LIVE flag at [+4]; source_hash at [+8]; deps_
#          hash at [+16]; elaborated_ref at [+24]; len at [+32];
#          rdtsc timestamp at [+40]; cache_key_inline[16] at [+48]
#          as raw bytes of (source_hash, deps_hash) packed as two
#          u64 LE stores). If is_fresh == 1, bump _sc_entry_count;
#          else no bump (eviction preserves count). Returns SCC_OK.
#          Guards: elaborated_ref != 0 (fail -> SCC_ERR_NULL_REF
#          0x6503); len != 0 (fail -> SCC_ERR_ZERO_LEN 0x6504).
#          6-push PURE LEAF (rbx=source_hash, rbp=deps_hash, r12=
#          elaborated_ref, r13=len, r14=scan/chosen_idx, r15=
#          min_timestamp carrier) with no nested CALL.
#        _sc_invalidate(source_hash) -> errno
#          Scan the 32-slot pool; for every row matching (magic ==
#          'SCCA', flags.LIVE == 1, source_hash agreement) zero
#          both the magic u32 at [+0] and the flags u32 at [+4]
#          and bump the local cleared_count. Decrement _sc_entry_
#          count by cleared_count. The elaborated_body_ref payload
#          is NOT scrubbed (the module owns no finalizer; the
#          caller is responsible for releasing the underlying
#          storage). Returns SCC_OK. 3-push PURE LEAF (rbx=
#          source_hash, rbp=scan_idx, r12=cleared_count) with no
#          nested CALL.
#        _sc_stats(out_stats_ptr) -> status
#          Write a 64-byte SCCS record at out_stats_ptr. Layout:
#          magic 'SCCS' u32 (0x53434353 LE) at [+0]; version u16 =
#          1 at [+4]; reserved u16 at [+6]; entry_count u64 at
#          [+8]; hit_count u64 at [+16]; miss_count u64 at [+24];
#          capacity u64 = 32 at [+32]; reserved u8[24] at [+40].
#          Returns SCC_OK on write; SCC_ERR_BAD_PTR (0x6500) on
#          out_stats_ptr == 0. 1-push PURE LEAF (rbx=out_stats_
#          ptr) with no nested CALL.
#      64-byte ScriptCache record layout: magic 'SCCA' (0x41434353
#      LE) u32 at [+0]; flags u32 at [+4] (bit 0 = SCC_FLAG_LIVE);
#      source_hash u64 at [+8]; deps_hash u64 at [+16]; elaborated_
#      body_ref u64 at [+24]; elaborated_body_len u64 at [+32];
#      timestamp_us u64 at [+40]; cache_key_inline u8[16] at [+48].
#      Stride 64 is a power of two (`shl idx, 6` addressing without
#      2-op imul-r-imm).
#      Pool + scalar state (.bss): `_sc_entries : [u8; 2048]` (= 32
#      slots x 64 B) @align(8); `_sc_entry_count : u64` @align(8);
#      `_sc_hit_count : u64` @align(8); `_sc_miss_count : u64`
#      @align(8). Pool layout: slot i occupies `_sc_entries[i * 64
#      .. (i + 1) * 64)`. At cold boot `.bss` zero-init makes every
#      slot's magic 0 and flags 0, which is the exact "never-used"
#      state the lookup / insert paths treat as empty.
#      64-byte Stats record layout: magic 'SCCS' (0x53434353 LE)
#      u32 at [+0]; version u16 = 1 at [+4]; reserved u16 at [+6];
#      entry_count u64 at [+8]; hit_count u64 at [+16]; miss_count
#      u64 at [+24]; capacity u64 at [+32]; reserved u8[24] at
#      [+40]. The stats magic 'SCCS' is DISTINCT from the record
#      magic 'SCCA' by their last byte ('S' vs 'A'); a downstream
#      reader that scans either record never confuses them.
#      Error band `0x6500..0x65FF` with the six SCC_* status codes
#      (SCC_OK, SCC_ERR_BAD_PTR 0x6500, SCC_ERR_RESERVED_01 0x6501,
#      SCC_ERR_RESERVED_02 0x6502, SCC_ERR_NULL_REF 0x6503, SCC_ERR_
#      ZERO_LEN 0x6504, SCC_ERR_NOT_FOUND 0x6510); 0x6505..0x650F
#      reserved for M7.1+ insert / invalidate diagnostics;
#      0x6511..0x65FF reserved for M7.2+ lookup diagnostics.
#      Twenty-two `pub` constants beyond the SCC_* status taxonomy:
#      SCC_POOL_SLOTS = 32; SCC_RECORD_BYTES = 64; SCC_POOL_BYTES =
#      2048; SCC_SLOT_IDX_SHIFT = 6; SCC_OFF_MAGIC = 0, SCC_OFF_
#      FLAGS = 4, SCC_OFF_SOURCE_HASH = 8, SCC_OFF_DEPS_HASH = 16,
#      SCC_OFF_BODY_REF = 24, SCC_OFF_BODY_LEN = 32, SCC_OFF_
#      TIMESTAMP = 40, SCC_OFF_KEY_INLINE = 48; SCC_FLAG_LIVE = 1;
#      SCC_RECORD_MAGIC = 0x41434353 'SCCA' LE; SCC_STATS_MAGIC =
#      0x53434353 'SCCS' LE; SCC_STATS_BYTES = 64; SCC_STATS_
#      VERSION = 1; SCC_STATS_OFF_MAGIC = 0, SCC_STATS_OFF_VERSION
#      = 4, SCC_STATS_OFF_RESERVED_6 = 6, SCC_STATS_OFF_ENTRY_COUNT
#      = 8, SCC_STATS_OFF_HIT_COUNT = 16, SCC_STATS_OFF_MISS_COUNT
#      = 24, SCC_STATS_OFF_CAPACITY = 32, SCC_STATS_OFF_RESERVED_40
#      = 40.
#      Labels prefixed `scc_` with per-function suffix (`scc_ini_`
#      init, `scc_lu_` lookup, `scc_in_` insert, `scc_iv_`
#      invalidate, `scc_st_` stats). `sc_` is RESERVED by the
#      kernel-side `src/kernel/core/pds/script_cmd.pdx` module
#      (ScriptCmd), which uses `sc_` as its local-label prefix
#      inside the kernel's linker scope. The parser-tier library
#      NEVER links the kernel so the local-label scopes are
#      disjoint by linker design, but to keep the per-prefix-grep
#      hygiene clean this module adopts `scc_` (the second `c` for
#      "cache") rather than reuse `sc_`. The PUBLIC entry symbols
#      `_sc_init`, `_sc_lookup`, `_sc_insert`, `_sc_invalidate`,
#      `_sc_stats` are GLOBAL linker symbols in the parser-tier
#      library's linker scope; they are DISJOINT from the kernel-
#      side `_sc_dispatch_fn` global (kernel linker scope) and the
#      kernel's local `sc_` label prefix (kernel linker scope).
#      Grep-confirmed virgin inside tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#      src/*.pdx (no prior `scc_` label in the parser-tier library).
#      Grep-confirmed disjoint from every landed parser-tier label
#      prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_, sch_sig_,
#      cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_, rp_,
#      fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_, hm_,
#      rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_,
#      rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_, vp_, pth_). Also disjoint from
#      the paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if, let, fn, ret,
#      done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. The script-body binary cache runs purely in
# user space over caller-supplied opaque u64 hashes + caller-opaque
# (ref, len) payload pairs + the module's own `.bss` pool; no KIND
# surface is touched at raw M7. The forward-path landings (M7.1
# pdxfs persistence; M7.2 hex rendering; M7.3 TurnFingerprint wire-
# up; M7.4 capacity bump; M7.5 cross-session concurrency; M7.6
# acceptance fixture) introduce NO new KIND surface at the parser
# tier -- the pdxfs persistence landing adds a kernel-side pdxfs
# path-tree KIND dep that will be CROSS-WIRED, not consumed here.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R227.M7 (script_cache.pdx) has NO external cross-
# library linker dependencies and NO intra-library cross-module
# linker dependency at raw M7. All five public entry points touch
# ONLY caller-supplied pointer args, caller-supplied u64 scalars,
# and the module's own `.bss` pool + three counter scalars. The
# declared forward-path intra-library deps are:
#   M7.3 -> R229.M10 TurnFingerprint feed-in (chaos-replay cache
#     state reproduction).
#   M7.6 -> R221.M9 property-test harness slot for the fixture.
#
# R227.M7 label prefix `scc_` is DISJOINT from every landed parser-
# tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_,
# rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_,
# hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_,
# rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_, vp_, pth_) AND from the
# kernel-side `sc_` prefix (ScriptCmd script_cmd.pdx) -- `scc_`
# and `sc_` differ on the third character; label resolution is
# prefix-anchored so even if the two shared a linker scope they
# would not collide. Also disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-
# keyword set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).

# ---- Error band 0x6400..0x64FF (R226.M2, predicate_types) ------------
#
# Parser-tier predicate-type resolution and argument-position
# typechecking for the Datalog surface. R226.M2 of the datalog
# umbrella (paideia-os#2490). Nine slots used at R226.M2:
#
#   PT_OK                      = 0        operation succeeded.
#   PT_ERR_BAD_PTR             = 0x6400   NULL pointer where a non-NULL
#                                         pointer was required
#                                         (name_ptr on register/lookup;
#                                         out_record_ptr on lookup;
#                                         predicate_record on
#                                         check_call; actual_type_codes
#                                         on check_call when
#                                         actual_n_args > 0).
#   PT_ERR_BAD_NAME            = 0x6401   name_len == 0 or name_len
#                                         > PT_NAME_MAX (16).
#   PT_ERR_BAD_ARITY           = 0x6402   arity > PT_MAX_ARITY (32)
#                                         on _pt_register.
#   PT_ERR_POOL_FULL           = 0x6403   _pt_predicate_count ==
#                                         PT_POOL_SLOTS (128).
#   PT_ERR_BAD_TYPE_CODE       = 0x6404   arg_type_codes[i] not in
#                                         the SS_TYPE_* set OR
#                                         PT_TYPE_VAR OR PT_TYPE_WILD
#                                         (i.e. not in {1..6, 8..11})
#                                         on _pt_register.
#   PT_ERR_ARITY_MISMATCH      = 0x6410   call-site arity != predicate
#                                         arity on _pt_check_call;
#                                         maps to the E0900 diagnostic
#                                         family.
#   PT_ERR_TYPE_MISMATCH       = 0x6411   call-site type code at some
#                                         position differs from the
#                                         predicate's signature;
#                                         maps to E0901.
#   PT_ERR_UNKNOWN_PREDICATE   = 0x6412   _pt_lookup: no record with
#                                         matching name_hash; maps to
#                                         E0902.
#   PT_ERR_BAD_RECORD          = 0x6413   _pt_check_call: record
#                                         magic u32 at [+0] does not
#                                         match 'PRTY' (0x59545250).
#
# Slots 0x6405..0x640F reserved for R226.M2.1+ register/lookup
# diagnostics (open-world catalogue bit, polymorphic position
# subsumption-rule refinement, duplicate-registration strictness
# toggle). Slots 0x6414..0x64FF reserved for R226.M2.2+ check-call
# diagnostics (per-position subsumption rule, implicit-coercion
# warn-level, open-world unknown-predicate relax).
#
# The 0x6400..0x64FF range is reserved for the PredicateTypes
# parser-tier substrate across R226.M2+. R225.M9 (proptest_hm) owns
# the disjoint 0x6100..0x61FF slice (landed Wave 132); R227.M6
# (version_pragma) owns 0x6200..0x62FF; 0x6300..0x63FF is reserved
# for the parallel R225.M10 per-turn type-check fingerprint arm (not
# yet landed); R227.M7 ScriptCache owns 0x6500..0x65FF (landed in
# the same parallel-arm set as this R226.M2 landing).
#
# The next kilo-step band 0x6500..0x65FF is now R227.M7 ScriptCache
# (landed in the same parallel-arm set as this R226.M2 landing).
#
# A downstream compound-error dispatcher can discriminate
# "predicate-type substrate faulted" from every other sibling
# satellite's failure modes via `shr r, 8; cmp r, 0x64`.
#
# Grep confirmation at R226.M2 landing time:
#
#   grep -rE '0x64[0-9A-F]{2}' tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/
#     -> hits only in src/predicate_types.pdx, caps.decl, STATUS.md,
#        and (via .plans/scratch/CHANGELOG-R226M2.md and
#        design/shell/predicate-type-resolution.md) the arm's
#        companion scratch + design files.
#   grep -rE '0x64(0[0-4]|1[0-3])\b' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
#     -> no packed-status slot assignments outside this file +
#        predicate_types.pdx + design/shell/predicate-type-resolution.md
#        + .plans/scratch/CHANGELOG-R226M2.md.
#
# ---- R226.M2 catalogue paragraph (predicate_types) -------------------
#
#   R226.M2 PredicateTypes (predicate_types.pdx, landed): parser-tier
#      predicate-type resolution and argument-position typechecking
#      for the Datalog surface. Implements the §R226.M2 clause of
#      the datalog umbrella: look up each predicate's typed arity;
#      register a predicate as user-defined if not in the schema;
#      type-check argument positions. Four public entry points over
#      caller-supplied buffers; the module carries a singleton `.bss`
#      pool (8192 B dense PredicateRecord table + two u64 scalars):
#        _pt_init() -> ()
#          Zero-fill the 8192 B pool, clear the counter, mark the
#          state flag initialised. Idempotent via sentinel. Pure
#          leaf; zero callee-save pushes; no stack frame; no nested
#          CALL.
#        _pt_register(name_ptr, name_len, arity, arg_types_ptr,
#                     flags) -> errno
#          Register a predicate OR return PT_OK on name_hash match
#          (idempotent re-register). Validates name_ptr != 0,
#          name_len in [1..16], arity <= 32, arg_types_ptr != 0 iff
#          arity > 0, each arg_type_codes[i] in {1..6, 8..11}. Calls
#          _pt_init + _pt_fnv1a64, linear-scans existing records on
#          name_hash match (idempotent), else allocates
#          _pt_predicates[count], zero-fills 64 B, stamps magic
#          'PRTY' + arity + flags + name_hash + name_inline (zero-
#          padded) + arg_type_codes (zero-padded past arity). 6-push
#          callee-save frame (rbx=name_ptr, rbp=name_len, r12=arity,
#          r13=arg_types_ptr, r14=flags, r15=name_hash) + sub rsp, 32
#          scratch frame = 80 B. Entry rsp%16==8, after 6 pushes
#          rsp%16==8, +32 scratch = rsp%16==0 at every nested CALL.
#        _pt_lookup(name_ptr, name_len, out_record_ptr) -> errno
#          Resolve a predicate by name_hash; byte-copies the 64 B
#          record into out_record_ptr on hit, else writes 64 zero
#          bytes (defensive) and returns PT_ERR_UNKNOWN_PREDICATE.
#          6-push callee-save frame (rbx=name_ptr, rbp=name_len,
#          r12=out_record_ptr, r13=name_hash, r14=i, r15=entry_addr)
#          + sub rsp, 8 pad = 56 B. Entry rsp%16==8, after 6 pushes
#          rsp%16==8, +8 pad = rsp%16==0 at every nested CALL.
#        _pt_check_call(predicate_record, actual_args_ptr,
#                       actual_n_args, actual_type_codes) -> status
#          Validate a call site's arity + per-position type codes
#          against the predicate's resolved signature. Pure leaf
#          (zero callee-save pushes; no stack frame; no nested CALL).
#          Validates predicate_record != 0, actual_type_codes != 0
#          iff actual_n_args > 0, magic u32 at [record+0] matches
#          'PRTY'. Compares u16 arity at [record+4] vs
#          actual_n_args (ARITY_MISMATCH on fail = E0900). For each
#          position, applies the compatibility rule (expected or
#          actual in {PT_TYPE_VAR, PT_TYPE_WILD} match any;
#          otherwise expected == actual required; TYPE_MISMATCH on
#          fail = E0901). The actual_args_ptr argument is OPAQUE at
#          R226.M2 and reserved for M3+ when the evaluator passes
#          its Clause.term_offsets pointer here.
#      64-byte PredicateRecord layout: magic 'PRTY' (0x59545250 LE)
#      u32 at [+0]; arity u16 at [+4]; flags u16 at [+6] (bit 0 =
#      PT_FLAG_USER_DEFINED); name_hash u64 at [+8] (FNV-1a 64);
#      name_inline u8[16] at [+16]; arg_type_codes u8[32] at [+32].
#      Stride 64 = 2^6 is a power of two so pool addressing uses
#      `shl idx, 6` with no 2-op imul-r-imm. The 64-byte stride is a
#      conscious reconciliation of the brief's stated "48B" against
#      the brief's own field-offset layout (0x20..0x40 arg_type_codes
#      u8[32]) AND the brief's "0..32 arity" cap -- all three
#      constraints are only satisfiable at stride 64, not 48. See
#      design doc section 2.1.
#      Type-code set (eleven codes): SS_TYPE_U64=1, SS_TYPE_I64=2,
#      SS_TYPE_STR=3, SS_TYPE_HEX=4, SS_TYPE_HASH32=5, SS_TYPE_TSC=6,
#      SS_TYPE_PATH=8, SS_TYPE_PID=9 (reused verbatim from R222.M1
#      SchemasSig), PT_TYPE_VAR=10 (Datalog variable; parser-tier M2
#      extension), PT_TYPE_WILD=11 (underscore placeholder; parser-
#      tier M2 extension). Codes 0 (SS_TYPE_END) and 7 (reserved
#      SF_TYPE_IMAGE slot) are explicitly disallowed.
#      Error band `0x6400..0x64FF` with the nine PT_* status codes
#      (PT_OK, PT_ERR_BAD_PTR 0x6400, PT_ERR_BAD_NAME 0x6401,
#      PT_ERR_BAD_ARITY 0x6402, PT_ERR_POOL_FULL 0x6403,
#      PT_ERR_BAD_TYPE_CODE 0x6404, PT_ERR_ARITY_MISMATCH 0x6410 =
#      E0900, PT_ERR_TYPE_MISMATCH 0x6411 = E0901,
#      PT_ERR_UNKNOWN_PREDICATE 0x6412 = E0902, PT_ERR_BAD_RECORD
#      0x6413); 0x6405..0x640F reserved for M2.1+ register/lookup
#      diagnostics; 0x6414..0x64FF reserved for M2.2+ check-call
#      diagnostics.
#      Nineteen `pub` constants beyond the PT_* status taxonomy:
#      PT_RECORD_BYTES = 64 (power of two for `shl idx, 6`
#      addressing); PT_POOL_SLOTS = 128; PT_POOL_BYTES = 8192;
#      PT_NAME_MAX = 16; PT_MAX_ARITY = 32; PT_RECORD_MAGIC =
#      0x59545250 'PRTY' LE; PT_OFF_MAGIC = 0, PT_OFF_ARITY = 4,
#      PT_OFF_FLAGS = 6, PT_OFF_NAME_HASH = 8, PT_OFF_NAME_INLINE =
#      16, PT_OFF_ARG_TYPE_CODES = 32; PT_FLAG_USER_DEFINED = 0x0001;
#      PT_TYPE_VAR = 10; PT_TYPE_WILD = 11; PT_TYPE_CODE_MAX = 11.
#      Labels prefixed `pt_` with per-function suffix (`pt_ini_`
#      init, `pt_reg_` register, `pt_lk_` lookup, `pt_cc_` check-call,
#      `pt_fnv_` FNV helper). Grep-confirmed virgin inside
#      tools/user/libpdx-pds-parser/src/*.pdx (no prior `pt_` label
#      in the parser-tier library; the sibling `pth_` R225.M9 prefix
#      differs at the third character and is prefix-anchored, so
#      the two never collide). Grep-confirmed disjoint from every
#      landed parser-tier label prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_,
#      sm_, fh_, sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_,
#      pl_, pl_lam_, rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_,
#      cr_, ls_, lp_, hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_,
#      vi_, ml_, ir_, rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_, vp_, pth_,
#      scc_). Also disjoint from the paideia-as reserved-keyword
#      set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).
#
# consumed_kinds: none. Predicate-type resolution runs purely in
# user space over caller-supplied name bytes + caller-owned 64-byte
# scratch records + the module's own singleton .bss pool; no KIND
# surface is touched at raw M2. The forward-path landings (M2.1
# open-world catalogue; M2.2 subsumption rule refinement; M2.3
# 30-test corpus + AFL++ shim; the eventual R220.M4 schema-registry
# wire-up) all stay in-process at the parser tier and introduce NO
# new KIND surface at this library's seam.
#
# libpdx-pds-parser.R226.M2 (predicate_types.pdx) has NO external
# cross-library linker dependencies at raw M2 and NO intra-library
# cross-module linker dependency. All four public entry points
# touch ONLY caller-supplied pointer args + the module's own
# singleton .bss (_pt_predicates, _pt_predicate_count,
# _pt_init_state). The module replicates the FNV-1a 64 helper
# (_pt_fnv1a64) rather than linking against _cmd_sig_fnv1a64 /
# _cmdreg_fnv1a64 / _fi_fnv1a64 / _ha_fnv1a64 so the module links
# standalone -- the five parser-family FNV leaves produce byte-
# identical hashes for byte-identical inputs so a downstream
# consumer may compare name_hash values across families. R226.M2
# declares a FORWARD intra-library dependency on R226.M1 Datalog
# (the datalog parser whose Clause records' predicate-name spans
# feed this module's _pt_register / _pt_lookup entry points) via
# the CALLER's stitching -- there is no direct call from this
# module into datalog.pdx (precedent: R225.M3+ bridges to R222.M1
# SchemasSig the same way). The post-R220.M4 landing will introduce
# a schema-registry seam at startup.
#
# R226.M2 label prefix `pt_` is DISJOINT from every landed parser-
# tier module's prefix (u8n_, gi_, wp_, cl_, ast_, sm_, fh_,
# sch_sig_, cmd_sig_, fi_, afs_, cmdreg_, dm_, ds_, pl_, pl_lam_,
# rp_, fo_, sgr_, ws_, ha_, st_, ll_, cc_, er_, cr_, ls_, lp_,
# hm_, rt_, eu_, dl_, se_, te_, jc_, h_, le_, vi_, ml_, ir_,
# rc_, rd_, dr_, lc_, lh_, tf_, mf_, vp_, pth_, scc_). The sibling
# R225.M9 `pth_` prefix differs at the third character and is
# prefix-anchored so the two never collide. Also disjoint from the
# paideia-as reserved-keyword set (loop, if, let, fn, ret, done).
